window.SVARA_CORPUS=[{"v":null,"t":"Note on the Source Text","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"The *Śiva Svarodaya* (शिव स्वरोदय) is a Sanskrit treatise of uncertain date, transmitted within the Nātha and Śaiva tantric lineages, structured as a dialogue in which Śiva instructs Pārvatī in *svara-vijñāna* — the systematic observation of nasal airflow and its correlation with physiological state, cognition, and behavioural outcome. Manuscript traditions vary between roughly 390 and 400 verses. The recension supplied for this study contains 313 numbered verses spanning 5 through 395, with lacunae at 6–37, 48–49, 51–63, 75–95, 98–99, 113, 115, 179, and 270–274. The text is not a devotional work. It contains almost no theology in the ordinary sense. …","a":"The *Śiva Svarodaya* (शिव स्वरोदय) is a Sanskrit treatise of uncertain date, transmitted within the Nātha and Śaiva tantric lineages, structured as a dialogue in which Śiva instructs Pārvatī in *svara-vijñāna* — the systematic observation of nasal airflow and its correlation with physiological state, cognition, and behavioural outcome. Manuscript traditions vary between roughly 390 and 400 verses. The recension supplied for this study contains 313 numbered verses spanning 5 through 395, with lacunae at 6–37, 48–49, 51–63, 75–95, 98–99, 113, 115, 179, and 270–274. The text is not a devotional work. It contains almost no theology in the ordinary sense. Its bulk is procedural: how to determine which nostril is dominant, how long dominance persists, what state changes accompany a switch, what classes of action succeed or fail under each condition, and how to force a switch deliberately. Read without its cosmological vocabulary, it is a manual of interoceptive self-measurement and autonomic self-regulation — arguably the most detailed such manual produced before the twentieth century. That is the reading this book pursues.","m":"","g":[]},{"v":null,"t":"What This Book Claims, and What It Does Not","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"It does not claim that the composers of the *Śiva Svarodaya* possessed knowledge of the locus coeruleus, the hypothalamic suprachiasmatic nucleus, or heart-rate variability. They did not. They possessed something different and, for our purposes, more interesting: a multi-generational observational programme trained on a signal that modern physiology has confirmed to be real, and that modern physiology has, until quite recently, largely ignored. It does not claim that the text's predictive apparatus works. …","a":"It does not claim that the composers of the *Śiva Svarodaya* possessed knowledge of the locus coeruleus, the hypothalamic suprachiasmatic nucleus, or heart-rate variability. They did not. They possessed something different and, for our purposes, more interesting: a multi-generational observational programme trained on a signal that modern physiology has confirmed to be real, and that modern physiology has, until quite recently, largely ignored. It does not claim that the text's predictive apparatus works. Large portions of the *Svarodaya* are frankly divinatory: which nostril is flowing determines whether a lost object will be recovered, whether a pregnancy will yield a son, whether a traveller will return. These claims are false and will be treated as false. What is analytically valuable in them is not the prediction but the *substrate* of the prediction — the fact that the text treats nasal dominance as a state variable stable enough to hang inferences on, and that this belief is grounded in an accurate observation about the body. What this book does claim is threefold. First, that the *Śiva Svarodaya* is an empirical document describing a real physiological rhythm — the nasal cycle — with a precision unmatched in the pre-modern literature of any culture. Second, that its behavioural prescriptions constitute a coherent, testable model of state-dependent performance: the proposition that certain classes of cognitive and motor task are executed more effectively in certain autonomic states, and that those states are externally readable. …","m":"","g":[]},{"v":null,"t":"Evidential Grading","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"Any project of this kind fails in one of two directions. It either dissolves into apologetics, where every verse is retro-fitted to a fashionable finding, or it dissolves into dismissal, where the whole corpus is discarded because parts of it are astrological. Both failures come from the same source: refusing to grade claims individually. Every substantive neuroscientific assertion in the commentaries below carries one of three markers: **[E] — Established.** Supported by replicated human studies, physiological measurement, or anatomical fact. …","a":"Any project of this kind fails in one of two directions. It either dissolves into apologetics, where every verse is retro-fitted to a fashionable finding, or it dissolves into dismissal, where the whole corpus is discarded because parts of it are astrological. Both failures come from the same source: refusing to grade claims individually. Every substantive neuroscientific assertion in the commentaries below carries one of three markers: **[E] — Established.** Supported by replicated human studies, physiological measurement, or anatomical fact. The existence of the nasal cycle, the anatomy of nasal autonomic innervation, respiratory sinus arrhythmia, and respiratory entrainment of limbic oscillations fall here. **[S] — Suggestive.** Supported by some human data, often small-sample, sometimes with failed or partial replications. Unilateral forced-nostril breathing effects on autonomic indices and cognitive performance fall here. So does most of the slow-breathing literature. **[X] — Speculative.** A mechanistic proposal offered by this author to explain why the text says what it says. It is a hypothesis, not a finding. It should be read as an invitation to design an experiment, not as a report of one. The reader who ignores these markers will misread the book. ---","m":"","g":[]},{"v":"INTRO-nasal","t":"The nasal cycle is real","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"In 1895 the German rhinologist Richard Kayser documented what clinicians had observed piecemeal for decades: in the majority of healthy adults, the two nasal passages are not equally patent. One is congested, the other open, and the asymmetry reverses at intervals ranging from roughly forty minutes to eight hours, with a modal period in the region of two to three hours. The mechanism is vascular rather than muscular. The nasal turbinates contain erectile venous sinusoids under the control of the sympathetic and parasympathetic branches of the autonomic nervous system. …","a":"In 1895 the German rhinologist Richard Kayser documented what clinicians had observed piecemeal for decades: in the majority of healthy adults, the two nasal passages are not equally patent. One is congested, the other open, and the asymmetry reverses at intervals ranging from roughly forty minutes to eight hours, with a modal period in the region of two to three hours. The mechanism is vascular rather than muscular. The nasal turbinates contain erectile venous sinusoids under the control of the sympathetic and parasympathetic branches of the autonomic nervous system. Sympathetic outflow constricts these sinusoids, draining the tissue and opening the airway; parasympathetic dominance engorges them and closes it. The alternation is centrally driven, not a local reflex — it persists across sleep, it survives topical anaesthesia of the nasal mucosa, and its period shifts with systemic autonomic state. **[E]** This means something specific and important. The relative patency of the two nostrils is a *continuously available, non-invasive readout of central autonomic asymmetry*. It is a dial on the outside of the skull that reports on the state of the machinery inside. **[E]** There is no instrument required to read it. There is no training required beyond attention. A person sitting still with the mouth closed can determine, in under five seconds, which side is dominant, and can therefore track a slow autonomic rhythm across the whole of a waking day. The *Śiva Svarodaya* is, in its entirety, an elaboration of what one might do with that fact.","m":"","g":[]},{"v":null,"t":"What the text got right about the rhythm","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"Verse 72 states that within a *nāḍī*'s period of flow, five elemental phases arise in sequence, each occupying roughly two and a half *ghaṭikās*. Elsewhere the text gives the full dominance period as approximately one hour, sometimes two and a half *ghaṭikās* per subdivision within a sixty-*ghaṭikā* day. The arithmetic is not always internally consistent across recensions, a point taken up in the commentary to verses 64 and 72. But the order of magnitude is correct: the text asserts a rhythm with a period on the order of one to three hours, subdivided into shorter phases of roughly twenty minutes to an hour. Compare this with the twentieth-century literature. …","a":"Verse 72 states that within a *nāḍī*'s period of flow, five elemental phases arise in sequence, each occupying roughly two and a half *ghaṭikās*. Elsewhere the text gives the full dominance period as approximately one hour, sometimes two and a half *ghaṭikās* per subdivision within a sixty-*ghaṭikā* day. The arithmetic is not always internally consistent across recensions, a point taken up in the commentary to verses 64 and 72. But the order of magnitude is correct: the text asserts a rhythm with a period on the order of one to three hours, subdivided into shorter phases of roughly twenty minutes to an hour. Compare this with the twentieth-century literature. Nathaniel Kleitman, working on sleep, proposed the Basic Rest–Activity Cycle: an ultradian rhythm of roughly ninety to one hundred and twenty minutes that persists into waking and modulates alertness, appetite, and cognitive throughput. The nasal cycle's modal period sits inside that window. Several investigators have argued that the two are manifestations of a single hypothalamically-generated ultradian oscillator. The coupling is not firmly established — the nasal cycle is considerably more variable in period than the classical BRAC, and the two can dissociate — but the temporal correspondence is not coincidental. **[S]** A pre-modern text asserting a one-to-three-hour bodily rhythm with a roughly half-hour substructure is not making a lucky guess. It is reporting sustained observation.","m":"","g":[]},{"v":null,"t":"The lateralization claim, and why this book treats it carefully","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"The most seductive bridge between this text and modern neuroscience is also the weakest, and it must be dealt with before anything else, because most popular treatments of the *Svarodaya* are built on it. The text repeatedly associates left-nostril flow (*iḍā*, *candra*, lunar) with cooling, receptivity, nourishment, and stillness, and right-nostril flow (*piṅgalā*, *sūrya*, solar) with heat, exertion, aggression, and mobility. …","a":"The most seductive bridge between this text and modern neuroscience is also the weakest, and it must be dealt with before anything else, because most popular treatments of the *Svarodaya* are built on it. The text repeatedly associates left-nostril flow (*iḍā*, *candra*, lunar) with cooling, receptivity, nourishment, and stillness, and right-nostril flow (*piṅgalā*, *sūrya*, solar) with heat, exertion, aggression, and mobility. In 1983 Werntz and colleagues at the University of California, San Diego, reported that the nasal cycle was accompanied by alternating relative dominance of EEG amplitude in the contralateral cerebral hemisphere — right-nostril dominance with relatively greater left-hemisphere activity, and vice versa. The finding was electrifying. It appeared to hand the *Svarodaya* a modern neural substrate on a plate: nostril alternation as an externally visible marker of hemispheric alternation. The finding has not held up cleanly. Subsequent attempts at replication have produced mixed and often null results. Sample sizes in the original work were small. The EEG measures were coarse by contemporary standards. Later work using better methods has generally failed to find a robust, reliable contralateral coupling of the kind originally described, and some studies report ipsilateral or inconsistent effects. **[S, weakly]** More fundamentally, the popular framework the finding is usually pressed into service to support — the \"left brain is analytic, right brain is creative\" schema — is itself substantially wrong. …","m":"","g":[]},{"v":null,"t":"Respiration as a pacemaker for the brain","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"Until roughly 2016, breathing was treated in most of neuroscience as a source of artefact — something to be regressed out of an fMRI time series. That position is no longer tenable. Zelano and colleagues, recording intracranially from patients with implanted electrodes, demonstrated that neural oscillations in the piriform cortex, amygdala, and hippocampus are phase-locked to the respiratory cycle, and critically, that this entrainment is dependent on nasal rather than oral airflow. When the patients breathed through the mouth, the entrainment weakened substantially. …","a":"Until roughly 2016, breathing was treated in most of neuroscience as a source of artefact — something to be regressed out of an fMRI time series. That position is no longer tenable. Zelano and colleagues, recording intracranially from patients with implanted electrodes, demonstrated that neural oscillations in the piriform cortex, amygdala, and hippocampus are phase-locked to the respiratory cycle, and critically, that this entrainment is dependent on nasal rather than oral airflow. When the patients breathed through the mouth, the entrainment weakened substantially. Behavioural consequences followed: fear recognition and memory retrieval performance varied systematically with respiratory phase, and only during nasal breathing. **[E]** The mechanism is mechanoreceptive. Olfactory sensory neurons in the nasal epithelium respond not only to odorants but to airflow itself, providing a rhythmic afferent barrage to the olfactory bulb at the frequency of breathing. The olfactory bulb is not a peripheral relay; it projects directly, without thalamic gating, into the piriform cortex, the amygdala, and the entorhinal-hippocampal system. Nasal breathing therefore delivers a rhythmic input signal straight into the limbic and mnemonic core of the brain, once per breath, all day. **[E]** This is the single most important finding for any modern reading of the *Śiva Svarodaya*, and it justifies the text's otherwise strange obsession. …","m":"","g":[]},{"v":null,"t":"The autonomic frame","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"Three further mechanisms recur throughout the commentaries and are stated once here. *Respiratory sinus arrhythmia.* Heart rate rises during inspiration and falls during expiration, because vagal outflow to the sinoatrial node is gated by the respiratory rhythm generator in the brainstem. Lengthening expiration relative to inspiration increases the duration of vagal braking per cycle. This is why slow breathing with prolonged exhalation raises heart-rate variability and shifts the autonomic balance parasympathetically. …","a":"Three further mechanisms recur throughout the commentaries and are stated once here. *Respiratory sinus arrhythmia.* Heart rate rises during inspiration and falls during expiration, because vagal outflow to the sinoatrial node is gated by the respiratory rhythm generator in the brainstem. Lengthening expiration relative to inspiration increases the duration of vagal braking per cycle. This is why slow breathing with prolonged exhalation raises heart-rate variability and shifts the autonomic balance parasympathetically. **[E]** *Baroreflex resonance.* At a breathing rate near six cycles per minute, the phase relationship between respiration-driven blood pressure oscillations and the baroreflex response enters resonance, producing very large oscillations in heart rate. Sustained practice at this rate appears to increase baroreflex gain. This is the mechanistic basis of the heart-rate-variability biofeedback literature, and the rate falls squarely within the range the *hatha* traditions independently converged on. **[E for the resonance; S for durable gain increases]** *Locus coeruleus coupling.* The locus coeruleus, the brain's principal noradrenergic nucleus, exhibits activity coupled to the respiratory cycle and is a major determinant of cortical arousal and signal-to-noise in sensory processing. Respiratory manipulation is therefore a plausible route by which voluntary breathing alters global arousal — the pathway by which a mechanical act at the nostrils reaches the cortex. **[S]**","m":"","g":[]},{"v":null,"t":"The category error to avoid","p":"Introduction","d":"","i":"","f":"The *Śiva Svarodaya* does not distinguish between description and prescription, or between physiology and omen. A verse observing that the left nostril flows at dawn during the bright fortnight sits adjacent to a verse claiming that acting during left flow guarantees success in all undertakings. To the text these are the same kind of statement. They are not the same kind of statement, and the commentaries below separate them at every turn. The first is a chronobiological claim, testable and sometimes true. The second is either a claim about state-dependent performance — testable, occasionally supported, often not — or a claim about supernatural causation, which is neither. …","a":"The *Śiva Svarodaya* does not distinguish between description and prescription, or between physiology and omen. A verse observing that the left nostril flows at dawn during the bright fortnight sits adjacent to a verse claiming that acting during left flow guarantees success in all undertakings. To the text these are the same kind of statement. They are not the same kind of statement, and the commentaries below separate them at every turn. The first is a chronobiological claim, testable and sometimes true. The second is either a claim about state-dependent performance — testable, occasionally supported, often not — or a claim about supernatural causation, which is neither. The discipline of holding these apart is what makes the text usable. Collapse them, and one is left with astrology. Hold them apart, and one is left with something rare: a systematic, self-administered, zero-cost protocol for reading and steering one's own autonomic state, developed over centuries by observers with nothing to measure with except attention. --- # PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT ### Verses 5, 38–47, 50 The opening movement of the text is anatomical and inventorial. Before any technique is given, Pārvatī asks what the irreducible variable is, and Śiva answers with a map: ten channels, ten winds, three of the channels privileged, two of those three assigned to opposing functional poles. Read as physiology this map is inaccurate in its details and unexpectedly shrewd in its architecture. This part examines both. ---","m":"","g":[]},{"v":"5","t":"The Question of the Irreducible Variable","p":"PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT","d":"तत्त्वमेव परं मूलं निश्चितं तत्त्ववादिभिः। तत्त्वस्वरूपं किं देव तत्त्वमेव प्रकाशय॥५॥","i":"tattvam eva paraṃ mūlaṃ niścitaṃ tattvavādibhiḥ | tattvasvarūpaṃ kiṃ deva tattvam eva prakāśaya ||5||","f":"The principle alone is held by its exponents to be the ultimate root. What is the intrinsic nature of that principle? Disclose it.","a":"The verse is not a statement. It is a question, and its placement at the head of the treatise is a structural decision with consequences for how the material that follows is processed by a reader or a hearer. Consider what the interrogative frame does. A declarative opening — *the principle is the root* — invites passive encoding. An interrogative opening establishes an unresolved state: a specified gap between what is known and what is sought. The cognitive literature on this is unambiguous. Information encountered in the presence of an activated question is retained substantially better than the same information encountered without one. The effect appears under several names — the pretesting effect, curiosity-enhanced encoding, question-generation benefits — and it survives across timescales and materials. Imaging work by Kang, Camerer, Loewenstein and colleagues found that states of high epistemic curiosity are accompanied by activation in the caudate and inferior frontal gyrus, structures associated with anticipated reward, and that the magnitude of that activation predicts later recall of the answer. **[E]** Curiosity is not a mood that happens to accompany learning. It is a reward-prediction state that alters the encoding of what arrives next. The entire *Śiva Svarodaya* is constructed as a dialogue for this reason, and the reason is pedagogical rather than literary. …","m":"The practical extraction is a technique, not a belief. Before engaging any body of difficult material — a technical paper, an unfamiliar domain, a skill acquisition task — formulate and write down the specific question the material is expected to answer. Not a topic label but an interrogative sentence with a missing term. The act of formulation activates the anticipatory reward circuitry that improves subsequent encoding, and it creates a retrieval structure the material can be hung on. This is the mechanism behind pre-questioning in instructional design and behind the well-replicated finding that testing before study improves learning even when every pretest answer is wrong. **[E]** The second extraction is the reductionist habit itself. When facing a complex system one is trying to regulate — mood, energy, attention, a chronic symptom pattern — the productive question is rarely \"what is wrong\". It is \"what is the smallest variable I can observe reliably that predicts the state of the whole\". For attention and arousal, respiration is a strong candidate for that variable: it is continuously observable, voluntarily modifiable, and mechanistically upstream of autonomic and cortical state in ways detailed throughout this volume. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"38","t":"Lateral Assignment of the Principal Channels","p":"PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT","d":"इडा वामे स्थिता भागे पिङ्गला दक्षिणे स्मृता। सुषुम्ना मध्यदेशे तु गान्धारी वामचक्षुषि॥३८॥","i":"iḍā vāme sthitā bhāge piṅgalā dakṣiṇe smṛtā | suṣumnā madhyadeśe tu gāndhārī vāmacakṣuṣi ||38||","f":"Iḍā occupies the left side of the body, Piṅgalā the right, Suṣumnā the central axis. Gāndhārī terminates at the left eye.","a":"The first question is what a *nāḍī* is, and the answer determines whether the rest of the text can be read empirically or not. The term is not a nerve, and the standard apologetic move of equating *nāḍī* with peripheral nerve is both anatomically false and unnecessary. *Nāḍī* derives from a root meaning tube, reed, or channel, and the tradition uses it for a functional conduit of *prāṇa* — of vital flow. The productive translation is neither \"nerve\" nor \"meridian\" but *functional axis*: a named pathway defined by what it does and where it terminates, not by a dissectible structure. Read this way, the anatomy stops being a false claim about tissue and becomes a coordinate system for interoceptive report. **[X]** The practitioner is not being told what exists inside the body; the practitioner is being given a shared vocabulary for locating sensation, which is a prerequisite for any transmissible discipline of self-observation. Granted that frame, the lateral assignment is significant. The text's fundamental organizing axis is left–right, and this is the correct axis for the phenomenon it is actually tracking. Autonomic control of the body is not laterally symmetric in its effects, and nowhere is this more accessible than in the nose. …","m":"The immediate application is diagnostic and takes under ten seconds. Close the mouth. Exhale through the nose onto the back of a hand, or occlude one nostril and then the other with light finger pressure and compare the effort of inspiration. One side will almost always be more patent. That side is dominant. Doing this four or five times across a working day, with a note of the time and a one-word note of current state — alert, flat, agitated, absorbed — generates within two weeks a personal record of a genuine ultradian rhythm alongside its subjective correlates. This is not mysticism; it is single-subject time-series data collection, and it is the same method the text's composers used. Most people who do it discover a period considerably more irregular than the text asserts and considerably more responsive to posture, exertion, meals, and stress than they expected. That discovery is itself the result: nasal dominance is not a clock, it is a state indicator, and it moves when state moves. **[E]** The second application concerns the *madhya* condition. Learn to recognize the changeover — the period, typically brief, when neither side is clearly dominant. Subjective reports and the small experimental literature both associate this window with a distinctive quality of attention: less goal-directed, more diffuse, less suited to grinding execution and more suited to reflection. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"39","t":"Gāndhārī, Hastijihvā, Pūṣā, Yaśasvinī, Alambuṣā","p":"PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT","d":"दक्षिणे हस्तिजिह्वा च पूषा कर्णे च दक्षिणे। यशस्विनी वामकर्णे आनने चाप्यलम्बुषा॥३९॥","i":"dakṣiṇe hastijihvā ca pūṣā karṇe ca dakṣiṇe | yaśasvinī vāmakarṇe ānane cāpyalambuṣā ||39||","f":"Hastijihvā terminates at the right eye, Pūṣā at the right ear, Yaśasvinī at the left ear, and Alambuṣā at the mouth.","a":"The verse completes the mapping of channels onto the cranial apertures: two eyes, two ears, one mouth, with the nostrils treated separately as the principal pair. Seven of the ten named channels terminate above the neck. This distribution is not arbitrary and it is not primarily anatomical. It is a map of where conscious sensory access is concentrated. Every aperture named here is a site of dense, highly-magnified cortical representation, and every one is a site over which voluntary motor control is unusually fine. The eyes, ears, and mouth are also the body's principal channels of exteroceptive intake, which places this inventory at the interface between what comes in from the world and what is available to inward attention. A discipline of self-observation that concentrates its coordinate system at these ports is concentrating it where the signal-to-noise ratio for introspection is highest. **[X]** The pairing structure carries additional information. Eyes are paired, ears are paired, nostrils are paired; the mouth and the two lower apertures are single. Each paired aperture receives its own named channel, and the names are assigned by side. The text is thus asserting that lateral differentiation applies not only at the nose but across the whole apertural system. …","m":"Use the verse as a scan sequence. Seated, mouth closed, move attention through the five named sites in the order given, resting two or three breaths at each: right eye, right ear, left ear, mouth, then the nostrils. Report to yourself, in a single word, the quality of sensation at each — temperature, pressure, tension, absence. The value is not in what is found but in the finding. Interoceptive accuracy — the correspondence between what a person believes about their internal state and what measurement shows — varies widely between individuals and is trainable. Low interoceptive accuracy is associated with difficulties in emotion identification and with poorer emotion regulation outcomes; the relationship is complex and not uniformly linear, since anxiety disorders often involve heightened rather than diminished interoceptive attention with distorted interpretation. **[S]** What structured scanning practice appears to improve is not raw sensitivity so much as the discrimination and labelling of internal signal, which is the component that regulation actually depends on. The practical rule that follows is worth stating plainly: a state you can name is a state you can act on. The five-point head scan takes ninety seconds and produces a name. That is its entire justification, and it is sufficient. ---","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"40","t":"Kuhū, Śaṅkhinī, and the Aperture Topology","p":"PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT","d":"कुहूश्च लिङ्गदेशे तु मूलस्थाने तु शङ्खिनी। एवं द्वारं समाश्रित्य तिष्ठन्ति दशनाडिकाः॥४०॥","i":"kuhūś ca liṅgadeśe tu mūlasthāne tu śaṅkhinī | evaṃ dvāraṃ samāśritya tiṣṭhanti daśanāḍikāḥ ||40||","f":"Kuhū terminates at the genital region and Śaṅkhinī at the perineal root. The ten channels are thus distributed with reference to the body's apertures.","a":"The verse closes the inventory and states its organizing principle explicitly: *dvāraṃ samāśritya* — the channels are defined by reference to the gates. The body is modelled as a bounded volume characterized by its openings. The two apertures added here are physiologically distinct from the cranial set in a way the text does not remark on but which its later practical instructions depend upon. The pelvic outlets are the only apertures under substantial voluntary skeletal-muscular control that also sit at the centre of dense autonomic regulation. The pelvic floor musculature is striated and voluntarily contractible; the visceral structures it surrounds are under autonomic control via the sacral parasympathetic outflow and the lumbar sympathetic chain. The region is therefore a genuine interface between voluntary and autonomic domains — one of the few places in the body where a deliberate motor act reliably perturbs autonomic tone. **[E]** This is the anatomical fact underlying the *bandha* techniques that the wider tantric and *hatha* literature builds on, and it explains why a text ostensibly about nostrils bothers to name these sites at all. Sustained pelvic-floor contraction alters intra-abdominal pressure, which alters venous return, which alters cardiac stretch-receptor firing, which feeds the baroreflex. …","m":"The extractable technique is a controlled autonomic perturbation, and it comes with a caution. Seated, exhale slowly and completely. At the end of exhalation, contract the pelvic floor moderately — not maximally — and hold for three to five seconds while the lungs remain empty. Release, and inhale normally. Repeat no more than four or five times. Practised this way the manoeuvre produces a brief, self-limiting vagal shift that many people find measurably settling. **[S]** The caution is not decorative. Forceful breath retention combined with strong abdominal or pelvic pressure reproduces the haemodynamics of a Valsalva manoeuvre and can cause substantial transient swings in blood pressure and cerebral perfusion. Anyone with uncontrolled hypertension, glaucoma, retinal disease, a history of cardiac arrhythmia, or who is pregnant should not perform breath-retention manoeuvres without medical advice. Reports of adverse events from aggressive retention practices exist and are not rare enough to ignore. **[E]** The traditional insistence that these techniques be learned under supervision was, in this respect, sound risk management rather than gatekeeping. The second and safer extraction is the aperture checklist as an anxiety intervention. Under acute stress, attention narrows and interoceptive signal is dominated by chest and throat. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"41","t":"The Three Principal Channels","p":"PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT","d":"इडा पिङ्गला सुषुम्ना च प्राणमार्गे समाश्रिताः। एता हि दशनाड्यस्तु देहमध्ये व्यवस्थिताः॥४१॥","i":"iḍā piṅgalā suṣumnā ca prāṇamārge samāśritāḥ | etā hi daśanāḍyas tu dehamadhye vyavasthitāḥ ||41||","f":"Iḍā, Piṅgalā and Suṣumnā are established along the principal path of *prāṇa*. These ten channels are arranged along the body's central region.","a":"The verse performs a hierarchical reduction: of ten channels, three are singled out as lying on the *prāṇa-mārga*, the main line. Everything the text subsequently does operationally uses only these three, and in practice only two, since Suṣumnā denotes the absence of lateral dominance rather than a third dominance. The reduction is methodologically important. A model with ten free parameters is unusable for real-time self-monitoring; a model with one binary variable and a transitional state is usable in seconds without instruments. What this verse accomplishes is dimensionality reduction on an observational scheme — the discarding of seven variables that cannot be measured in favour of one that can. **[X]** The seven secondary channels never reappear in any practical instruction in the remaining three hundred verses. They are inventory, not apparatus. Recognizing this is the difference between reading the *Svarodaya* as a system and reading it as a catalogue. The anatomical claim that the principal channels run *deha-madhye* — along the body's midline — invites the standard identification with the spinal cord, and the identification is usually made too quickly. …","m":"The operational lesson is to reduce the monitored variable set to what can actually be measured, and then measure it consistently. For self-regulation purposes the useful state variables are few: nasal dominance, respiratory rate, respiratory ratio of inspiration to expiration, and a single subjective arousal rating. Four numbers, obtainable in under thirty seconds without equipment. Tracked several times daily for a fortnight, they produce a dataset with real structure. Adding more variables degrades adherence without adding information — the constraint on self-monitoring is not what can be measured but what will actually be measured on a bad day. **[E, from the behavioural-adherence literature]** The second extraction concerns antiphase. Where a regulatory system alternates rather than varies continuously, the correct intervention is usually not to push harder on the active side but to shift the phase. Applied to attention: sustained effort on a difficult task does not fail because effort is insufficient but because the underlying arousal state has moved. The response supported by the vigilance literature is not intensification but a state change — a short interval of different activity, different posture, different sensory input — after which the original task is resumed at restored efficiency. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"42–45","t":"The Ten Vāyus: A Functional Decomposition of Autonomic Control","p":"PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT","d":"नामान्येतानि नाडीनां वातानान्तु वदाम्यहम्। प्राणोऽपानः समानश्च उदानो व्यान एव च॥४२॥ नागः कूर्मोऽथ कृकलो देवदत्तो धनञ्जयः। हृदि प्राणो वसेन्नित्यमपानो गुह्यमण्डले॥४३॥ समानो नाभिदेशे तु उदानः कण्ठमध्यगः। व्यानो व्यापी शरीरेषु प्रधानाः दशवायवः॥४४॥ प्राणाद्याः पञ्चविख्याताः नागाद्याः पञ्चवायवः। तेषामपि पञ्चानां स्थानानि च वदाम्यहम्॥४५॥","i":"nāmāny etāni nāḍīnāṃ vātānāṃ tu vadāmy aham | prāṇo 'pānaḥ samānaś ca udāno vyāna eva ca ||42|| nāgaḥ kūrmo 'tha kṛkalo devadatto dhanañjayaḥ | hṛdi prāṇo vasen nityam apāno guhyamaṇḍale ||43|| samāno nābhideśe tu udānaḥ kaṇṭhamadhyagaḥ | vyāno vyāpī śarīreṣu pradhānāḥ daśavāyavaḥ ||44|| prāṇādyāḥ pañcavikhyātāḥ nāgādyāḥ pañcavāyavaḥ | teṣām api pañcānāṃ sthānāni ca vadāmy aham ||45||","f":"The names of the winds associated with the channels are these: Prāṇa, Apāna, Samāna, Udāna and Vyāna; and Nāga, Kūrma, Kṛkala, Devadatta and Dhanañjaya. Prāṇa resides constantly in the region of the heart, Apāna in the pelvic region, Samāna at the navel, Udāna in the throat, and Vyāna throughout the body. These are the ten principal winds. Of the five beginning with Nāga, the locations will now also be stated.","a":"This is the most physiologically substantive passage in the opening section, and it is routinely mistranslated into vitalism by commentators who render *vāyu* as \"vital air\" and stop there. Read the assignments as they stand. Prāṇa at the heart and chest: cardiorespiratory function. Apāna in the pelvic region: excretory and reproductive function, and by extension the sacral parasympathetic and lumbar sympathetic outflows that govern them. Samāna at the navel: digestive and metabolic function, corresponding to the enteric and coeliac territory. Udāna at the throat: phonation, deglutition, and the upper-airway control that separates swallowing from breathing. Vyāna pervading the body: distributed circulatory and vasomotor function. This is not a list of airs. It is a five-way functional partition of visceral regulation, organized by anatomical region, and its regions correspond with striking accuracy to the actual segmental organization of autonomic innervation. **[E for the correspondence]** Consider what that correspondence requires. Cardiac and pulmonary structures are innervated sympathetically from the upper thoracic segments and parasympathetically by the vagus. Digestive structures receive sympathetic supply through the coeliac and superior mesenteric plexuses, centred on the region the text calls *nābhi*. …","m":"The scheme's practical yield is a differential vocabulary for internal state. Most people, asked what they feel under stress, report undifferentiated distress. The five-territory partition provides a structured alternative: chest and breath, throat, gut, pelvis, periphery. Asked to locate distress in one of five named regions, people produce reports that are more specific and more actionable — and specificity of emotional labelling is one of the better-supported predictors of regulation success in the affect-labelling literature, with imaging evidence that verbal labelling of affective states attenuates amygdala response and engages ventrolateral prefrontal cortex. **[S to E, depending on the specific measure]** The *udāna* territory yields the most directly usable technique. Slow exhalation through a gently narrowed glottis — the mechanism of *ujjāyī*, and equivalently of humming or of exhaling through pursed lips — extends expiratory duration, increases expiratory resistance, and thereby amplifies the vagal component of respiratory sinus arrhythmia. Measured effects on heart-rate variability are consistent and reproducible. **[E]** Humming specifically has been shown to produce large increases in nasal nitric oxide output, with implications for paranasal sinus ventilation that are still being characterized. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"46–47","t":"The Minor Vāyus: A Catalogue of Brainstem Reflexes","p":"PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT","d":"उद्गारे नाग आख्यातः कूर्म उन्मीलने स्मृतः। कृकलः क्षुतकृज्ज्ञेयो देवदत्तो विजृम्भणे॥४६॥ न जहाति मृतं वापि सर्वव्यापी धनञ्जयः। एते नाडीषु सर्वासु भ्रमन्ते जीवरूपिणः॥४७॥","i":"udgāre nāga ākhyātaḥ kūrma unmīlane smṛtaḥ | kṛkalaḥ kṣutakṛj jñeyo devadatto vijṛmbhaṇe ||46|| na jahāti mṛtaṃ vāpi sarvavyāpī dhanañjayaḥ | ete nāḍīṣu sarvāsu bhramante jīvarūpiṇaḥ ||47||","f":"Nāga governs eructation, Kūrma the movement of the eyelids, Kṛkala sneezing, Devadatta yawning. Dhanañjaya pervades everything and does not leave even the dead body. These circulate through all the channels as the forms of the living principle.","a":"Strip away the nomenclature and this is a list of stereotyped, involuntary, centrally-patterned motor acts: belching, blinking, sneezing, yawning. The composers of the text grouped these together and assigned them a category distinct from the tonic regulatory functions of the preceding verse. That grouping is defensible on grounds they could not have articulated. Every item on the list is a fixed action pattern generated by a brainstem central pattern generator, triggered by a specific afferent condition, executed as a stereotyped sequence, and only partially subject to voluntary control. The sneeze reflex is organized in the lateral medulla and involves a coordinated sequence of deep inspiration, glottic closure, and explosive expiration against briefly maintained resistance. Blinking is governed by circuitry involving the trigeminal afferents, the facial nucleus, and modulation from the basal ganglia — a modulation clinically visible in Parkinson's disease, where spontaneous blink rate falls markedly, and in schizophrenia and stimulant states, where it rises, making blink rate a crude but genuine peripheral index of central dopaminergic tone. **[E]** Belching involves transient lower oesophageal sphincter relaxation under vagal control. Yawning is generated in the paraventricular hypothalamus and brainstem and is associated with transitions in arousal state. …","m":"The usable insight here is that involuntary reflexes carry information about central state, and can be observed without instruments. Spontaneous blink rate is the most tractable. It is a crude, noisy, but real index of central dopaminergic activity, and it varies with cognitive load, fatigue, and engagement — dropping during demanding visual tasks and rising during disengagement and during periods of internally-directed thought. **[E for the dopamine association; S for the practical inference]** A person who notices their own blink rate climbing during reading has been given a signal that attentional engagement has lapsed, typically before the subjective sense of lapse arrives. Yawning is the more useful marker. Treated as a transition signal rather than a fatigue signal, a yawn is information: the arousal system is shifting. In practice this means that a yawn during focused work should not be interpreted as needing more coffee but as marking the end of an attentional block. The productive response is to close the block — finish the paragraph, note where to resume — rather than to push through, since the state that follows a yawn is measurably worse for sustained-attention tasks. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"50","t":"The Solar–Lunar Polarity and the Balanced State","p":"PART ONE: THE INSTRUMENT","d":"इडायां तु स्थितश्चन्द्रः पिङ्गलायां च भास्करः। सुषुम्ना शम्भुरूपेण शम्भुर्हंसस्वरूपतः॥५०॥","i":"iḍāyāṃ tu sthitaś candraḥ piṅgalāyāṃ ca bhāskaraḥ | suṣumnā śambhurūpeṇa śambhur haṃsasvarūpataḥ ||50||","f":"The moon resides in Iḍā, the sun in Piṅgalā. Suṣumnā is Śiva himself, and Śiva's essential nature is the *haṃsa* — the sound of the breath.","a":"This is the load-bearing verse of the entire text. Everything prescriptive that follows depends on it, and it makes three distinct claims that must be assessed separately. The first is that the two lateral channels carry opposed functional signatures — cooling, receptive, conserving on the left; heating, active, expending on the right. As a description of the autonomic correlates of nasal dominance this is a real empirical claim, and the evidence for it is genuine but weaker than its popularity suggests. Unilateral forced nostril breathing has been reported to produce lateralized autonomic effects: right-nostril breathing associated with increases in heart rate, blood pressure, oxygen consumption and blood glucose relative to left-nostril breathing, and left-nostril breathing associated with the reverse. The findings come predominantly from small studies, several from a limited number of laboratories, with variable methodological quality and inconsistent replication. Effect sizes where reported are modest. **[S]** The honest summary is that a lateralized autonomic effect of unilateral nostril breathing is plausible, has some empirical support, and is not established to the standard that would justify the confident claims made for it in the popular literature. The second claim concerns Suṣumnā, and it is stronger. …","m":"Three protocols follow, in ascending order of evidential support. *Lateral state modulation.* If seeking to reduce physiological arousal, occlude the right nostril and breathe through the left for three to five minutes at a natural rate. If seeking to raise arousal, reverse it. The evidence is suggestive rather than established, and part of any observed effect may be attributable to the attentional and expectancy components of the practice rather than to laterality as such. It is free, it is safe in healthy adults, and it is worth testing on oneself with an honest record of results. **[S]** *Transition-aware scheduling.* Track nasal dominance at intervals across a working day. When the changeover window is identified, do not schedule tasks requiring sustained precision into it. Use it instead for review, planning, or rest. This is a specific, falsifiable scheduling hypothesis derived from the text; it has not been tested and the reader who tries it should treat it as an experiment. **[X]** *Haṃsa observation.* Sit with the mouth closed. Attend to the sound and sensation of air at the nostrils. Do not alter the breath. When attention departs — it will, repeatedly — return it without commentary. Ten to fifteen minutes daily. This last is the intervention with the strongest support, and its mechanism is the least exotic. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"64","t":"The Five Elemental Phases Within a Cycle","p":"PART TWO: THE CLOCK","d":"वहेयुस्तद्घटीमध्ये पञ्चतत्त्वानि निर्दिशेत्। प्रतिपत्तो दिनान्याहुर्विपरीते विवर्जयेत्॥६४॥","i":"vaheyus tadghaṭīmadhye pañcatattvāni nirdiśet | pratipatto dināny āhur viparīte vivarjayet ||64||","f":"Within the period of a channel's flow, the five elemental phases arise in succession and should be identified. Reckoning is made from the first lunar day; when the observed order is contrary to the expected, one should refrain from undertaking action.","a":"The verse makes a nested-periodicity claim: within each period of nasal dominance, five sub-phases occur in a fixed sequence. It also introduces the text's characteristic error, and the two must be separated. The nesting claim is structurally sound and physiologically plausible. Biological rhythms are hierarchically organized as a general rule — circadian oscillators modulate ultradian ones, which in turn modulate rhythms in the seconds-to-minutes range, and the coupling between levels is the norm rather than the exception. **[E]** A practitioner attending closely to a two-hour dominance period would in fact observe systematic variation within it: changes in breath depth, in the felt temperature and moisture of the airflow, in the relative involvement of thoracic and abdominal movement, in subjective quality of attention. Assigning five named phases to these variations is a reasonable act of categorization by an observer without instruments. The specific correspondence — earth, water, fire, air, space, each with characteristic breath qualities detailed later in the text — is a taxonomy of interoceptive respiratory sensation. **[X]** Whether the sequence is fixed and whether the sub-periods are equal are separate matters, and the text asserts both without qualification. Neither is likely. …","m":"The extractable practice is nested rhythm awareness with the divinatory apparatus removed. Within a period of focused work, attend at intervals to the qualitative character of the breath: its depth, its rate, whether movement is predominantly abdominal or thoracic, the perceived temperature of inspired and expired air. These vary systematically and observably over the course of an hour, and they covary with cognitive state. Recording them alongside task performance produces a personal, honest dataset — one that permits genuine inference, because it records failures as well as successes. The critical methodological discipline is to record predictions before outcomes and to record all outcomes without exception. This is the single procedure that separates self-experimentation from self-deception, and it is precisely the procedure verse 64 abandons. A person who wishes to test whether their own performance varies with nasal dominance should log the dominance, log the intended task, and log the result — including on the occasions when the result contradicts expectation. Two weeks of such logging is worth more than any amount of received doctrine, in either direction. **[E, from basic methodology]** ---","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"65","t":"Lunar Phase and the Expected Opening","p":"PART TWO: THE CLOCK","d":"शुक्लपक्षे भवेद्वामा कृष्णपक्षे च दक्षिणा। जानीयात्प्रतिपत्पूर्वं योगी तद्यतमानसः॥६५॥","i":"śuklapakṣe bhaved vāmā kṛṣṇapakṣe ca dakṣiṇā | jānīyāt pratipatpūrvaṃ yogī tadyatamānasaḥ ||65||","f":"In the waxing fortnight the left channel should flow, in the waning fortnight the right, reckoned from the first lunar day. The practitioner, with mind fixed on this, should determine it.","a":"The verse asserts that nasal dominance at a defined reference time is keyed to the lunar cycle. This is a chronobiological claim and it should be evaluated as one. The evidence does not support it. The nasal cycle's period ranges from under an hour to several hours in healthy adults and shows no established lunar-monthly modulation. Its determinants that are documented — posture, unilateral pressure on the trunk, exercise, temperature, allergic status, hormonal state, sleep stage — are all considerably more powerful than any putative lunar influence, and no controlled study has demonstrated the association this verse claims. **[E, negative]** More broadly, the literature on lunar effects on human physiology and behaviour is a case study in publication bias and post-hoc analysis; the largest and best-controlled studies of lunar effects on sleep, on psychiatric admissions, and on birth rates find effects that are absent or vanishingly small, and the field's positive findings have not survived meta-analysis. **[E]** Why then does the claim appear here, stated with such confidence, in a text that elsewhere reports its observations accurately? The answer is that this verse is doing something different from the anatomical verses. It is not reporting an observation; it is importing a framework. …","m":"Discard the lunar reckoning. Retain the anchoring principle. Self-monitoring protocols fail on adherence rather than on design, and the intervention that most improves adherence is anchoring the observation to an existing, unavoidable, daily cue. Waking, the first meal, and the transition out of work are the strongest candidates because they occur reliably and require no additional memory load. **[E]** A practitioner tracking nasal dominance should attach the check to such a cue rather than to a clock time or a lunar day. The deeper application is epistemic and it is the most important lesson this section of the text offers. When a model produces a confident prediction, ask whether the prediction was derived from observation of the system or from the internal logic of the model's own vocabulary. Predictions of the second kind are common, feel identical from the inside, and are usually wrong. The verse before us is a clean specimen: a claim about the human airway generated entirely by the semantics of the word \"moon\". Recognizing that pattern is a transferable skill, and the *Śiva Svarodaya* provides an unusually clear training example precisely because its error is so visible from outside its own framework. ---","g":["E","E, negative","X"]},{"v":"66–67","t":"Deliberate Reversal of Dominance","p":"PART TWO: THE CLOCK","d":"शशाङ्कं वारयेद्रात्रौ दिवा वारयेद्भास्करम्। इत्यभ्यासरतो नित्यं स योगी नात्र संशयः॥६६॥ सूर्येण बध्यते सूर्यश्चन्द्रश्चन्द्रेण बध्यते। यो जानाति क्रियामेतां त्रैलोक्यं वशगं क्षणात्॥६७॥","i":"śaśāṅkaṃ vārayed rātrau divā vārayed bhāskaram | ity abhyāsarato nityaṃ sa yogī nātra saṃśayaḥ ||66|| sūryeṇa badhyate sūryaś candraś candreṇa badhyate | yo jānāti kriyām etāṃ trailokyaṃ vaśagaṃ kṣaṇāt ||67||","f":"One should check the left channel at night and the right by day. Whoever is constantly devoted to this practice is a *yogī*, without question. Sun is bound by sun and moon by moon; whoever knows this operation gains mastery in an instant.","a":"These two verses mark the transition from observation to intervention, and they contain the text's central technical proposition: nasal dominance is not merely readable but *steerable*. That proposition is correct, and it is one of the better-established facts in this entire corpus. Nasal dominance can be shifted by several mechanisms, all of which the tradition discovered and none of which it could explain. Sustained pressure applied to the lateral chest wall or axilla shifts congestion to the ipsilateral nostril, opening the contralateral one — an effect mediated by cutaneous pressure receptors modulating sympathetic outflow, and formalized in the tradition as the *yogadaṇḍa*, the crutch-staff used to apply exactly this pressure. **[E]** Lying on one side for several minutes produces the same effect. Vigorous unilateral forced breathing through the congested side, sustained, will often reverse dominance. Physical exertion decongests both sides through generalized sympathetic activation. **[E]** The claim in verse 67 is more specific and more interesting. *Sun is bound by sun, moon by moon*: the operation is described as like acting on like. The most coherent reading is that a dominant state is checked by deliberately engaging that same side — forcing breath through the already-open nostril until the cycle reverses — rather than by attempting to force the closed side open. …","m":"The reversal technique, stated plainly and with its evidence graded: To open a congested nostril, lie on the opposite side for five to ten minutes, or apply firm sustained pressure into the opposite axilla — a fist wedged under the armpit while seated works — for two to three minutes. Dominance will usually shift. **[E]** This is a reliable, immediate, zero-cost manipulation of an autonomic variable, and its reliability makes it useful as a demonstration: a person sceptical that voluntary action can alter autonomic state can verify the effect on themselves in under five minutes. Whether the shift produces the psychological consequences the text claims is the open question, and it is open in both directions. The autonomic effects of unilateral nostril breathing have some support **[S]**; the cognitive and behavioural effects have less. A reader who wants to know should test it on themselves with a pre-registered prediction and honest recording, as described in the commentary to verse 64. The more durable extraction is the training frame implicit in *abhyāsa-rataḥ*, constant devotion to practice. What is being trained is not the nostril but the act of overriding an automatic bodily default under voluntary control, repeatedly, without external reward. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"68–70","t":"Weekday, Fortnight, and the Boundary of Evidence","p":"PART TWO: THE CLOCK","d":"उदयं चन्द्रमार्गेण सूर्येणास्तमनं यदि। तदा ते गुणसंघाता विपरीतं तु विवर्जयेत्॥६८॥ गुरुशुक्रबुधेन्दूनां वासरे वामनाडिका। सिद्धिदा सर्वकार्येषु शुक्लपक्षे विशेषतः॥६९॥ अर्काङ्गारकसौरीणां वासरे दक्षनाडिका। स्मर्तव्या चरकार्येषु कृष्णपक्षे विशेषतः॥७०॥","i":"udayaṃ candramārgeṇa sūryeṇāstamanaṃ yadi | tadā te guṇasaṃghātā viparītaṃ tu vivarjayet ||68|| guruśukrabudhendūnāṃ vāsare vāmanāḍikā | siddhidā sarvakāryeṣu śuklapakṣe viśeṣataḥ ||69|| arkāṅgārakasaurīṇāṃ vāsare dakṣanāḍikā | smartavyā carakāryeṣu kṛṣṇapakṣe viśeṣataḥ ||70||","f":"If the day begins with the left channel and ends with the right, outcomes are favourable; the reverse should be avoided. On Thursday, Friday, Wednesday and Monday the left channel yields success in all undertakings, particularly during the waxing fortnight. On Sunday, Tuesday and Saturday the right channel should be observed for active undertakings, particularly during the waning fortnight.","a":"These verses have no physiological content. The weekday assignments are planetary correspondences imported wholesale from *jyotiṣa*, and the fortnight qualification repeats the error analysed under verse 65. Nasal dominance is not modulated by the day of the week, and no mechanism by which it could be has ever been proposed. **[E, negative]** They are included in full here for two reasons. The first is completeness: a book that decodes only the defensible verses is not a decoding of the text but a selection from it, and the reader is entitled to see where the material fails. The second is that the *pattern* of failure is analytically informative, and it is the same pattern that governs the majority of the text's remaining prescriptive content. Notice how the framework is constructed. The planets are already classified in the inherited tradition as benefic — Jupiter, Venus, Mercury, Moon — and malefic — Sun, Mars, Saturn. The left channel is already classified as cool, gentle and nourishing, the right as hot and forceful. The verse simply aligns two pre-existing classification schemes. No observation is required and none is reported. This is the operation cognitive psychology describes as schema-driven inference: when a new domain is encountered, an available structured framework is applied to it, and the framework's structure is mistaken for discovered order. …","m":"Nothing in verses 69 and 70 should be applied. What survives is the underlying principle, which is state-dependent task scheduling, and which does have empirical support in a form unconnected to planets. Cognitive performance varies systematically across the day as a function of circadian phase and homeostatic sleep pressure, and the direction of that variation differs by task type: tasks requiring sustained focused attention and inhibitory control tend to peak near an individual's circadian arousal maximum, while tasks benefiting from broader, less filtered associative access can show the opposite pattern, performing better at the individual's non-optimal time — the so-called synchrony effect, with its counterintuitive reversal for insight problems. **[S to E, depending on the specific effect; the basic circadian modulation of vigilance is well established, the insight reversal less so]** The practical scheme is therefore to identify one's own chronotype, note the times of day at which focused work is reliably easier and harder, and schedule accordingly: analytic and error-sensitive work at the arousal peak, exploratory and generative work at the trough. This is what verses 69 and 70 are gesturing at with the wrong variable. The observation that different classes of work suit different physiological states is correct. …","g":["E","E, negative","S"]},{"v":"71–72","t":"The Elemental Sequence and Its Timing","p":"PART TWO: THE CLOCK","d":"प्रथमं वहते वायुर्द्वितीयं च तथानलः। तृतीयं वहते भूमिश्चतुर्थं वरुणो वहेत्॥७१॥ सार्धद्विघटिके पञ्च क्रमेणैवोदयन्ति च। क्रमादेकैकनाड्यां च तत्त्वानां पृथगुद्भवः॥७२॥","i":"prathamaṃ vahate vāyur dvitīyaṃ ca tathānalaḥ | tṛtīyaṃ vahate bhūmiś caturthaṃ varuṇo vahet ||71|| sārdhadvighaṭike pañca krameṇaivodayanti ca | kramād ekaikanāḍyāṃ ca tattvānāṃ pṛthag udbhavaḥ ||72||","f":"Air flows first, then fire, then earth, then water. The five arise in sequence within a period of two and a half *ghaṭikās*, each element arising distinctly in turn within each channel's flow.","a":"The arithmetic first, because it is frequently mangled in commentary. A *ghaṭikā* is one sixtieth of a nychthemeron, that is, twenty-four minutes. Two and a half *ghaṭikās* is therefore sixty minutes. The verse states that five elemental phases arise within this hour, which yields twelve minutes per phase. Other passages in the text assign two and a half *ghaṭikās* to each individual element rather than to the full set, producing a two-and-a-half-hour dominance period. The recension is internally inconsistent, and different manuscript traditions resolve the inconsistency differently. The inconsistency is itself informative. A schematic system imposed on an irregular natural rhythm will generate exactly this: competing regularizations, each internally tidy, none matching the phenomenon. The measured nasal cycle has a modal period in the region of two to four hours in many individuals but with enormous variance, and a substantial minority of healthy people show no clear regular alternation at all. **[E]** Any fixed number the tradition settled on would be wrong for most people most of the time. What the elemental sequence is actually describing, stripped of cosmology, is a taxonomy of respiratory sensation. …","m":"Use the elemental categories as a descriptive vocabulary, explicitly labelled as vocabulary rather than as ontology. A workable adaptation: at each observation, classify the current breath on three dimensions rather than five categories — location of movement, abdominal to thoracic; rate, slow to fast; and depth, shallow to full. These three are directly observable, mutually independent, and externally verifiable with a phone camera and a stopwatch if desired. They capture most of what the elemental scheme was tracking without importing its metaphysics. The second point is a caution against the training of confidence without accuracy. Any practice that involves making fine subjective discriminations will increase reported certainty about those discriminations. Increased certainty is not increased accuracy, and in interoceptive domains the two dissociate reliably. **[E]** Practitioners of any interoceptive discipline should periodically check their reports against an external measure — pulse against a monitor, breath rate against a clock, nasal patency against a mirror-fogging test. The discipline of external calibration is what would have transformed the *Śiva Svarodaya* from a proto-science into a science, and it is available to any reader for the cost of a stopwatch. ---","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"73–74","t":"The Twelve Transits","p":"PART TWO: THE CLOCK","d":"अहोरात्रस्य मध्ये तु ज्ञेया द्वादश संक्रमाः। वृषकर्कटकन्यालिमृगमीना निशाकरे॥७३॥ मेषसिंहौ च कुम्भश्च तुला च मिथुनं धनुः। उदये दक्षिणे ज्ञेयः शुभाशुभविनिर्णयः॥७४॥","i":"ahorātrasya madhye tu jñeyā dvādaśa saṃkramāḥ | vṛṣakarkaṭakanyālimṛgamīnā niśākare ||73|| meṣasiṃhau ca kumbhaś ca tulā ca mithunaṃ dhanuḥ | udaye dakṣiṇe jñeyaḥ śubhāśubhavinirṇayaḥ ||74||","f":"Twelve transits are to be recognized within the day and night. Taurus, Cancer, Virgo, Scorpio, Capricorn and Pisces belong to the left channel; Aries, Leo, Aquarius, Libra, Gemini and Sagittarius to the right. By these the determination of favourable and unfavourable is made.","a":"The zodiacal assignment is astrology and has no physiological content. The division is the standard even–odd classification of the signs, with the even-numbered signs — traditionally feminine, receptive — assigned to the left channel and the odd-numbered — masculine, active — to the right. As with the weekday scheme, two pre-existing classifications are aligned, and the alignment is presented as discovery. **[E, negative]** There is, however, one structural feature worth examining, because it is the strongest thing in this section and it is easy to miss beneath the zodiac. The verse asserts twelve transitions across a full day and night. Twenty-four hours divided by twelve gives two hours per phase. This is a claim about the period of the underlying rhythm, and expressed in this form — twelve alternations per day, roughly two hours each — it is a reasonable approximation of the nasal cycle in the population where a clear cycle is observable. **[S]** The zodiacal labels are decoration. The number twelve is a measurement, and it is roughly the right measurement. That a pre-modern tradition arrived at an approximately two-hourly figure for an autonomic rhythm that was not described in Western medicine until 1895 is the substantive fact here, and it survives the removal of everything else in the verse. This is worth pausing on, because it is the pattern the whole book turns on. …","m":"The twelve-transit figure is the one usable output. Assume, provisionally, an alternation period of approximately two hours, and structure work in blocks of that order with deliberate transitions between them. This is close to the block length that the ultradian and vigilance literatures converge on independently, and it is close to what the productivity literature has arrived at by trial and error. **[S]** The more valuable extraction is the precommitment principle stripped of its divinatory content. Decide in advance, at a time of low pressure, which categories of work occupy which parts of the day, and then do not revisit the decision during the day itself. The gain does not come from the schedule being correct. It comes from the schedule existing, because it converts a recurring high-friction decision into a settled one, and the depletion of self-regulatory capacity by repeated deliberation is well documented even where the stronger claims of the ego-depletion literature have failed to replicate. **[S]** What the *Śiva Svarodaya* offers here, once its astrology is discarded, is a precommitment device that its users found credible enough to follow. The credibility came from the cosmology. The benefit came from the structure. …","g":["E","E, negative","S","X"]},{"v":"96","t":"The Void Channel and the Inversion Rule","p":"PART THREE: THE LEDGER OF ACTIONS","d":"शून्यनाड्या विपर्यस्तं यत्पूर्वं प्रतिपादितम्। जायते नान्यथा चैव यथा सर्वज्ञभाषितम्॥९६॥","i":"śūnyanāḍyā viparyastaṃ yatpūrvaṃ pratipāditam | jāyate nānyathā caiva yathā sarvajñabhāṣitam ||96||","f":"When the channel is empty, the results previously described are reversed. It happens in no other way; so it has been declared by the omniscient.","a":"*Śūnya* means empty, void, null. The *śūnya nāḍī* is the non-flowing side — the congested nostril at any given moment — and the verse establishes a formal inversion rule: outcomes assigned to a channel's flow invert when action is undertaken on the empty side. The first thing to note is that this is a logical operation, not an observation. The text is not reporting that it watched inverted outcomes; it is closing its system. A two-state model requires a rule for the complementary case, and inversion is the simplest available. What the verse actually demonstrates is that the *Svarodaya*'s prescriptive apparatus had reached the stage of formal systematization, where internal completeness begins to drive content rather than observation driving it. **[X]** The second point concerns the concept of emptiness itself, and it is more interesting. The congested nostril is not inert. It is the site of *active* vascular engorgement, produced by sustained parasympathetic drive to the venous sinusoids. **[E]** From the standpoint of autonomic outflow, the \"empty\" side is the busy side; the \"flowing\" side is the one where sympathetic vasoconstriction has emptied the tissue. The text's phenomenology — one side full of air, the other empty — is an exact inversion of the underlying physiology, in which one side is full of blood and the other drained. This inversion is not a trivial curiosity. …","m":"The usable content is the diagnostic heuristic, and it generalizes well beyond this text. In any body of received practical knowledge — clinical tradition, technical folklore, an organization's institutional habits — the claims justified by reference to authority, seniority, or \"how it has always been done\" are systematically less reliable than the claims stated flatly with an implicit invitation to check. The reason is structural rather than moral: claims that can be checked do not need authority behind them, so authority accumulates precisely around the claims that cannot. Scanning a practice for the distribution of its appeals to authority is a fast, cheap way to locate its weak points. **[X, methodological]** The second extraction is a caution about interoceptive inference. The felt quality of a bodily state is a downstream product, and the intuitive causal story attached to it is usually wrong. A blocked nose feels like an obstruction and is in fact an act of vascular engorgement. A racing heart feels like the cause of anxiety and is in substantial part a consequence of it. Interoceptive signals are reliable as *indices* of state and unreliable as *explanations* of it. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"97","t":"Confrontation and the Open Nostril","p":"PART THREE: THE LEDGER OF ACTIONS","d":"व्यवहारे खलोच्चाटे द्वेषिविद्यादिवञ्चके। कुपितस्वामिचौराद्ये पूर्णस्थाः स्युर्भयङ्कराः॥९७॥","i":"vyavahāre khaloccāṭe dveṣividyādivañcake | kupitasvāmicaurādye pūrṇasthāḥ syur bhayaṅkarāḥ ||97||","f":"In disputes, in driving off a hostile party, in outwitting an adversary, and when confronting an enraged superior or a thief, those positioned on the side of the open channel hold the formidable position.","a":"The instruction is spatial and tactical: in a confrontation, place the adversary on the side of one's own open nostril. Stripped of the divinatory frame, the verse makes a claim about the conditions under which a person performs well in adversarial encounters, and it makes it in terms of a readable autonomic state. There is a real physiological relationship in the vicinity of this claim, and it runs in a direction the text did not suspect. Acute threat produces generalized sympathetic activation, and one of its immediate effects is nasal decongestion — vasoconstriction of the turbinate sinusoids drains both sides and increases total airway patency. **[E]** This is functionally sensible: a threatened animal needs airway. The relationship between arousal and nasal patency is therefore not merely correlational but causal, and it runs from central state to nose rather than the reverse. A person whose nostrils are open is, statistically, a person whose sympathetic tone is elevated. That gives the verse a defensible reading it does not know it has. Open airflow is a marker of a mobilized state, and mobilized states are, within limits, advantageous in confrontation: catecholamine release increases muscular power output, raises pain threshold, accelerates simple reaction time, and narrows attention onto threat-relevant cues. …","m":"Run a state check before any high-stakes interaction: nasal patency, resting pulse at the wrist over fifteen seconds, and the location of muscular tension. Thirty seconds, no equipment. The check has value only if it is paired with a response, and the response should be selected by task type rather than by a general preference for calm. For an encounter whose demands are primarily reactive — physical, fast, adversarial — a moderately elevated state is functional and attempting to suppress it is counterproductive. For an encounter whose demands are primarily analytic — negotiation, complex explanation, judgement under ambiguity — arousal above one's optimum degrades exactly the capacities required, and downregulation is warranted. The downregulation technique with the best support is the extended exhalation described under verses 42–45: inhalation of roughly four seconds, exhalation of roughly six, for two to three minutes. This produces measurable reductions in heart rate and increases in heart-rate variability within that timeframe. **[E]** For the opposite direction, the evidence is weaker and the practical options are cruder: brief vigorous physical exertion, cold exposure to the face or hands, or faster breathing with emphasis on inhalation. …","g":["E"]},{"v":"100–101","t":"The Three-State Model and Deliberate Switching","p":"PART THREE: THE LEDGER OF ACTIONS","d":"चन्द्रचारे विषहते सूर्यो बलिवशं नयेत्। सुषुम्नायां भवेन्मोक्ष एको देवस्त्रिधा स्थितः॥१००॥ शुभान्यशुभकार्याणि क्रियन्तेऽहर्निशं यदा। तदा कार्यानुरोधेन कार्यं नाडीप्रचालनम्॥१०१॥","i":"candracāre viṣahate sūryo balivaśaṃ nayet | suṣumnāyāṃ bhaven mokṣa eko devas tridhā sthitaḥ ||100|| śubhāny aśubhakāryāṇi kriyante 'harniśaṃ yadā | tadā kāryānurodhena kāryaṃ nāḍīpracālanam ||101||","f":"Under left flow, poison is neutralized; under right flow, the powerful are subdued; in Suṣumnā, release is attained. One principle stands in three conditions. Since actions of every kind are performed at all hours, the channel should accordingly be set flowing to match the action.","a":"These two verses contain the intellectual core of the treatise, compressed into eight lines, and they should be read together. Verse 100's closing phrase — *eko devas tridhā sthitaḥ*, one principle established threefold — is the text's own statement of its model. It asserts that the three channel conditions are not three separate things but three states of a single system. This is a tri-state model of a unitary regulatory apparatus, and it is a considerably better description of autonomic organization than the two-state model that dominated twentieth-century physiology. The classical framing treats sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions as reciprocal and opposed, a single axis with two directions. That framing is now known to be inadequate. The two divisions can be co-activated, co-inhibited, or reciprocally activated depending on the behavioural context, and their relationship is better described in two dimensions than one. **[E]** Autonomic space contains states of high sympathetic with high parasympathetic drive — the profile associated with certain forms of freezing and orienting — and states of low drive in both, which are not equivalent to reciprocal balance. …","m":"The protocol implied by verse 101, stated in modern terms: **Classify the task.** Does it demand sustained precision, memory encoding, social attunement, or tolerance of ambiguity? Or does it demand speed, force, risk tolerance, and decisive action? Most work decomposes into blocks of one kind or the other, and the decomposition is usually obvious once the question is asked. **Determine the current state.** Nasal patency, pulse, breath rate and depth, muscular tension. Thirty seconds. **Induce the required state if it is absent.** For downregulation: extended-exhalation breathing at approximately six breaths per minute for three to five minutes, which reliably shifts cardiac autonomic balance. **[E]** For upregulation: brief physical exertion. For the transitional or diffuse state: reduce sensory input, close the eyes, stop instrumental activity for several minutes. **Verify, then act.** Re-check the index before beginning. This is a testable protocol and it should be tested rather than believed. The component with the strongest support is the downregulation step; the component with the least is the claim that nasal dominance meaningfully indexes the induced state. A reader who runs it as a personal experiment, with predictions recorded before outcomes, will learn more in a month than this commentary can establish. ---","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"102–105","t":"The Left Column: Actions of Establishment","p":"PART THREE: THE LEDGER OF ACTIONS","d":"स्थिरकर्मण्यलङ्कारे दूराध्वगमने तथा। आश्रमे धर्मप्रासादे वस्तूनां सङ्ग्रहेऽपि च॥१०२॥ वापीकूपतडागादिप्रतिष्ठास्तम्भदेवयोः। यात्रादाने विवाहे च वस्त्रालङ्कारभूषणे॥१०३॥ शान्तिके पौष्टिके चैव दिव्यौषधिरसायने। स्वस्वामिदर्शने मित्रे वाणिज्ये कणसंग्रहे॥१०४॥ गृहप्रवेशे सेवायां कृषौ च बीजवपने। शुभकर्मणि सङ्घे च निर्गमे च शुभः शशी॥१०५॥","i":"sthirakarmaṇy alaṅkāre dūrādhvagamane tathā | āśrame dharmaprāsāde vastūnāṃ saṅgrahe 'pi ca ||102|| vāpīkūpataḍāgādipratiṣṭhāstambhadevayoḥ | yātrādāne vivāhe ca vastrālaṅkārabhūṣaṇe ||103|| śāntike pauṣṭike caiva divyauṣadhirasāyane | svasvāmidarśane mitre vāṇijye kaṇasaṃgrahe ||104|| gṛhapraveśe sevāyāṃ kṛṣau ca bījavapane | śubhakarmaṇi saṅghe ca nirgame ca śubhaḥ śaśī ||105||","f":"The left channel is favourable for enduring work, for ornamentation, for long journeys, for founding hermitages and temples, and for accumulating goods; for the consecration of wells, tanks, pillars and images; for setting out, for giving, for marriage, and for matters of dress and adornment; for rites of pacification and nourishment, for preparing medicines and restoratives, for meeting one's superior or a friend, for trade and for storing grain; for entering a house, for service, for agriculture and sowing, for beneficial undertakings, for assemblies, and for departure.","a":"The list looks arbitrary until it is sorted by demand rather than by content. Do that, and four categories emerge, each of which has a defensible relationship to the parasympathetic-leaning state the text assigns it to. The first is *long time horizon*. Founding a temple, digging a well, sowing seed, storing grain, contracting a marriage, accumulating goods: every item is an action whose payoff is distant and whose cost is immediate. These are the archetypal delayed-gratification decisions. The relevant finding is that intertemporal preference is state-dependent rather than fixed. Acute stress and sympathetic activation shift choice toward smaller-sooner rewards, an effect demonstrated with both laboratory stressors and pharmacological manipulation, and mediated at least in part by cortisol and catecholaminergic action on prefrontal-striatal valuation circuitry. **[E]** Conversely, higher resting vagal tone is associated with better performance on tasks requiring inhibitory control and with reduced impulsive choice, though the literature here is correlational and the causal direction is not settled. **[S]** A tradition that says *do not commit to a twenty-year project while sympathetically aroused* has stated something that survives translation. The second category is *affiliative and social*. Meeting one's superior, meeting a friend, assemblies, service, giving. …","m":"The transferable rule: **do not make long-horizon commitments while physiologically activated.** This is the best-supported single application in the entire text, and it is routinely violated. Major financial decisions, contractual commitments, resignations, confrontational emails and relationship-defining conversations are disproportionately initiated during states of acute arousal, and the reliable consequence is a shift of preference toward the immediate. **[E]** The practical rule follows without needing any of the text's metaphysics: install a mandatory delay between the impulse to make an irreversible commitment and the making of it, and use the delay to downregulate rather than to deliberate. Deliberation while aroused tends to rationalize the aroused preference; changing the state first changes what is deliberated. A workable version: for any decision that is expensive to reverse, require that it be made only after a state check and only when the state check comes back settled — low resting pulse, unforced breath, no muscular bracing. The check takes half a minute and the rule can be stated in one sentence to another person, which matters, because precommitment rules that can be explained are the ones that survive contact with pressure. For social encounters, the corresponding application is a two-minute downregulation before any conversation that matters. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"106–112","t":"The Left Column Continued, and a Nested Condition","p":"PART THREE: THE LEDGER OF ACTIONS","d":"विद्यारम्भादिकार्येषु बान्धवानां च दर्शने। जन्ममोक्षे च धर्मे च दीक्षायां मन्त्रसाधने॥१०६॥ कालविज्ञानसूत्रे तु चतुष्पादगृहागमे। कालव्याधिचिकित्सायां स्वामिसंबोधने तथा॥१०७॥ गजाश्वरोहणे धन्वि गजाश्वानां च बन्धने। परोपकारणे चैव निधीनां स्थापने तथा॥१०८॥ गीतवाद्यादिनृत्यादौ नृत्यशास्त्रविचारणे। पुरग्रामनिवेशे च तिलकक्षेत्रधारणे॥१०९॥ आर्तिशोकविषादे च ज्वरिते मूर्च्छितेऽपि वा। स्वजनस्वामिसम्बन्धे अन्नादेर्दारुसङ्ग्रहे॥११०॥ स्त्रीणां दन्तादिभूषायां वृष्ट्यागमने तथा। गुरुपूजाविषादीनां चालने च वरानने॥१११॥ इडायां सिद्धिदं प्रोक्तं योगाभ्यासादिकर्म च। तत्रापि वर्जयेद्वायुं तेज आकाशमेव च॥११२॥","i":"vidyārambhādikāryeṣu bāndhavānāṃ ca darśane | janmamokṣe ca dharme ca dīkṣāyāṃ mantrasādhane ||106|| kālavijñānasūtre tu catuṣpādagṛhāgame | kālavyādhicikitsāyāṃ svāmisaṃbodhane tathā ||107|| gajāśvarohaṇe dhanvi gajāśvānāṃ ca bandhane | paropakāraṇe caiva nidhīnāṃ sthāpane tathā ||108|| gītavādyādinṛtyādau nṛtyaśāstravicāraṇe | puragrāmaniveśe ca tilakakṣetradhāraṇe ||109|| ārtiśokaviṣāde ca jvarite mūrcchite 'pi vā | svajanasvāmisambandhe annāder dārusaṅgrahe ||110|| strīṇāṃ dantādibhūṣāyāṃ vṛṣṭyāgamane tathā | gurupūjāviṣādīnāṃ cālane ca varānane ||111|| iḍāyāṃ siddhidaṃ proktaṃ yogābhyāsādikarma ca | tatrāpi varjayed vāyuṃ teja ākāśam eva ca ||112||","f":"The left channel is favourable for beginning study, for meeting kin, for matters of birth and release, for religious observance, initiation and mantra practice; for the study of chronology, for the acquisition of livestock, for the treatment of chronic illness and for addressing a superior; for mounting and tethering animals, for archery, for acts of benefit to others and for depositing valuables; for song, music and dance and their study, for founding settlements, and for matters of land; in conditions of distress, grief, dejection, fever or fainting, in dealings with kin and superiors, and in gathering food and timber; in personal adornment, at the onset of rain, and in the worship of the teacher. Practices such as yoga are accomplished under the left channel — but even then, the air, fire and space phases are to be avoided.","a":"Three items in this block deserve treatment on their own, and the last verse changes the structure of the whole prescriptive system. *Vidyā-ārambha* — the beginning of study — heads the list, and its assignment to the parasympathetic-leaning state is defensible on the memory literature. Encoding and consolidation are both modulated by autonomic and neuroendocrine state, but not in a simple direction, and the details matter. Moderate stress at the time of encoding can *enhance* memory for emotionally salient material, through amygdala-mediated modulation of hippocampal consolidation involving noradrenaline and glucocorticoids. **[E]** But the same elevation impairs the encoding of neutral, complex, relational material, and impairs retrieval — hence the well-documented dissociation whereby stress before learning and stress before recall have opposite consequences. **[E]** For the acquisition of a structured body of neutral knowledge, which is what *vidyā-ārambha* denotes, a low-arousal state is the correct prescription. The text's assignment is right, and right for reasons that took experimental psychology most of a century to characterize. *Kāla-vyādhi-cikitsā* — the treatment of chronic disease — is the second, and it is grouped in verse 110 with distress, grief, fever and fainting. …","m":"Two applications, one strong and one methodological. **Learning.** Schedule the acquisition of new, structured, non-emotional material into low-arousal periods, and schedule retrieval practice separately. The dissociation between stress effects on encoding and on retrieval means that the state you learn in and the state you test in should be considered separately, and that pre-examination arousal impairs retrieval even for well-encoded material. **[E]** The practical consequences: study when settled, and invest in downregulation immediately before any high-stakes recall task rather than in last-minute encoding, which is the reverse of what most people do. Consolidation is the other half. Memory consolidation is strongly sleep-dependent, with slow-wave sleep implicated in the consolidation of declarative material, and sleep deprivation impairs subsequent encoding capacity as well. **[E]** The traditional prescription of a calm state for the beginning of study is incomplete without its modern complement: the state *after* study matters as much as the state during it. **The methodological application** concerns verse 112 and is the more valuable of the two. When a personal rule of thumb — about diet, about productivity, about mood — starts requiring exceptions to survive, the exceptions are data. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"114–121","t":"The Right Column: Actions of Force","p":"PART THREE: THE LEDGER OF ACTIONS","d":"कठिनक्रूरविद्यानां पठने पाठने तथा। स्त्रीसङ्गवेश्यागमने महानौकाधिरोहणे॥११४॥ भ्रष्टकार्ये सुरापाने वीरमन्त्राद्युपासने। विह्वलोध्वंसदेशादौ विषदाने च वैरिणाम्॥११५॥ शास्त्राभ्यासे च गमने मृगयापशुविक्रये। इष्टिकाकाष्ठपाषाणरत्नघर्षणे दारुणे॥११६॥ गत्यभ्यासे यन्त्रतन्त्रे दुर्गपर्वतारोहणे। द्यूते चौर्ये गजाश्वादिरथसाधनवाहने॥११७॥ व्यायामे मारणोच्चाटे षट्कर्मादिसाधने। यक्षिणीयक्षवेतालविषभूतादिनिग्रहे॥११८॥ खरोष्ट्रमहिषादीनां गजाश्वारोहणे तथा। नदीजलौघतरणे भेषजे लिपिलेखने॥११९॥ मारणे मोहने स्तम्भे विद्वेषोच्चाटने वशे। प्रेरणे कर्षणे क्षोभे दाने च क्रयविक्रये॥१२०॥ प्रेताकर्षणविद्वेषशत्रुनिग्रहणेऽपि च। खड्गहस्ते वैरियुद्धे भोगे वा राजदर्शने। भोज्ये स्नाने व्यवहारे दीप्तकार्ये रविः शुभः॥१२१॥","i":"kaṭhinakrūravidyānāṃ paṭhane pāṭhane tathā | strīsaṅgaveśyāgamane mahānaukādhirohaṇe ||114|| bhraṣṭakārye surāpāne vīramantrādyupāsane | vihvalodhvaṃsadeśādau viṣadāne ca vairiṇām ||115|| śāstrābhyāse ca gamane mṛgayāpaśuvikraye | iṣṭikākāṣṭhapāṣāṇaratnagharṣaṇe dāruṇe ||116|| gatyabhyāse yantratantre durgaparvatārohaṇe | dyūte caurye gajāśvādirathasādhanavāhane ||117|| vyāyāme māraṇoccāṭe ṣaṭkarmādisādhane | yakṣiṇīyakṣavetālaviṣabhūtādinigrahe ||118|| kharoṣṭramahiṣādīnāṃ gajāśvārohaṇe tathā | nadījalaughataraṇe bheṣaje lipilekhane ||119|| māraṇe mohane stambhe vidveṣoccāṭane vaśe | preraṇe karṣaṇe kṣobhe dāne ca krayavikraye ||120|| pretākarṣaṇavidveṣaśatrunigrahaṇe 'pi ca | khaḍgahaste vairiyuddhe bhoge vā rājadarśane | bhojye snāne vyavahāre dīptakārye raviḥ śubhaḥ ||121||","f":"The right channel is favourable for the study and teaching of difficult and severe disciplines, for sexual congress, and for embarking on a large vessel; for disreputable actions, for drinking, for the practice of forceful mantras, for entering disturbed or ruined territory, and for acting against enemies; for the study of technical treatises, for setting out, for hunting and trading in animals, and for working brick, timber, stone and gems; for training in movement, for mechanical and tantric contrivance, for scaling forts and mountains, for gambling, for theft, and for driving animals and vehicles; for physical exercise and for the forceful rites; for riding beasts of burden, for crossing rivers in flood, for medicine and for writing; for the rites of subjugation, for impelling and agitating, for buying and selling; for confrontation with enemies, for bearing arms, for enjoyment, for audience with a ruler, for eating, bathing and transactions, and for all fierce undertakings.","a":"A note on content before analysis. This block contains instructions for poisoning adversaries, for theft, and for the *ṣaṭkarma* — the six tantric rites, which include *māraṇa*, the rite of killing. These are presented here as what they are: historical material from a specific ritual tradition, translated for scholarly purposes. Nothing in them is endorsed, and the rites in question have no efficacy of the kind claimed. Their inclusion is a fact about the text. Sorted by demand, the right column resolves into three categories, all of which correspond to functions of sympathetic mobilization. *Gross motor output under load.* Physical exercise, riding, scaling fortifications, crossing floodwaters, driving animals, stonework, timber-felling, brickmaking. Sympathetic activation increases cardiac output, redirects blood flow to skeletal muscle, mobilizes glucose and free fatty acids, and increases muscular force production and speed of contraction. **[E]** For tasks whose limiting factor is power output rather than precision, the aroused state is straightforwardly superior, and the text's assignment is correct without qualification. *Confrontation and risk.* Combat, gambling, theft, entering hostile territory, subduing enemies, audience with a king. The relevant physiology is the same, with the addition of the well-documented effects of arousal on risk preference. …","m":"The clean application is to physical training and to effortful practice, and it is close to what sports science already does. For any task whose limiting factor is power, speed or force — physical training, but also confrontational conversations and time-pressured execution — a raised state is functional and should be induced deliberately rather than waited for. The reliable methods are physical: brief high-intensity movement, faster breathing weighted toward inhalation, cold exposure to the face. Note the caution from verse 97: voluntary hyperventilation induces hypocapnia and cerebral vasoconstriction, so it is appropriate before physical exertion and inappropriate before anything requiring judgement. **[E]** For effortful practice, the application is the distinction the text makes. Separate sessions of *acquisition* — first exposure to material, low arousal, unhurried, no time pressure — from sessions of *drilling* — retrieval under time pressure, difficult conditions, deliberate discomfort. Combining them, which is what most study looks like in practice, produces the worst of both: acquisition degraded by pressure and drilling made too easy by the availability of the source material. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"122–123","t":"Sleep Posture, Digestion, and the Text's Best Clinical Observation","p":"PART THREE: THE LEDGER OF ACTIONS","d":"भुक्तमार्गेण मन्दाग्नौ स्त्रीणां वश्यादिकर्मणि। शयनं सूर्यवाहेन कर्तव्यं सर्वदा बुधैः॥१२२॥ क्रूराणि सर्वकर्माणि चराणि विविधानि च। तानि सिध्यन्ति सूर्येण नात्र कार्या विचारणा॥१२३॥","i":"bhuktamārgeṇa mandāgnau strīṇāṃ vaśyādikarmaṇi | śayanaṃ sūryavāhena kartavyaṃ sarvadā budhaiḥ ||122|| krūrāṇi sarvakarmāṇi carāṇi vividhāni ca | tāni sidhyanti sūryeṇa nātra kāryā vicāraṇā ||123||","f":"When digestion is sluggish after eating, and in matters of attraction, the wise should always lie down with the right channel flowing. All harsh and mobile undertakings of every kind succeed under the right channel; there is no need to deliberate on this.","a":"Verse 122 contains the single most defensible clinical recommendation in the *Śiva Svarodaya*, and it is defensible for a reason the text does not state and could not have known. Work the mechanics. To lie down with the right nostril flowing, one must lie on the *left* side, because lateral recumbency produces contralateral nasal decongestion — pressure on the down-side of the body shifts sympathetic drive so that the upper nostril opens. **[E]** The instruction \"sleep with the sun channel flowing\" is therefore, operationally, the instruction \"sleep on your left side.\" Left lateral decubitus is the recommended sleeping position for gastro-oesophageal reflux, and the evidence is good. In the left lateral position the gastro-oesophageal junction sits above the level of the gastric contents, whereas in the right lateral position it lies below, allowing the pool of gastric fluid to bathe the sphincter. Manometric and pH-monitoring studies consistently show fewer and shorter reflux episodes and lower oesophageal acid exposure in the left lateral position than in the right. **[E]** The effect is large enough that positional therapy is a standard non-pharmacological recommendation in reflux management. The text's stated indication — *manda-agni*, weak digestive fire, following a meal — is exactly the clinical context in which this matters. …","m":"The recommendation transfers directly and without modification: **sleep on the left side, particularly after a large or late meal, and particularly if prone to reflux.** Combined with the other well-supported positional measures — elevating the head of the bed by six to eight inches, and leaving three hours between the last meal and lying down — this constitutes the non-pharmacological core of reflux management. **[E]** The mechanism to remember is the geometry rather than the nostril. The stomach's greater curvature lies to the left, and the oesophageal junction enters on the right side of the fundus; lying left puts the junction above the fluid line. Nothing about nasal airflow is doing any work here. There is a second, more general lesson, and it is the reason this verse has been given its own treatment. Traditional practical recommendations should be evaluated on their operational content rather than on their stated justification. The justification offered by a tradition is a claim about mechanism, and pre-modern mechanistic claims are almost always wrong. The recommendation itself is a claim about outcome, and outcomes were what the tradition was actually in a position to observe. Discarding a practice because its explanation is false discards the observation along with the theory. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"124–125","t":"Definition of the Transitional State","p":"PART FOUR: THE UNSTABLE STATE","d":"क्षणं वामे क्षणं दक्षे यदा वहति मारुतः। सुषुम्ना सा च विज्ञेया सर्वकार्यहरा स्मृता॥१२४॥ तस्यां नाड्यां स्थितो वह्निर्ज्वलते कालरूपकः। विषवत्तं विजानीयात् सर्वकार्यविनाशनम्॥१२५॥","i":"kṣaṇaṃ vāme kṣaṇaṃ dakṣe yadā vahati mārutaḥ | suṣumnā sā ca vijñeyā sarvakāryaharā smṛtā ||124|| tasyāṃ nāḍyāṃ sthito vahnir jvalate kālarūpakaḥ | viṣavat taṃ vijānīyāt sarvakāryavināśanam ||125||","f":"When the breath flows momentarily in the left and momentarily in the right, that condition is to be recognized as Suṣumnā, and it is held to carry away all undertakings. A fire in the form of Time burns in that channel; know it to be like poison, destructive of every enterprise.","a":"The definition in verse 124 is precise and it is physiologically correct. Suṣumnā is not a third channel with its own flow; it is the condition of rapid alternation, *kṣaṇaṃ vāme kṣaṇaṃ dakṣe* — a moment on the left, a moment on the right. This is the changeover period of the nasal cycle, and it is a real, observable, transient state in which lateral asymmetry passes through a minimum and the dominant side is unstable. **[E]** The claim attached to it is that instrumental action fails during this window. Stated as a general prediction about the world this is false. Stated as a claim about the actor's own performance it is a specific hypothesis about state-dependent capability, and it has a plausible basis that has not been directly tested. The basis is this. Transitions between autonomic states are periods of regulatory instability by definition — the system is not at a set point, and the variables that would be held steady in either stable state are in motion. There is a broader principle here that is well established in dynamical systems and increasingly documented in physiology: systems approaching a transition between states show characteristic destabilization, including increased variance and slowed recovery from perturbation, phenomena studied under the heading of critical slowing down and used as early-warning indicators in domains from ecology to psychiatry. …","m":"The practical protocol is to recognize the state and to stop trying to execute in it. Recognition markers, in order of reliability: an inability to settle on a task after two or three attempts; a specific quality of restlessness that is neither fatigue nor agitation; and — as the text's own marker, offered here as a hypothesis rather than a finding — approximately equal patency in both nostrils. The response supported by the vigilance and attention literature is not to intensify effort. Sustained attention declines over time on task, and the decline is not reversed by trying harder; brief breaks, particularly those involving a change in activity and physical movement, restore performance more effectively than continued effort. **[E]** The specific finding that even very brief diversions restore performance on prolonged vigilance tasks has been replicated, and the practical implication is that the correct response to an inability to engage is a deliberate, bounded interruption rather than persistence. The text's own recommendation for this state is taken up in verses 129 and 130 and is more interesting than a break. ---","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"126–128","t":"Irregularity as a Warning Sign","p":"PART FOUR: THE UNSTABLE STATE","d":"यदानुक्रममुल्लङ्घ्य यस्य नाडीद्वयं वहेत्। तदा तस्य विजानीयादशुभं नात्र संशयः॥१२६॥ क्षणं वामे क्षणं दक्षे विषमं भावमादिशेत्। विपरीतं फलं ज्ञेयं ज्ञातव्यं च वरानने॥१२७॥ उभयोरेव सञ्चारे विषवत्तं विदुर्बुधैः। न कुर्यात्क्रूरसौम्यानि तत्सर्वं विफलं भवेत्॥१२८॥","i":"yadānukramam ullaṅghya yasya nāḍīdvayaṃ vahet | tadā tasya vijānīyād aśubhaṃ nātra saṃśayaḥ ||126|| kṣaṇaṃ vāme kṣaṇaṃ dakṣe viṣamaṃ bhāvam ādiśet | viparītaṃ phalaṃ jñeyaṃ jñātavyaṃ ca varānane ||127|| ubhayor eva sañcāre viṣavat taṃ vidur budhaiḥ | na kuryāt krūrasaumyāni tat sarvaṃ viphalaṃ bhavet ||128||","f":"When both channels flow in violation of the regular sequence, that is to be recognized as an unfavourable sign. Momentary alternation between left and right indicates an irregular condition and a contrary outcome. When flow occurs in both, the wise regard it as like poison; neither harsh nor gentle undertakings should be attempted, for all of them come to nothing.","a":"These three verses distinguish two conditions that the text's vocabulary does not clearly separate but which are physiologically distinct, and the distinction matters. The first is *rapid alternation* — verse 127's *kṣaṇaṃ vāme kṣaṇaṃ dakṣe*, restating the definition of Suṣumnā. The second is *simultaneous bilateral flow* — verse 128's *ubhayor eva sañcāre*, both sides open together. These are not the same. Bilateral patency is not a transitional state; it is the state produced by generalized sympathetic vasoconstriction of both turbinates simultaneously, and it occurs during exercise, acute stress, fear, and in response to sympathomimetic drugs. **[E]** It also occurs in the absence of a normal nasal cycle, which is a genuine clinical phenomenon. Verse 126 is the interesting one, because it makes a claim not about a state but about a *pattern*: flow that transgresses the regular sequence, *anukramam ullaṅghya*. The text is asserting that irregularity of the cycle itself — as distinct from any particular phase of it — is an unfavourable sign. This claim has more support than most of what surrounds it. A regular nasal cycle is a marker of intact autonomic function, since the cycle is generated by central autonomic drive. …","m":"Treat cycle irregularity as a health signal, at the level of confidence appropriate to a crude, non-specific, unvalidated marker — which is to say, as a prompt to check other things rather than as a finding. The practical form: a person who has tracked nasal dominance for some weeks and has a sense of their own typical pattern will notice a departure from it. Sustained bilateral patency, sustained unilateral blockage that does not shift, or unusually rapid oscillation are all departures worth noticing. Each has mundane explanations first — allergy, upper respiratory infection, a septal deviation becoming symptomatic, recent exertion, a decongestant. **[E]** The signal's value is as a prompt for attention, not as a diagnosis, and anyone with persistent unilateral nasal obstruction should see a clinician rather than a text, since persistent one-sided blockage has a differential that includes conditions requiring treatment. The transferable principle is the one about rhythmicity. Regularity in autonomically-generated rhythms is a general marker of physiological integrity, and departures from an individual's own established baseline carry more information than any single measurement compared against a population norm. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"129–130","t":"What the Unstable State Is For","p":"PART FOUR: THE UNSTABLE STATE","d":"जीविते मरणे प्रश्ने लाभालाभे जयाजये। विषमे विपरीते च संस्मरेज्जगदीश्वरम्॥१२९॥ ईश्वरे चिन्तिते कार्यं योगाभ्यासादिकर्म च। अन्यत्तत्र न कर्तव्यं जयलाभसुखैषिभिः॥१३०॥","i":"jīvite maraṇe praśne lābhālābhe jayājaye | viṣame viparīte ca saṃsmarej jagadīśvaram ||129|| īśvare cintite kāryaṃ yogābhyāsādikarma ca | anyat tatra na kartavyaṃ jayalābhasukhaiṣibhiḥ ||130||","f":"In questions of life and death, of gain and loss, of victory and defeat, when the condition is irregular or contrary, one should turn the mind to what is ultimate. With the mind so turned, only practices such as yoga should be undertaken; those who seek success, gain and wellbeing should do nothing else at such a time.","a":"This pair closes the section and constitutes the text's positive recommendation for the transitional state. Having established that instrumental action fails in Suṣumnā, the *Svarodaya* specifies what does not fail: contemplative practice, and the turning of attention away from outcome. The recommendation deserves to be taken seriously on grounds independent of its theology, because it identifies a genuine and non-obvious complementarity. The states in which goal-directed execution is difficult are not states of reduced capacity in general. They are states of *differently distributed* capacity, and the reallocation is characterizable. The relevant finding concerns the default mode network — the medial prefrontal cortex, posterior cingulate, precuneus and angular gyrus — whose activity increases during rest and decreases during externally-directed tasks. Default network activity is associated with self-referential processing, autobiographical memory, mental simulation of the future, and the consideration of other minds. **[E]** These are precisely the operations verse 129 lists: life and death, gain and loss, the long view. A state characterized by reduced task-directed engagement and increased internally-directed processing is not a defective state for instrumental action so much as a state configured for something else. There is a further finding worth attaching. …","m":"The protocol, with the metaphysics made optional: **Recognize the state.** Inability to engage after several attempts; restlessness distinct from fatigue; equalized nasal patency if one is tracking it. **Close the instrumental task cleanly.** Write down the next concrete step, so the task is not held open in working memory. Recording an intention reduces the intrusive persistence of an unfinished task, and the effect is on the persistence, not on the intention itself — writing it down is what stops it recurring. **[E]** **Take up a defined alternative for a bounded period.** Walking, particularly outdoors, is the best-supported option: it improves subsequent divergent-thinking performance in controlled studies, and it satisfies the conditions for productive incubation. **[E for the walking effect on divergent thinking; S for the incubation mechanism]** Structured breath observation, as described under verse 50, is the text's own choice and has its own support. Consumption of short-form media is the common modern default and is the worst available option, since it maintains external attentional load without task-directed benefit and does not permit incubation. **Do not check whether the instrumental task has become possible again.** The oscillation is the cost. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"131","t":"The Ambivalent Junction","p":"PART FIVE: THE JUNCTION","d":"सूर्येण वहमानायां सुषुम्नायां मुहुर्मुहुः। शापं दद्याद्वरं दद्यात्सर्वथैव तदन्यथा॥१३१॥","i":"sūryeṇa vahamānāyāṃ suṣumnāyāṃ muhurmuhuḥ | śāpaṃ dadyād varaṃ dadyāt sarvathaiva tad anyathā ||131||","f":"When Suṣumnā runs repeatedly alongside the right channel, a curse pronounced or a boon granted will in every case turn out contrary to intention.","a":"The verse describes a compound condition: the transitional state occurring repeatedly in conjunction with right-channel dominance. The consequence assigned to it is inversion — whatever is intended produces its opposite. As divination this is unremarkable. As a description of a physiological condition it is more interesting than it looks, because the compound it names is real and clinically recognizable. Repeated rapid changeover in the presence of elevated sympathetic tone is what an unstable autonomic state looks like. The nasal cycle under stress does not simply lock into one side; both sides decongest under generalized sympathetic drive, and the ordinary reciprocal alternation is disrupted rather than accelerated. **[E]** The text's *muhurmuhuḥ* — again and again — captures the phenomenology of a cycle that has lost its regularity without settling. What is worth extracting is the specific behavioural claim: that speech acts performed in this state produce outcomes opposite to those intended. *Śāpa* and *vara* — curse and boon — are the two paradigmatic performative utterances in this literature, statements that are supposed to change the world by being said. The claim is that in an unstable state, one's consequential utterances misfire. That claim is defensible once relocated from cosmology to communication. …","m":"The rule: **do not deliver consequential speech acts while dysregulated.** Consequential speech acts are those whose effect depends on how they land rather than on their propositional content: praise, criticism, apology, refusal, commitment, the setting of a boundary. Their success is carried substantially by prosody and timing, both of which are degraded by arousal in ways the speaker cannot monitor from the inside. The practical implementation is a delay rule with a physiological gate rather than a temporal one. Before delivering feedback, an apology, a refusal or a commitment, run the state check from verse 97 — nasal patency, pulse, location of muscular tension, breath rate. If the state is elevated or unstable, defer. If deferral is impossible, spend two minutes on extended-exhalation breathing first, which measurably lowers heart rate and raises heart-rate variability within that window. **[E]** Written communication deserves separate mention because it removes the prosodic channel entirely while preserving the arousal effects on lexical choice, message length and sentence construction. The reader supplies prosody from context, and under ambiguity supplies the least generous reading available. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"132–133","t":"The Prohibition at the Junction","p":"PART FIVE: THE JUNCTION","d":"नाडीसङ्क्रमणे काले तत्त्वसङ्गमनेऽपि च। शुभं किञ्चन्न कर्तव्यं पुण्यदानानि किञ्चन॥१३२॥ विषमस्योदयो यत्र मनसापि च चिन्तयेत्। यात्रा हानिकरा तस्य मृत्युः क्लेशो न संशयः॥१३३॥","i":"nāḍīsaṅkramaṇe kāle tattvasaṅgamane 'pi ca | śubhaṃ kiñcan na kartavyaṃ puṇyadānāni kiñcana ||132|| viṣamasyodayo yatra manasāpi ca cintayet | yātrā hānikarā tasya mṛtyuḥ kleśo na saṃśayaḥ ||133||","f":"At the time when a channel is in transit, and likewise when the elemental phases are changing over, nothing of consequence should be undertaken, nor any act of giving. Where the irregular condition arises, a journey undertaken — even one merely contemplated — brings loss, affliction and worse.","a":"Verse 132 extends the prohibition from the channel changeover to the elemental changeover, generating a system in which transitions occur at two nested timescales and both are excluded from action. Verse 133 escalates the consequence to death. Two things are worth separating here: the scheduling structure and the escalation. The scheduling structure is the more defensible. What the text has constructed is a day partitioned into performance windows separated by explicit non-performance windows at every level of the rhythmic hierarchy — a schedule in which transitions are protected rather than worked through. This is not how most contemporary work is organized. The default assumption of modern work design is that time is homogeneous and that any hour is interchangeable with any other, with transitions treated as dead time to be minimized. The evidence does not support the assumption. Task-switching carries measurable costs in reaction time and error rate; the residue of an abandoned task persists into the next one and degrades it; and the cost is larger when the switch is abrupt. **[E]** A schedule that builds explicit buffers around transitions is better matched to what is known about attentional dynamics than one that does not, whatever the theory used to justify it. …","m":"Keep the buffer, discard the dread. **The buffer.** Build explicit transition periods into the working day at boundaries between different kinds of work — five to ten minutes, no instrumental task, ideally involving movement and a change of physical location. The function is to allow the residue of the previous task to decay before the next begins. Brief breaks restore performance on sustained tasks more effectively than continued effort, and physical movement between work blocks has additional benefits for subsequent cognitive performance. **[E]** The corollary is that transitions should not be filled with a *different* demanding task, which is what checking messages between meetings amounts to. The buffer only works if it is empty. **The dread.** A self-observation practice must be non-evaluative to be accurate, and this is a technical requirement rather than a matter of temperament. If a particular observed state carries a penalty — moral, catastrophic, or merely self-critical — the observation will be distorted toward the acceptable finding. This holds for nasal dominance, for mood tracking, for food logging, and for every other form of self-measurement. The practical rule: record what is observed before assigning it any meaning, and keep the two operations separate in time. Observation first, interpretation later, and never in the same pass. …","g":["E"]},{"v":"134–135","t":"The Omen Verses","p":"PART FIVE: THE JUNCTION","d":"पुरो वामोर्ध्वतश्चन्द्रो दक्षाधः पृष्ठतो रविः। पूर्णा रिक्ताविवेकोऽयं ज्ञातव्यो देशिकैः सदा॥१३४॥ ऊर्ध्ववामाग्रतो दूतो ज्ञेयो वामपथि स्थितः। पृष्ठे दक्षे तथाधस्तात्सूर्यवाहागतः शुभः॥१३५॥","i":"puro vāmordhvataś candro dakṣādhaḥ pṛṣṭhato raviḥ | pūrṇā riktāviveko 'yaṃ jñātavyo deśikaiḥ sadā ||134|| ūrdhvavāmāgrato dūto jñeyo vāmapathi sthitaḥ | pṛṣṭhe dakṣe tathādhastāt sūryavāhāgataḥ śubhaḥ ||135||","f":"Front, left and above belong to the moon; right, below and behind to the sun. The discrimination of full from empty is always to be known by teachers. A messenger approaching from above, from the left, or from in front is to be reckoned as belonging to the left channel; one arriving from behind, from the right, or from below to the right channel, which is favourable.","a":"These verses convert nasal dominance into a divinatory apparatus for reading the significance of a messenger's arrival, and their predictive content is worthless. What is analytically interesting is the *spatial* scheme they impose, because it is not arbitrary and it is not unique to this text. The mapping is consistent: left, front, above are grouped and assigned to the lunar pole; right, behind, below to the solar. This is a synthesis of three independent binary oppositions — lateral, sagittal and vertical — into a single valenced axis. Cross-culturally, such syntheses are common and their directionality is not random. The association of *up* with positive valence and *down* with negative is robust across languages and appears in behavioural measures: people categorize positive words faster when they appear in an upper visual position and negative words faster in a lower one, and evaluative judgements interact with vertical spatial attention. **[E]** The association of *forward* with the future and *behind* with the past is likewise widespread, though not universal, and is grounded in the asymmetry of locomotion and vision. The lateral component is the least stable and the most revealing. …","m":"Nothing in the divinatory content transfers. The transferable material is the observation about spatial-affective mapping, and it has a practical application in the design of information displays and physical environments. Vertical position carries evaluative weight independent of content; items placed higher are attended to earlier and evaluated more positively, an effect exploited routinely in retail shelf placement and interface design. **[E]** A person constructing their own working environment — a task list, a dashboard, a wall of notes — is making valence assignments whether or not they intend to, and can make them deliberately instead. The second application concerns the discrimination skill. *Pūrṇā-riktā-viveka* is worth practising on its own terms, because the capacity to detect small asymmetries in one's own physiological state is the foundation of every technique in this text and generalizes beyond it. The training method is straightforward: attempt the discrimination, then verify it against an external check — exhaling onto a mirror or a cool metal surface and comparing the two condensation patches gives an immediate objective answer. Repeated pairing of a subjective judgement with objective feedback is how perceptual discrimination is trained in every domain where it has been studied. **[E]** The point generalizes. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"136–138","t":"The Redefinition of Sandhyā and Veda","p":"PART FIVE: THE JUNCTION","d":"अनादिर्विषमः सन्धिर्निराहारो निराकुलः। परे सूक्ष्मे विलीयेत सा संध्या सद्भिरुच्यते॥१३६॥ न वेदं वेद इत्याहुर्वेदो वेदो न विद्यते। परमात्मा विद्यते येन स वेदो वेद उच्यते॥१३७॥ न संध्या संधिरित्याहुः संध्या संधिर्निगद्यते। विषमः संधिगः प्राणः स संधिजः संधिरुच्यते॥१३८॥","i":"anādir viṣamaḥ sandhir nirāhāro nirākulaḥ | pare sūkṣme vilīyeta sā saṃdhyā sadbhir ucyate ||136|| na vedaṃ veda ityāhur vedo vedo na vidyate | paramātmā vidyate yena sa vedo veda ucyate ||137|| na saṃdhyā saṃdhir ityāhuḥ saṃdhyā saṃdhir nigadyate | viṣamaḥ saṃdhigaḥ prāṇaḥ sa saṃdhijaḥ saṃdhir ucyate ||138||","f":"The junction that is without beginning, uneven, unfed and undisturbed, in which one dissolves into the subtle — that is what the wise call *sandhyā*. They say the Veda is not the Veda: what bears that name is not it. That by which the ultimate is known — that is what is properly called Veda. Likewise they say *sandhyā* is not the ritual juncture: the *sandhyā* properly so called is the breath moving at the junction, and what is born of that junction is the true junction.","a":"This is the intellectual centre of the treatise and it deserves treatment as argument rather than as instruction. *Sandhyā* in ordinary Brahmanical usage denotes the twilight junctures — dawn, noon, dusk — and the mandatory ritual observances performed at them. *Veda* denotes the received scriptural corpus. Verses 137 and 138 take both terms and detach them from their institutional referents by means of a paradoxical formula: the Veda is not the Veda; *sandhyā* is not the junction. The resolution offered in each case is functional. Whatever produces knowledge of the ultimate *is* Veda, whatever text it happens to be. Whatever junction the breath actually passes through *is sandhyā*, whatever the hour. The rhetorical structure — the negation of a term followed by its reassignment to a functional criterion — is a recognizable move in Indian philosophical literature and it is being deployed here for a specific purpose. The text is relocating religious authority from an external calendar to an internal event. The ritual day is fixed by the sun; the *sandhyā* of this text is fixed by the observer's own physiology and occurs many times daily. The consequence is that the practice becomes self-scheduling and independent of institutional supervision. This is a substantive claim about who controls religious practice, dressed as an etymological argument. …","m":"The practice: attend to the pause at the end of exhalation, before inhalation begins. Do not extend it, do not hold, do not manipulate. Simply place attention at that point and let the breath proceed on its own. Two cautions. First, this is an attentional exercise, not a retention exercise, and the distinction matters. Deliberate breath-holding produces hypercapnia and rising CO₂ drive, which is arousing and can be distressing; the practice here involves no holding at all. Anyone who finds the pause becoming effortful has begun holding and should stop. Second, individuals with panic disorder frequently find attention to respiration itself anxiogenic, since interoceptive attention to breathing can trigger the catastrophic misinterpretation that maintains the disorder. **[E]** For that population, respiratory attention practices should be approached with clinical guidance rather than from a book. The general principle extracted from verses 137 and 138 is worth stating separately, because it is the most portable thing in this section and it is a claim about method. Both verses replace a *nominal* definition with a *functional* one. What counts as Veda is not determined by the label attached to a corpus but by whether it does the thing Veda is supposed to do. What counts as *sandhyā* is determined by whether a junction is actually occurring, not by what the calendar says. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"139–140","t":"The Claim of Sufficiency","p":"PART FIVE: THE JUNCTION","d":"देव देव महादेव सर्वसंसारतारक। स्थितं त्वदीयहृदये रहस्यं वद मे प्रभो॥१३९॥ स्वरज्ञानरहस्यात्तु न काचिच्चेष्टदेवता। स्वरज्ञानरतो योगी स योगी परमो मतः॥१४०॥","i":"deva deva mahādeva sarvasaṃsāratāraka | sthitaṃ tvadīyahṛdaye rahasyaṃ vada me prabho ||139|| svarajñānarahasyāt tu na kācic ceṣṭadevatā | svarajñānarato yogī sa yogī paramo mataḥ ||140||","f":"Tell me the secret that resides in your own heart. There is no object of devotion higher than the knowledge of breath; the practitioner devoted to it is held to be the highest.","a":"Verse 140 makes a claim of sufficiency: that respiratory self-knowledge is not merely one practice among many but supersedes the others. Rhetorically this is standard — technical treatises in every tradition claim primacy for their own subject — and the claim as stated is unevaluable. But it points at something worth examining, which is why respiration should be the privileged object at all. Several candidate answers are available and they are not equivalent. The first is *accessibility*. Respiration is continuously present, requires no equipment, and is available in any posture and any circumstance. Any practice whose object is always available can be practised at a frequency no scheduled practice can match, and frequency is what determines whether a behaviour becomes automatic. **[E]** The second is *dual control*. Respiration is the only major visceral function that is both automatically regulated and fully voluntarily controllable. The brainstem respiratory rhythm generator maintains breathing without attention; corticospinal projections from motor cortex to respiratory motoneurons permit complete voluntary override. **[E]** This makes the breath the natural interface between voluntary and autonomic domains — the one place where a deliberate act reliably produces an autonomic consequence. Heart rate cannot be commanded; it can be shifted through respiration. …","m":"Treat respiratory practice as the *interface*, not the programme. The programme is the ordinary one and the evidence for its components is not seriously contested: adequate sleep, regular physical activity, sustained social contact, and whatever domain-specific practice one's work requires. Respiratory technique does not substitute for any of these and any presentation suggesting it does should be distrusted. What it does is provide acute state control that nothing else in the list provides. Sleep operates on a timescale of days, exercise on weeks, social connection on years. Respiration operates on a timescale of minutes, which makes it the only tool in the set that can be deployed inside a situation rather than around it. The practical consequence is that respiratory technique belongs at the points where acute state is the binding constraint — before a difficult conversation, at the onset of acute stress, before a task requiring precision, at the transition between kinds of work. The claim to hold onto from this section, discarding the superlatives, is a claim about where a small intervention has disproportionate effect. In a system with many variables, the ones worth acting on are those that are both influential and controllable. Respiration is unusual in scoring highly on both. That is the whole of the case, and it is enough. ---","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"141–144","t":"The Elemental Framework","p":"PART SIX: THE FIVE PHASES","d":"पञ्चतत्त्वाद्भवेत्सृष्टिस्तत्त्वे तत्त्वं प्रलीयते। पञ्चतत्त्वं परं तत्त्वं तत्त्वातीतं निरञ्जनम्॥१४१॥ तत्त्वानां नाम विज्ञेयं सिद्धयोगेन योगिभिः। भूतानां दुष्टचिह्नानि जानातीह स्वरोत्तमः॥१४२॥ पृथिव्यापस्तथा तेजो वायुराकाशमेव च। पञ्चभूतात्मकं विश्वं यो जानाति स पूजितः॥१४३॥ सर्वलोकस्थजीवानां न देहो भिन्नतत्त्वकः। भूलोकसत्यपर्यन्तं नाडीभेदः पृथक् पृथक्॥१४४॥","i":"pañcatattvād bhavet sṛṣṭis tattve tattvaṃ pralīyate | pañcatattvaṃ paraṃ tattvaṃ tattvātītaṃ nirañjanam ||141|| tattvānāṃ nāma vijñeyaṃ siddhayogena yogibhiḥ | bhūtānāṃ duṣṭacihnāni jānātīha svarottamaḥ ||142|| pṛthivyāpas tathā tejo vāyur ākāśam eva ca | pañcabhūtātmakaṃ viśvaṃ yo jānāti sa pūjitaḥ ||143|| sarvalokasthajīvānāṃ na deho bhinnatattvakaḥ | bhūlokasatyaparyantaṃ nāḍībhedaḥ pṛthak pṛthak ||144||","f":"Creation arises from the five elements and dissolves back into them; the five constitute the supreme principle, and beyond them is what is unconditioned. Their names are known to practitioners through accomplished practice, and one expert in breath discerns their adverse indications. Whoever understands that the world consists of these five is honoured. The bodies of living beings throughout all worlds are not composed of different elements, though the differentiation of their channels varies from one to another.","a":"The *pañcabhūta* scheme is not a discovery of this text; it is the standard classificatory system of Indian natural philosophy, shared with Sāṃkhya, Vaiśeṣika and Āyurveda, and the *Svarodaya* inherits rather than argues for it. As physics it is false. As a classification scheme it is worth understanding, because everything the text does in the next hundred verses is built on it. The five categories are not substances in the modern sense but *states* organized by a coherent principle. Earth is that which has extension and resistance; water that which flows and coheres; fire that which transforms and radiates; air that which moves without form; space that which contains. Read as a taxonomy of *material behaviour* rather than of material composition, the scheme is a reasonable pre-analytic partition — roughly, solid, liquid, energetic, gaseous, and the void that permits arrangement. Every early natural philosophy that lacked chemistry arrived at something similar, and the convergence is not coincidental: these are the distinctions available to unaided observation. **[X]** What matters for the present enquiry is how the scheme functions when applied to respiration, and here verse 142 is the key. It states that the practitioner knows the *duṣṭa-cihnāni*, the adverse signs, of the elements. …","m":"The application is a caution about classification, and it applies to every framework a reader might adopt for understanding their own states — the five elements, the *doṣas*, chronotypes, personality types, or the informal categories anyone constructs for their own moods. A classification is a tool for prediction. Its value is entirely determined by whether the categories predict anything: whether knowing which category one is in tells you what will happen next, or what intervention will work. A category system that generates no predictions is not knowledge but vocabulary, and vocabulary has a specific hazard, which is that fluency in it produces the sensation of understanding without the substance. **[E, from the literature on the illusion of explanatory depth]** The test to apply to any such system, including this one: **state a prediction the category makes, then check it.** If the earth phase is a real state, then being in it should predict something — performance on a specific task, response to a specific intervention, duration before change. If no such prediction can be stated, the category is a label. The second application is verse 144's point about individual variation, and it is the practical form of everything this book has said about self-experimentation. Population norms are the right starting point and the wrong endpoint. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"145–148","t":"The Eightfold Analytical Scheme","p":"PART SIX: THE FIVE PHASES","d":"वामे वा दक्षिणे वापि उदयाः पञ्च कीर्तिताः। अष्टधा तत्त्वविज्ञानं शृणु वक्ष्यामि सुन्दरि॥१४५॥ प्रथमे तत्त्वसङ्ख्यानं द्वितीये श्वाससन्धयः। तृतीये स्वरचिह्नानि चतुर्थे स्थानमेव च॥१४६॥ पञ्चमे तस्य वर्णाश्च षष्ठे तु प्राण एव च। सप्तमे स्वादसंयुक्ता अष्टमे गतिलक्षणम्॥१४७॥ एवमष्टविधं प्राणं विषुवन्तं चराचरम्। स्वरात्परतरं देवि नान्यथा त्वम्बुजेक्षणे॥१४८॥","i":"vāme vā dakṣiṇe vāpi udayāḥ pañca kīrtitāḥ | aṣṭadhā tattvavijñānaṃ śṛṇu vakṣyāmi sundari ||145|| prathame tattvasaṅkhyānaṃ dvitīye śvāsasandhayaḥ | tṛtīye svaracihnāni caturthe sthānam eva ca ||146|| pañcame tasya varṇāś ca ṣaṣṭhe tu prāṇa eva ca | saptame svādasaṃyuktā aṣṭame gatilakṣaṇam ||147|| evam aṣṭavidhaṃ prāṇaṃ viṣuvantaṃ carācaram | svarāt parataraṃ devi nānyathā tvam bujekṣaṇe ||148||","f":"Five phases are declared to arise, whether in the left or the right. Hear the eightfold analysis of them: first their enumeration, second the junctures of the breath, third the signs of the breath, fourth their location, fifth their colours, sixth the vital force, seventh their associated tastes, and eighth the characteristics of their movement. The breath in these eight aspects pervades all that moves and does not move; there is nothing beyond it.","a":"These verses set out a formal analytical scheme, and their structure is worth examining separately from their content. What is specified is a fixed set of eight dimensions along which each of five categories will be characterized. The dimensions are heterogeneous by design: they include temporal properties (the junctures of the breath), spatial properties (location in the body, direction of movement), sensory properties (colour, taste), and physical properties (the characteristics of movement). The commitment is that every category will receive a value on every dimension. This is a factorial description matrix, and it is a genuinely sophisticated methodological instrument for a text of this period. Its virtues are the virtues of any such scheme. It enforces completeness, so that no category is characterized on only the dimensions that happen to be convenient. It enables comparison, since categories can be placed side by side dimension against dimension. And it is *falsifiable in a structured way*, since a category that cannot be assigned a value on a required dimension is a problem the scheme itself surfaces. **[X]** The comparison worth drawing is to how modern psychology characterizes states. …","m":"The transferable instrument is the description matrix, and it is worth constructing for any state one is trying to track. The procedure: choose a small number of dimensions — four to six is workable — on which every state will be rated, and rate every observation on all of them. The dimensions should be chosen to be as independent of one another as possible, and at least half of them should be externally verifiable. For respiratory and autonomic state a defensible set would be: breath rate (countable), nasal dominance (checkable against a mirror), location of respiratory movement (observable), subjective arousal (self-report), and subjective valence (self-report). The critical discipline, and the one the *Svarodaya* lacks, is to record the verifiable and unverifiable dimensions separately and to analyse them separately. If the self-report dimensions turn out to correlate with the measured ones, that is a finding. If they do not, that is a more important finding, and one that a merged instrument would conceal. The second application is to the design of any personal tracking system. Most self-tracking fails not because the wrong variables were chosen but because the variables were chosen inconsistently — recorded when convenient, on dimensions that shifted over time, with the criteria for each rating never fixed. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"149","t":"The Daily Observation","p":"PART SIX: THE FIVE PHASES","d":"निरीक्षितव्यं यत्नेन सदा प्रत्यूषकालतः। कालस्य वञ्चनार्थाय कर्म कुर्वन्ति योगिनः॥१४९॥","i":"nirīkṣitavyaṃ yatnena sadā pratyūṣakālataḥ | kālasya vañcanārthāya karma kurvanti yoginaḥ ||149||","f":"It should be examined carefully every day from daybreak. Practitioners act with the aim of outwitting Time.","a":"The verse prescribes a daily observation, anchored to waking, performed with deliberate effort. Its practical content is a protocol; its interesting content is the stated purpose, *kālasya vañcanārtham* — for the purpose of cheating Time. Take the protocol first. Anchoring the observation to daybreak solves the adherence problem that defeats most self-monitoring: the observation is attached to an unavoidable daily event rather than to a clock time or an intention. **[E]** The specification *yatnena*, with effort, is also correct. Interoceptive discrimination is not automatic and does not improve through casual attention; it requires deliberate, effortful engagement of the kind that characterizes practice rather than habit. There is a physiological reason why waking is the right observation point, and the text could not have known it. The transition from sleep to wake involves a large, stereotyped, and well-characterized autonomic shift: sympathetic activity rises sharply in the hour around waking, and the cortisol awakening response produces a rise of fifty per cent or more in circulating cortisol within thirty to forty-five minutes of waking. …","m":"The protocol, and it is the single most important practical instruction in this book: **On waking, before rising, before any device, take one minute.** Note three things: which nostril is dominant, the approximate rate and depth of the breath, and a single word for the current state. Record them somewhere fixed. Do not interpret. The value accumulates rather than arriving. A single morning observation is nearly uninformative. Thirty of them establish a baseline; sixty reveal structure. What most people discover is that their morning state predicts more about the day than they had assumed, and that the states they had attributed to circumstances were present before the circumstances were. Three design notes matter for whether this survives past the first fortnight. Attach it to waking, not to a time. Keep it under a minute — every additional element reduces the probability that it happens on a bad morning, and the bad mornings are the informative ones. And separate recording from interpretation strictly, as argued under verse 133; the moment an observation acquires a verdict, the observation begins to bend. The reframing that the practice delivers, and which is its actual product, is the one verse 149 names. A state that has been observed and recorded is a state one is no longer simply inside. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"150–152","t":"The Ṣaṇmukhī Test and the Colour Assignments","p":"PART SIX: THE FIVE PHASES","d":"श्रुत्योरङ्गुष्ठकौ मध्याङ्गुल्यौ नासापुटद्वये। वदनप्रान्तके चान्याङ्गुलीर्दद्याच्च नेत्रयोः॥१५०॥ अस्यान्तस्तु पृथिव्यादितत्त्वज्ञा भवेत्क्रमात्। पीतश्वेतारुणश्यामैर्बिन्दुभिर्निरुपाधिकम्॥१५१॥ आपः श्वेतं क्षितिः पीता रक्तवर्णो हुताशनः। मरुतो नीलजीमूत आकाशं सर्ववर्णकम्॥१५२॥","i":"śrutyor aṅguṣṭhakau madhyāṅgulyau nāsāpuṭadvaye | vadanaprāntake cānyāṅgulīr dadyāc ca netrayoḥ ||150|| asyāntas tu pṛthivyāditattvajñā bhavet kramāt | pītaśvetāruṇaśyāmair bindubhir nirupādhikam ||151|| āpaḥ śvetaṃ kṣitiḥ pītā raktavarṇo hutāśanaḥ | maruto nīlajīmūta ākāśaṃ sarvavarṇakam ||152||","f":"Place the thumbs on the ears, the middle fingers on the nostrils, the remaining fingers at the corners of the mouth and over the eyes. Within this closure, knowledge of the elemental phases arises in sequence, appearing as points of yellow, white, red and dark without any external cause. Water appears white, earth yellow, fire red, air the colour of a storm cloud, and space of all colours mixed.","a":"This is a specific, replicable technique with a specific, replicable outcome, and it is the most directly testable claim in the *Śiva Svarodaya*. It is also, in this author's assessment, the clearest case in the text of a real perceptual phenomenon being correctly reported and incorrectly explained. The procedure — *ṣaṇmukhī mudrā*, the closing of the six gates — occludes ears, eyes, nostrils and mouth with the fingers of both hands, producing near-total elimination of exteroceptive input across the cranial apertures. The reported outcome is the appearance of coloured points and patterns in the visual field, arising *nirupādhikam*, without external cause. The phenomenon is real and is well documented outside any contemplative context. Reduction of patterned visual input reliably produces visual phenomena in healthy observers. In the classic Ganzfeld condition — homogeneous, unstructured visual stimulation — observers report the emergence of colours, forms and eventually complex imagery within minutes. **[E]** Prolonged darkness or blindfolding produces similar effects, and the phenomenon appears in a stronger clinical form in Charles Bonnet syndrome, where progressive loss of visual input from eye disease produces vivid, formed hallucinations in people with intact insight and no psychiatric disorder. **[E]** The mechanism is understood in outline and it is instructive. …","m":"The technique is worth performing, with the traditional interpretation set aside and one safety point observed. **The safety point first.** Pressure on the eyeballs is not benign. Sustained or firm pressure raises intraocular pressure and can compress retinal circulation; there is also the oculocardiac reflex, a trigeminal-vagal pathway by which pressure on the globe produces bradycardia, occasionally marked. **[E]** Anyone with glaucoma, retinal disease, recent eye surgery, or high myopia should not perform this at all. Anyone else should rest the fingers on the closed lids with no perceptible pressure, and stop immediately if there is pain, visual disturbance beyond the expected phenomena, or light-headedness. **The practice.** Seated, close the eyes. Rest the thumbs lightly on the tragus of each ear to close the ear canals, the index and middle fingers resting on the closed lids without pressure, the remaining fingers alongside the nose and at the corners of the mouth. Breathe normally through the nose — the traditional instruction includes retention, which is unnecessary and adds risk. Remain for three to five minutes. Observe what appears without attempting to classify it. **What it demonstrates.** The value is a direct, first-person demonstration that perceptual experience is constructed rather than received. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"153–155","t":"The Mirror Test and the Direction of Flow","p":"PART SIX: THE FIVE PHASES","d":"दर्पणेन समालोक्य तत्र श्वासं विनिक्षिपेत्। आकारैस्तु विजानीयात्तत्त्वभेदं विचक्षणः॥१५३॥ चतुरस्रं चार्धचन्द्रं त्रिकोणं वर्तुलं स्मृतम्। बिन्दुभिस्तु नभो ज्ञेयमाकारैस्तत्त्वलक्षणम्॥१५४॥ मध्ये पृथ्वी ह्यधश्चापश्चोर्ध्वं वहति चानलः। तिर्यग्वायुप्रवाहश्च नभो वहति सङ्क्रमे॥१५५॥","i":"darpaṇena samālokya tatra śvāsaṃ vinikṣipet | ākārais tu vijānīyāt tattvabhedaṃ vicakṣaṇaḥ ||153|| caturasraṃ cārdhacandraṃ trikoṇaṃ vartulaṃ smṛtam | bindubhis tu nabho jñeyam ākārais tattvalakṣaṇam ||154|| madhye pṛthvī hy adhaś cāpaś cordhvaṃ vahati cānalaḥ | tiryag vāyupravāhaś ca nabho vahati saṅkrame ||155||","f":"Breathe onto a mirror and examine the shape formed. The discerning identify the elemental phase by its outline: square, crescent, triangle, circle, or scattered points for space. Earth flows through the centre of the nostril, water downward, fire upward, air obliquely, and space at the moment of changeover.","a":"Verse 153 introduces the only external instrument mentioned in the entire treatise, and its introduction is a significant methodological moment. Everything preceding has relied on interoception. Here the text calls for a mirror, an exhalation onto it, and an inference from a physical trace. The underlying physics is straightforward and the measurement is real. Exhaled air is saturated with water vapour at approximately body temperature; on contact with a cooler surface it condenses, and the resulting patch of condensation has a spatial distribution determined by the geometry of the exhaled jet — its direction, its divergence, its velocity, and the position of the nostrils relative to the surface. **[E]** A mirror held at a fixed distance and breathed upon will produce a reproducible pattern for a given breathing configuration, and different configurations will produce different patterns. What is being measured, in modern terms, is the *plume geometry of the nasal exhalation jet*. This is not an exotic quantity. Nasal exhalation produces a directed jet whose orientation is determined by the anatomy of the nasal valve and the inferior turbinate, and whose velocity profile is determined by flow rate and by the cross-sectional area of the airway. Both the anatomy and the flow rate change with congestion state. …","m":"The mirror test is worth adopting, for what it can actually measure. **As a dominance check.** Exhale through the nose onto a cool mirror or a smooth metal surface held a few inches below the nostrils. Two patches form. The larger patch indicates the more patent nostril. This is an objective, immediate, external verification of nasal dominance, and it is the calibration tool that the interoceptive discrimination practice recommended throughout this book requires. Judge first, then check. Repeated pairing of judgement with feedback is how perceptual discrimination is trained. **[E]** **As a flow-rate indicator.** The rate at which the patch forms and the sharpness of its edges vary with exhalation velocity, and both are crudely observable. This gives a rough index of expiratory flow with no equipment. It is not a substitute for a peak flow meter, and anyone with asthma or another respiratory condition should use an actual meter, which is inexpensive and validated. **Not as a phase diagnostic.** The shape assignments should be set aside. If a reader wants to test them, the protocol is the one given under verse 151: produce the condensation pattern, photograph it, and classify it later without knowledge of the breathing state at the time. Classification made in ignorance of the expected answer is the only classification that carries information. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"156–158","t":"Location, Taste, and the Measured Extent of the Breath","p":"PART SIX: THE FIVE PHASES","d":"स्कन्धद्वये स्थितो वह्निर्नाभिमूले प्रभञ्जनः। जानुदेशे क्षितिस्तोयं पादान्ते मस्तके नभः॥१५६॥ माहेयं मधुरं स्वादे कषायं जलमेव च। तीक्ष्णं तेजः समीरोऽम्ल आकाशं कटुकं तथा॥१५७॥ अष्टाङ्गुलं वहेद्वायुरनलश्चतुरङ्गुलम्। द्वादशाङ्गुलं माहेयं वारुणं षोडशाङ्गुलम्॥१५८॥","i":"skandhadvaye sthito vahnir nābhimūle prabhañjanaḥ | jānudeśe kṣitis toyaṃ pādānte mastake nabhaḥ ||156|| māheyaṃ madhuraṃ svāde kaṣāyaṃ jalam eva ca | tīkṣṇaṃ tejaḥ samīro 'mla ākāśaṃ kaṭukaṃ tathā ||157|| aṣṭāṅgulaṃ vahed vāyur analaś caturaṅgulam | dvādaśāṅgulaṃ māheyaṃ vāruṇaṃ ṣoḍaśāṅgulam ||158||","f":"Fire is located at the shoulders, air at the base of the navel, earth at the knees, water at the feet, and space in the head. In taste, earth is sweet, water astringent, fire pungent, air sour and space bitter. In extent, the air phase carries eight finger-breadths, fire four, earth twelve and water sixteen.","a":"These three verses complete the description matrix, and they differ sharply in evidential character. Verse 158 is the most valuable thing in this section and deserves the bulk of the analysis. **Verse 156** assigns each phase a bodily location. There is no defensible physiological reading; the locations do not correspond to autonomic territories, to referred sensation patterns, or to anything measurable. The assignment appears to follow a simple vertical logic — heaviest element lowest, subtlest highest — with fire at the shoulders as an anomaly probably driven by the association of heat with the upper body. It is schematic ordering, not observation. **[X, and unsupported]** Its residual use is as an attentional map, as argued for verses 38 and 39: a fixed sequence of bodily locations gives structure to a body scan, and the structure is what does the work. **Verse 157** assigns tastes. This is more interesting than it first appears, because the tastes named are the standard *rasa* categories of Āyurvedic pharmacology, and their appearance here indicates that the *Svarodaya* is drawing on the medical rather than the ritual side of its inheritance. …","m":"**Measure your exhalation reach.** It takes ten seconds and it is the text's own instruction. Hold the back of the hand in front of the nostrils and move it away until the exhalation is no longer felt. Note the distance in finger-breadths. Repeated across a day, this yields a crude time series of expiratory flow — a real physiological variable, obtained with no equipment, using a protocol six centuries old. It will vary with congestion, with exertion, with posture, and with time of day. Whether it varies with anything psychologically interesting is a question the reader can settle for themselves in a fortnight, which is the whole point. **Use taste as a supplementary index.** Resting oral sensation and mouth dryness track autonomic state well enough to be worth noting alongside the other markers. Dry mouth in particular is a reliable and immediately available marker of sympathetic activation, and noticing it is often the earliest available signal that a state shift has occurred — frequently earlier than the subjective sense of stress. **[E]** **Use the body locations as a scan sequence, not a diagnosis.** Feet, knees, navel, shoulders, head is a five-point sequence covering the vertical extent of the body. Ninety seconds. Its value is the structuring of attention, as set out under verse 39. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"159","t":"Direction and Consequence","p":"PART SEVEN: THE APPLIED PHASE SYSTEM","d":"ऊर्ध्वं मृत्युरधः शान्तिस्तिर्यगुच्चाटनं तथा। मध्ये स्तम्भं विजानीयान्नभः सर्वत्र मध्यमम्॥१५९॥","i":"ūrdhvaṃ mṛtyur adhaḥ śāntis tiryag uccāṭanaṃ tathā | madhye stambhaṃ vijānīyān nabhaḥ sarvatra madhyamam ||159||","f":"Upward flow indicates destruction, downward flow quiescence, oblique flow displacement, and central flow arrest. Space is neutral in all cases.","a":"The verse maps four directions of exhalation onto four of the classical *ṣaṭkarma* outcomes. As a predictive scheme it has no content. What is worth extracting is the fourth term, *stambha*, and the fact that the text needs a category for it. *Stambha* means immobilization, arrest, paralysis — a state in which motion is prevented rather than redirected. It is a distinct category from *śānti*, quiescence, and the text keeps them separate. That separation is not obvious and it is correct. The modern account of defensive responding is not the two-term flight-or-fight schema but a graded sequence whose stages differ in their autonomic signatures. The defence cascade model describes a progression from orienting through freezing to active defence and, under conditions of inescapable threat, to tonic immobility. **[E]** Freezing — attentive immobility — is characterized by motor arrest with heightened vigilance and, distinctively, by bradycardia rather than tachycardia, produced by parasympathetic drive occurring simultaneously with sympathetic activation. **[E]** This is a co-activated state, not a low-arousal one, and it is physiologically and behaviourally distinct from calm. The distinction matters practically and it is one that ordinary language handles badly. …","m":"**Distinguish stillness from arrest before choosing an intervention.** The discriminating features are available without instruments. Quiescence carries a sense of ease, an unforced breath, and available attention. Arrest carries muscular bracing, breath held or shallow and high in the chest, narrowed attention, and a specific difficulty in initiating movement — the sense that action requires an effort disproportionate to the task. The heart rate in arrest may be slow, which is precisely what misleads. **For quiescence:** nothing is required. This is a functional state. **For arrest:** mobilize rather than relax. Stand, move, change the physical location, turn the head deliberately to survey the surroundings, engage in a task with clear motor output. Orienting movements — looking around slowly and deliberately, letting the eyes settle on distinct objects — are the standard first-line approach in trauma-informed practice for immobility states, and the rationale is the reversal of the narrowed, fixed attentional profile that characterizes them. **[S]** The general point applies well beyond this verse: an intervention selected for the wrong state is not merely ineffective but can be counterproductive, and the most common error in self-regulation is applying calming techniques indiscriminately. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"160–163","t":"Task Assignment, Outcome, and Latency","p":"PART SEVEN: THE APPLIED PHASE SYSTEM","d":"पृथिव्यां स्थिरकर्माणि चरकर्माणि वारुणे। तेजसि क्रूरकर्माणि मारणोच्चाटने ऽनिले॥१६०॥ व्योम्नि किञ्चिन्न कर्तव्यमभ्यसेद्योगसेवनम्। शून्यता सर्वकार्येषु नात्र कार्या विचारणा॥१६१॥ पृथ्वीजलाभ्यां सिद्धिः स्यान्मृत्युर्वह्नौ क्षयोऽनिले। नभसो निष्फलं सर्वं ज्ञातव्यं तत्त्ववादिभिः॥१६२॥ चिरलाभः क्षितेर्ज्ञेयस्तत्क्षणे तोयतत्त्वतः। हानिः स्याद्धि वाताभ्यां नभसो निष्फलं भवेत्॥१६३॥","i":"pṛthivyāṃ sthirakarmāṇi carakarmāṇi vāruṇe | tejasi krūrakarmāṇi māraṇoccāṭane 'nile ||160|| vyomni kiñcin na kartavyam abhyased yogasevanam | śūnyatā sarvakāryeṣu nātra kāryā vicāraṇā ||161|| pṛthvījalābhyāṃ siddhiḥ syān mṛtyur vahnau kṣayo 'nile | nabhaso niṣphalaṃ sarvaṃ jñātavyaṃ tattvavādibhiḥ ||162|| ciralābhaḥ kṣiter jñeyas tatkṣaṇe toyatattvataḥ | hāniḥ syād dhi vātābhyāṃ nabhaso niṣphalaṃ bhavet ||163||","f":"Stable undertakings belong to the earth phase, mobile ones to water, harsh ones to fire, and destructive ones to air. In the space phase nothing should be undertaken; only contemplative practice is appropriate, and all enterprises come to nothing. Earth and water bring accomplishment, fire brings ruin, air brings loss, and space brings no result at all. Gain from the earth phase arrives after long delay; from the water phase it arrives immediately; from air comes loss; from space, nothing.","a":"Verse 163 contains something the rest of the ledger does not: a claim about *latency*. Earth yields *cira-lābha*, gain after long delay; water yields *tat-kṣaṇe*, gain in that very moment. The text is asserting that the phases differ not only in whether an outcome is favourable but in *when* the outcome arrives. This is a more sophisticated variable than it appears, and it is the only place in the corpus where the temporal structure of consequences enters the model. Consider what it does for a practitioner. A prediction of the form \"this will succeed\" is unfalsifiable in practice, because no time limit is specified and the predictor can always await confirmation. A prediction of the form \"this will succeed immediately\" or \"this will succeed only after long delay\" is bounded, and boundedness is what makes a prediction testable. Verse 163 therefore improves the epistemic status of the surrounding scheme, whether or not its specific assignments are correct. **[X]** It also has psychological content worth separating from the divination. Expectations about *when* an outcome will arrive determine whether effort is sustained through the interval before it does. This is the core of the persistence problem, and it is well characterized. …","m":"**Forecast latency explicitly, in writing, before beginning.** For any undertaking of consequence, state at the outset when the first evidence of progress should be visible. Not the completion date — the date of the first detectable signal. Write it down. This single act does two things: it protects effort during the interval, because the absence of results before the stated date is not evidence of failure; and it makes the project falsifiable, because the absence of results *after* it is. The systematic bias to correct for is optimism about duration. The planning fallacy — the reliable tendency to underestimate the time a task will take, even by people with extensive experience of similar tasks underrunning — is one of the more robust findings in judgement research, and it persists despite awareness of it. **[E]** The best-supported correction is reference-class forecasting: rather than estimating from the inside by decomposing the specific task, estimate from the outside by asking how long comparable undertakings actually took. **[E]** Classifying a project as *cira-lābha* at the outset is a crude version of the same correction. **Schedule the null state.** Recovery periods should appear in the schedule as named blocks with an assigned function, not as the space between commitments. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"164–168","t":"The Sensory Signatures","p":"PART SEVEN: THE APPLIED PHASE SYSTEM","d":"पीतः शनैर्मध्यवाही हनुर्यावद्गुरुध्वनिः। कवोष्णः पार्थिवो वायुः स्थिरकार्यप्रसाधकः॥१६४॥ अधोवाही गुरुध्वानः शीघ्रगः शीतलः स्थितः। यः षोडशाङ्गुलो वायुः स आपः शुभकर्मकृत्॥१६५॥ आवर्तगश्चात्युष्णश्च शोणाभश्चतुरङ्गुलः। ऊर्ध्ववाही च यः क्रूरकर्मकारी स तैजसः॥१६६॥ उष्णशीतः कृष्णवर्णस्तिर्यगन्यष्टकाङ्गुलः। वायुः पवनसंज्ञस्तु चरकर्मप्रसाधकः॥१६७॥ यः समीरः समरसः सर्वतत्त्वगुणावहः। अम्बरं तं विजानीयाद्योगिनां योगदायकम्॥१६८॥","i":"pītaḥ śanair madhyavāhī hanur yāvad gurudhvaniḥ | kavoṣṇaḥ pārthivo vāyuḥ sthirakāryaprasādhakaḥ ||164|| adhovāhī gurudhvānaḥ śīghragaḥ śītalaḥ sthitaḥ | yaḥ ṣoḍaśāṅgulo vāyuḥ sa āpaḥ śubhakarmakṛt ||165|| āvartagaś cātyuṣṇaś ca śoṇābhaś caturaṅgulaḥ | ūrdhvavāhī ca yaḥ krūrakarmakārī sa taijasaḥ ||166|| uṣṇaśītaḥ kṛṣṇavarṇas tiryagany aṣṭakāṅgulaḥ | vāyuḥ pavanasaṃjñas tu carakarmaprasādhakaḥ ||167|| yaḥ samīraḥ samarasaḥ sarvatattvaguṇāvahaḥ | ambaraṃ taṃ vijānīyād yogināṃ yogadāyakam ||168||","f":"The earth breath is yellow, slow, flowing centrally, reaching as far as the jaw, heavy in sound and slightly warm; it accomplishes stable undertakings. The water breath flows downward, is heavy in sound, swift, cool, and extends sixteen finger-breadths; it performs favourable action. The fire breath moves in eddies, is very hot, red in appearance, extends four finger-breadths, flows upward, and performs harsh action. The air breath is variable in temperature, dark, oblique, and extends eight finger-breadths; it accomplishes mobile undertakings. The breath that is uniform in quality and carries the character of all the phases is to be known as space, and it grants union to practitioners.","a":"This is the description matrix filled in, and it is the most detailed phenomenological account of respiratory variation in pre-modern literature. Its dimensions divide sharply by verifiability, and separating them is the work of the analysis. **Sound.** *Guru-dhvani*, heavy sound, is assigned to both earth and water. Nasal breathing sound is a real, measurable, clinically-used variable. Turbulent airflow through a narrowed nasal passage produces audible noise; the degree of turbulence is a function of flow velocity and airway cross-section. Clinicians distinguish stertor — noise from nasal or nasopharyngeal obstruction — from stridor arising lower in the airway, and the character of the sound carries diagnostic information about the level and degree of narrowing. **[E]** A practitioner attending to the sound of their own nasal breathing is attending to a genuine index of airway patency and flow rate. **Temperature.** The claims here are the most interesting and the most likely to be reporting something real. The nose is a heat exchanger of considerable efficiency: inspired air is warmed toward body temperature and humidified during passage over the turbinates, and heat and moisture are partially recovered from expired air on the way out. …","m":"**Attend to the sound of your own breath as an index of airway state.** Close the mouth, breathe at a normal rate, and listen. Audible turbulence indicates narrowing; silence indicates patency. This is free, immediate, and tracks a real variable. It is also the mechanism by which *ujjāyī* breathing works as an attentional anchor: deliberately narrowing the glottis produces a consistent audible signal, giving attention a continuous target rather than an intermittent one. **Attend to thermal sensation at the nostril.** Inspired air feels cool at the nostril rim and expired air warm; the magnitude of the difference varies with mucosal state, ambient temperature and flow rate. This is a fine-grained interoceptive discrimination with a real physical basis, which makes it a good training target — unlike colour, it can in principle be checked against a thermometer. **A note on what \"cooling\" and \"heating\" breath practices actually do.** Several traditional techniques are described as cooling — *śītalī*, drawing air over the curled tongue, and *śītkārī*, drawing it between the teeth. Both increase evaporative cooling of the oral mucosa and produce a genuine local cooling sensation. Whether they reduce core body temperature is a different question and the evidence is weak; systemic thermoregulation is not readily altered by upper-airway manoeuvres. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"169–173","t":"The Duplicate Recension","p":"PART SEVEN: THE APPLIED PHASE SYSTEM","d":"पीतवर्णं चतुष्कोणं मधुरं मध्यमाश्रितम्। भोगदं पार्थिवं तत्त्वं प्रवाहे द्वादशाङ्गुलम्॥१६९॥ श्वेतमर्धेन्दुसंकाशं स्वादु काषायमार्द्रकम्। लाभकृद्वारुणं तत्त्वं प्रवाहे षोडशाङ्गुलम्॥१७०॥ रक्तं त्रिकोणं तीक्ष्णं च ऊर्ध्वभागप्रवाहकम्। दीप्तं च तैजसं तत्त्वं प्रवाहे चतुरङ्गुलम्॥१७१॥ नीलं च वर्तुलाकारं स्वादाम्लं तिर्यगाश्रितम्। चपलं मारुतं तत्त्वं प्रवाहेऽष्टाङ्गुलं स्मृतम्॥१७२॥ वर्णाकारस्वादवाहे अव्यक्तं सर्वगामिनम्। मोक्षदं नभसं तत्त्वं सर्वकार्येषु निष्फलम्॥१७३॥","i":"pītavarṇaṃ catuṣkoṇaṃ madhuraṃ madhyamāśritam | bhogadaṃ pārthivaṃ tattvaṃ pravāhe dvādaśāṅgulam ||169|| śvetam ardhendusaṃkāśaṃ svādu kāṣāyam ārdrakam | lābhakṛd vāruṇaṃ tattvaṃ pravāhe ṣoḍaśāṅgulam ||170|| raktaṃ trikoṇaṃ tīkṣṇaṃ ca ūrdhvabhāgapravāhakam | dīptaṃ ca taijasaṃ tattvaṃ pravāhe caturaṅgulam ||171|| nīlaṃ ca vartulākāraṃ svādāmlaṃ tiryagāśritam | capalaṃ mārutaṃ tattvaṃ pravāhe 'ṣṭāṅgulaṃ smṛtam ||172|| varṇākārasvādavāhe avyaktaṃ sarvagāminam | mokṣadaṃ nabhasaṃ tattvaṃ sarvakāryeṣu niṣphalam ||173||","f":"The earth phase is yellow, square, sweet, centrally directed, grants enjoyment, and extends twelve finger-breadths. The water phase is white, crescent-shaped, sweet and astringent, moist, produces gain, and extends sixteen. The fire phase is red, triangular, pungent, blazing, directed upward, and extends four. The air phase is dark, circular, sour, oblique, unsteady, and extends eight. The space phase has no definite colour, shape, taste or direction, pervades everything, grants release, and yields no result in any undertaking.","a":"These five verses restate what verses 164 to 168 have just said. The two accounts are consistent in their measurements — the *aṅgula* values are identical in both — and differ in emphasis: the first set foregrounds sound, temperature and speed, the second foregrounds colour, shape and taste. The redundancy is not an error and it should not be treated as one. Deliberate redundancy is a transmission technology, and its function in an oral corpus is precise. Any transmission channel with a nonzero error rate degrades a message over successive copies; the standard remedy is to encode the message with redundancy, so that a corrupted portion can be reconstructed from the surviving portion. This is the foundational insight of information theory and it applies to manuscript and oral transmission as directly as to any other channel. **[E]** A text that states its core parameters twice, in different words, is a text that survives partial loss. If verse 166 is corrupted, verse 171 preserves the fire values. The Sanskrit technical literature is full of such devices, and their density correlates with how critical the information is. Numerical values — here the *aṅgula* measurements — are the most vulnerable content in any orally transmitted text, since a corrupted number is not detectable from context in the way a corrupted word often is. …","m":"Two applications, one about learning and one about evaluating traditions. **Redundancy in encoding.** Material encoded once in one modality is more fragile than the same material encoded twice in different modalities. The relevant finding is that varied encoding — the same content approached through different examples, formats or sensory channels — produces better retention and better transfer than repeated identical exposure. **[E]** Repetition of the identical presentation produces fluency without durability; the sense of familiarity rises while performance on delayed tests does not. **[E]** The practical instruction: when learning something that must be retained, restate it in a different form rather than rereading it. Write it as prose, then as a diagram, then explain it aloud. Three different encodings beat three readings, and the difference is large. **Evaluating inherited systems.** When examining any traditional body of practical knowledge — medical, agricultural, technical, contemplative — locate the points where multiple independent lines of transmission can be compared. Content that has remained stable across independent transmission has, in most cases, been stabilized by contact with reality; content that varies has not. This is not an infallible test. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"174–177","t":"Ranking, Direction, and Diurnal Assignment","p":"PART SEVEN: THE APPLIED PHASE SYSTEM","d":"पृथ्वीजले शुभे तत्त्वे तेजो मिश्रफलोदयम्। हानिमृत्युकरौ पुंसामशुभौ व्योममारुतौ॥१७४॥ आपः पूर्वे पश्चिमे पृथ्वी तेजश्च दक्षिणे तथा। वायुश्चोत्तरदिग्ज्ञेयो मध्ये कोणगतं नभः॥१७५॥ चन्द्रे पृथ्वीजले यातां सूर्येऽग्निर्वा यदा भवेत्। तदा सिद्धिर्न सन्देहः सौम्यासौम्येषु कर्मसु॥१७६॥ लाभः पृथ्वीकृतोऽह्नि स्यान्निशायां लाभकृज्जलम्। वह्नौ मृत्युः क्षयो वायुर्नभःस्थानं दहेत्क्वचित्॥१७७॥","i":"pṛthvījale śubhe tattve tejo miśraphalodayam | hānimṛtyukarau puṃsām aśubhau vyomamārutau ||174|| āpaḥ pūrve paścime pṛthvī tejaś ca dakṣiṇe tathā | vāyuś cottaradig jñeyo madhye koṇagataṃ nabhaḥ ||175|| candre pṛthvījale yātāṃ sūrye 'gnir vā yadā bhavet | tadā siddhir na sandehaḥ saumyāsaumyeṣu karmasu ||176|| lābhaḥ pṛthvīkṛto 'hni syān niśāyāṃ lābhakṛj jalam | vahnau mṛtyuḥ kṣayo vāyur nabhaḥsthānaṃ dahet kvacit ||177||","f":"Earth and water are favourable phases, fire yields mixed results, and air and space bring loss. Water is assigned to the east, earth to the west, fire to the south, air to the north, and space to the centre and the intermediate directions. When earth or water occurs during left-channel flow, or fire during right-channel flow, undertakings succeed whether gentle or harsh. Gain comes from the earth phase by day and from the water phase by night; fire brings ruin, air brings loss, and the space condition is sometimes destructive.","a":"Verse 176 is the structurally interesting one, and it deserves attention because it demonstrates that the *Svarodaya*'s two classification systems were designed to be used together rather than in parallel. The verse specifies a *combination*: earth or water phase occurring during left-channel dominance, or fire occurring during right-channel dominance. These are cases where the phase and the channel are congruent in character — settled phase with settled channel, activating phase with activating channel — and the verse assigns success specifically to congruence rather than to either variable alone. That is an interaction term, and its presence is methodologically significant. Most of the text's prescriptive material operates on single variables: this channel favours these actions, this phase favours those. Verse 176 states that the two variables combine non-additively, so that the effect of the phase depends on which channel it occurs in. Whether the specific claim is true is unknown and untested. But the recognition that two state variables might interact rather than simply sum is a level of modelling sophistication that most folk-physiological systems never reach. **[X]** The underlying intuition — that congruence between components of a state matters — has a modern analogue worth noting. …","m":"**Expect and account for nocturnal nasal congestion.** Nasal resistance rises when supine and rises further with circadian phase, which is why nasal obstruction that is unnoticeable during the day becomes prominent at night. **[E]** The practical measures are the ordinary ones: elevate the head of the bed, address allergic rhinitis if present, and note that alcohol produces nasal vasodilation and worsens the effect. Persistent nocturnal obstruction with snoring, witnessed apnoeas, or daytime sleepiness warrants clinical assessment for sleep-disordered breathing rather than any breathing technique. **Attend to congruence between components of your state.** This is the transferable content of verse 176 and it is a genuinely useful check. When taking a state reading, note not only the values but whether they agree. Slow breath with clenched jaw is not a settled state. Reported calm with a resting pulse fifteen beats above baseline is not a settled state. High subjective energy with shallow, rapid breathing is a mobilized state that will not sustain. Incongruent readings are more informative than congruent ones, because congruence is the default and mismatch indicates that something is being suppressed, overridden, or misreported. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"181, 185–186","t":"Prediction About Persons and Journeys","p":"PART EIGHT: THE INTERROGATION APPARATUS","d":"पृथिव्यां बहुपादाः स्युर्द्विपदस्तोयवायुतः। तेजस्येव चतुष्पादो नभसा पादवर्जितः॥१८१॥ प्रश्वासप्रश्न आदित्ये यदि राहुर्गतोऽनिले। तदासौ चलितो ज्ञेयः स्थानान्तरमपेक्षते॥१८५॥ आयाति वारुणे तत्त्वे तत्रैवास्ति शुभं क्षितौ। प्रवासी पवनेऽन्यत्र मृत्युरेवानले भवेत्॥१८६॥","i":"pṛthivyāṃ bahupādāḥ syur dvipadas toyavāyutaḥ | tejasy eva catuṣpādo nabhasā pādavarjitaḥ ||181|| praśvāsapraśna āditye yadi rāhur gato 'nile | tadāsau calito jñeyaḥ sthānāntaram apekṣate ||185|| āyāti vāruṇe tattve tatraivāsti śubhaṃ kṣitau | pravāsī pavane 'nyatra mṛtyur evānale bhavet ||186||","f":"In the earth phase the subject is many-footed, in water and air two-footed, in fire four-footed, and in space without feet. If the question concerns a person and the right channel is flowing with the air phase active, that person has moved and is seeking another location. In the water phase he is returning, in the earth phase he remains well where he is, in the air phase he is elsewhere, and in the fire phase he has died.","a":"Verse 186 is the darkest passage examined so far, and it should be treated as what it is: an instruction to inform an anxious enquirer that an absent family member has died, on the basis of the practitioner's own nasal state. There is no defence of this, and the harm it is capable of causing is not hypothetical. The analysis worth conducting concerns why such systems are consulted at all, since the demand for them is the durable phenomenon and it has not diminished. Uncertainty about the welfare of an absent person is among the more aversive psychological conditions, and the aversiveness is not proportional to the probability of harm. The relevant finding is that uncertainty about an outcome can be more distressing than certainty of a bad outcome: in an experimental demonstration, participants who knew they would definitely receive an electric shock showed lower stress responses than participants told they might receive one, and intolerance of uncertainty is a well-supported transdiagnostic factor in anxiety disorders. **[E]** Ambiguous loss — the situation of a person absent without confirmed status — is associated with poorer adjustment than confirmed bereavement, and the mechanism is generally held to be the impossibility of completing the psychological work that confirmed loss permits. **[S to E]** This is what the divinatory apparatus is responding to. …","m":"**On uncertainty about others.** The technique that has support is not the manufacture of certainty but the management of its absence. Interventions targeting intolerance of uncertainty — which involve exposure to uncertain situations without resolution-seeking, and the deliberate reduction of checking and reassurance behaviours — have evidence for reducing anxiety symptoms. **[S to E]** The mechanism is that reassurance-seeking is negatively reinforced: it reduces distress briefly and thereby strengthens the behaviour, so that tolerance for uncertainty declines over time. Divinatory consultation is a reassurance-seeking behaviour and has this structure exactly. The practical implication for anyone waiting on news: the checking is the problem, not the solution. Bounded checking at fixed intervals, with the intervals decided in advance rather than in the moment, is the standard behavioural approach and it is more effective than either continuous checking or an attempt to stop entirely. **On providing certainty to others.** The rule that follows from verse 186, stated as its inverse: **do not supply confident answers about matters you have no information on, however much the person wants one.** The desire for certainty is real and the impulse to relieve it is decent, but a confident guess is worse than an acknowledged unknown, because the recipient will act on it. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"182–184","t":"The Planetary Assignments and an Internal Contradiction","p":"PART EIGHT: THE INTERROGATION APPARATUS","d":"कुजो वह्निः रविः पृथ्वी सौरिरापः प्रकीर्तितः। वायुस्थानास्थितो राहुर्दक्षरन्ध्रः प्रवाहकः॥१८२॥ जलं चन्द्रो बुधः पृथ्वी गुरुर्वातः सितोऽनलः। वामनाड्यां स्थिताः सर्वे सर्वकार्येषु निश्चिताः॥१८३॥ पृथ्वी बुधो जलादिन्दुः शुक्रो वह्निरविकुजः। वायू राहुः शनी व्योम गुरुरेव प्रकीर्तितः॥१८४॥","i":"kujo vahniḥ raviḥ pṛthvī saurir āpaḥ prakīrtitaḥ | vāyusthānāsthito rāhur dakṣarandhraḥ pravāhakaḥ ||182|| jalaṃ candro budhaḥ pṛthvī gurur vātaḥ sito 'nalaḥ | vāmanāḍyāṃ sthitāḥ sarve sarvakāryeṣu niścitāḥ ||183|| pṛthvī budho jalād induḥ śukro vahniravikujaḥ | vāyū rāhuḥ śanī vyoma gurur eva prakīrtitaḥ ||184||","f":"In the right channel, Mars corresponds to fire, the Sun to earth, Saturn to water, and Rāhu to air. In the left channel, the Moon corresponds to water, Mercury to earth, Jupiter to air, and Venus to fire. Elsewhere it is declared that Mercury is earth, the Moon water, Venus and the Sun and Mars fire, Rāhu and Saturn air, and Jupiter space.","a":"These three verses assign planets to elemental phases, and they do not agree with one another. Verse 183 assigns Jupiter to air and Venus to fire. Verse 184 assigns Jupiter to space, and gives fire to Venus together with the Sun and Mars, while verse 182 has assigned the Sun to earth. Saturn is water in 182 and air in 184. These are not variations in emphasis; they are direct contradictions in a system whose only content is the assignments themselves. The contradiction is worth dwelling on because of what it demonstrates about the text's composition. A scheme with no external referent cannot be corrected by observation, and the only constraint on its transmission is the memory and consistency of its transmitters. Over generations, competing assignments from different source traditions accumulate, and there is no mechanism by which any of them can be eliminated, because no assignment can be shown to be wrong. The result is exactly what is visible here: a compiled text carrying mutually incompatible versions of the same scheme, apparently without discomfort. Compare this with the treatment of the *aṅgula* measurements analysed under verses 169 to 173, which are preserved identically across two independent recensions within the same text. The contrast is instructive and it is the strongest internal evidence available for the thesis this book has been arguing. …","m":"Nothing in these verses transfers as practice. One thing transfers as method, and it is the most portable diagnostic in this book. **Look for internal contradictions in any system before evaluating its claims.** Where two authoritative statements within the same tradition assign incompatible values to the same variable, and the tradition has not noticed or has not cared, that tradition is not error-correcting on that variable. This is a cheap test. It requires no domain expertise, no experimental apparatus, and no access to external evidence — only careful reading and the willingness to notice. The test has a specific advantage over evaluating claims one at a time: it is *domain-general*. A person with no ability to assess whether Jupiter is more like air or more like space can still observe that the text says both. The same procedure applies to a management framework whose components contradict one another, a dietary system whose rules conflict across sources, or a technical folklore whose maxims cannot all be true simultaneously. The corresponding positive test is the one identified under verses 169 to 173: where independent lines of transmission agree exactly, especially on quantities, something was probably being checked. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"187–189","t":"The Twelve Determinations","p":"PART EIGHT: THE INTERROGATION APPARATUS","d":"पार्थिवे मूलविज्ञानं शुभं कार्यं जले तथा। आग्नेयं धातुविज्ञानं व्योम्नि शून्यं विनिर्दिशेत्॥१८७॥ तुष्टिपुष्टी रतिः क्रीडा जयहर्षौ धराजले। तेजो वायुश्च सुप्ताक्षो ज्वरकम्पः प्रवासिनः॥१८८॥ गतायुर्मृत्युराकाशे तत्त्वस्थाने प्रकीर्तितः। द्वादशैताः प्रयत्नेन ज्ञातव्या देशिकैः सदा॥१८९॥","i":"pārthive mūlavijñānaṃ śubhaṃ kāryaṃ jale tathā | āgneyaṃ dhātuvijñānaṃ vyomni śūnyaṃ vinirdiśet ||187|| tuṣṭipuṣṭī ratiḥ krīḍā jayaharṣau dharājale | tejo vāyuś ca suptākṣo jvarakampaḥ pravāsinaḥ ||188|| gatāyur mṛtyur ākāśe tattvasthāne prakīrtitaḥ | dvādaśaitāḥ prayatnena jñātavyā deśikaiḥ sadā ||189||","f":"In the earth phase the matter concerns what is rooted, in water what is favourable, in fire what is mineral, and in space one should declare it void. Contentment, nourishment, pleasure, play, victory and joy belong to the earth and water phases; sleep, fever and trembling, and the condition of one absent, to fire and air; and in the space phase, the end of life. These twelve determinations should always be known by teachers, and learned with effort.","a":"Verse 189 closes the section by enumerating and numbering the determinations, and by insisting they be learned *prayatnena*, with effort. The closing formula is standard. What deserves examination is the content of verse 188, because it is the only verse in this section that lists *bodily states* rather than external outcomes, and it therefore admits a reading the rest do not. The list divides into two groups. To earth and water: *tuṣṭi* (contentment), *puṣṭi* (nourishment), *rati* (pleasure), *krīḍā* (play), *jaya* and *harṣa* (victory and joy). To fire and air: *suptākṣa* (eyes closed, drowsiness), *jvara* (fever), *kampa* (trembling). Read as prediction about the world, this is worthless. Read as a description of the practitioner's own state, the grouping is coherent and physiologically sensible. The first group describes a state of settled, positive engagement — sufficient energy, positive affect, appetitive and social behaviour available. In autonomic terms, adequate vagal tone with moderate sympathetic availability; in behavioural terms, the profile that supports approach behaviour, play and affiliation. Play in particular is a state-dependent behaviour that is suppressed under threat across mammalian species and requires a specific physiological condition to occur at all. **[E]** The second group is more interesting: drowsiness, fever, and trembling grouped together. …","m":"**Recognize sickness behaviour as a syndrome and respond to it as one.** Fatigue, low mood, social withdrawal, reduced appetite and poor concentration occurring together, particularly with any febrile component, constitute a recognized coordinated response to inflammation rather than a set of independent problems. **[E]** The practical consequence is that the appropriate response is rest and recovery rather than the behavioural activation strategies that would be appropriate for low mood arising from other causes. Attempting to push through sickness behaviour with stimulants and effort works against a response that has adaptive functions, and the cognitive impairment associated with it is real and measurable. The distinction is clinically important and worth being precise about. Depressive symptoms and sickness behaviour overlap substantially in presentation, and the relationship between inflammation and depression is an active research area with evidence for inflammatory involvement in at least a subset of depressive presentations. **[S]** This is not grounds for self-diagnosis in either direction; it is grounds for taking the presence of a febrile or inflammatory context seriously when interpreting a change in mood and energy, and for seeking assessment when low mood is persistent. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"191–196","t":"The Five-Level Partition of the Organism","p":"PART NINE: THE BODY ANALYSED","d":"पृथिव्यापस्तथा तेजो वायुराकाशमेव च। पञ्चभूतात्मको देहो ज्ञातव्यश्च वरानने॥१९१॥ अस्थिमांसं त्वचा नाडी रोम चैव तु पञ्चमम्। पृथ्वीपञ्चगुणा प्रोक्ता ब्रह्मज्ञानेन भाषितम्॥१९२॥ शुक्रशोणितमज्जाश्च मूत्रं लाला च पञ्चमम्। आपः पञ्चगुणा प्रोक्ता ब्रह्मज्ञानेन भाषितम्॥१९३॥ क्षुधा तृषा तथा निद्रा कान्तिरालस्यमेव च। तेजः पञ्चगुणा प्रोक्ता ब्रह्मज्ञानेन भाषितम्॥१९४॥ धावनं चलनं ग्रन्थिः संकोचनप्रसारणम्। वायोः पञ्चगुणा प्रोक्ता ब्रह्मज्ञानेन भाषितम्॥१९५॥ रागद्वेषौ तथा लज्जा भयं मोहश्च पञ्चमः। नभः पञ्चगुणा प्रोक्ता ब्रह्मज्ञानेन भाषितम्॥१९६॥","i":"pṛthivyāpas tathā tejo vāyur ākāśam eva ca | pañcabhūtātmako deho jñātavyaś ca varānane ||191|| asthimāṃsaṃ tvacā nāḍī roma caiva tu pañcamam | pṛthvīpañcaguṇā proktā brahmajñānena bhāṣitam ||192|| śukraśoṇitamajjāś ca mūtraṃ lālā ca pañcamam | āpaḥ pañcaguṇā proktā brahmajñānena bhāṣitam ||193|| kṣudhā tṛṣā tathā nidrā kāntir ālasyam eva ca | tejaḥ pañcaguṇā proktā brahmajñānena bhāṣitam ||194|| dhāvanaṃ calanaṃ granthiḥ saṃkocanaprasāraṇam | vāyoḥ pañcaguṇā proktā brahmajñānena bhāṣitam ||195|| rāgadveṣau tathā lajjā bhayaṃ mohaś ca pañcamaḥ | nabhaḥ pañcaguṇā proktā brahmajñānena bhāṣitam ||196||","f":"The body is to be understood as constituted of five elements. Bone, flesh, skin, vessel and hair are the five properties of earth. Semen, blood, marrow, urine and saliva are the five of water. Hunger, thirst, sleep, radiance and lassitude are the five of fire. Running, movement, gripping, contraction and extension are the five of air. Attraction, aversion, shame, fear and delusion are the five of space.","a":"Read the five groups in order and something becomes visible that the elemental labels obscure: this is a hierarchy of levels of biological organization, ordered from the most material to the least, and the ordering is correct. Earth is structural tissue — bone, flesh, skin, vessel, hair. These are the solid, persistent, dissectible components. Water is the fluids — blood, marrow, semen, urine, saliva — the mobile transport and secretory media. Fire is a set of *drives and energetic states*. Air is *motor function*. Space is *affect*. The progression runs from anatomy to physiology to motivation to action to emotion, and each level is composed of things one could not have identified by dissection at the level before. Verse 194 is the most striking and warrants close attention. Its five items are hunger, thirst, sleep, radiance and lassitude. Four of these — hunger, thirst, sleep-wake regulation, and the arousal-energy dimension underlying *kānti* and *ālasya* — are integrated by a single anatomical structure. The hypothalamus contains the principal circuitry for feeding, for osmotic and volume regulation of drinking, and for sleep-wake switching, with the lateral hypothalamic orexin system providing a shared substrate that links feeding, arousal and wakefulness. …","m":"**Use the hierarchy as a diagnostic ladder for undifferentiated complaint.** When something is wrong and cannot be named — the common presentation of both fatigue and low mood — the five levels give an ordered set of questions that move from the cheapest to check to the most complex: *Structural:* is there pain, injury, or a physical problem? Check first because it is checkable. *Fluid:* is there dehydration, or a sign of systemic illness? Hydration status in particular is a common and easily corrected contributor to fatigue and poor concentration, and mild dehydration produces measurable decrements in mood and cognitive performance. **[E]** *Drive:* how is sleep, hunger, thirst, energy? This level should be interrogated *before* the affective one, and in practice it usually is not. Sleep restriction produces low mood, irritability, reduced positive affect and impaired executive function, and the effects are large. **[E]** A great deal of what presents as an emotional problem resolves at this level. *Motor:* has there been movement? Physical inactivity is causally implicated in low mood, and the effect of exercise on depressive symptoms is among the better-supported non-pharmacological findings, though effect sizes in well-controlled trials are more modest than popular accounts suggest. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"197–200","t":"Durations, Latencies, and the Sensory Ladder","p":"PART NINE: THE BODY ANALYSED","d":"पृथिव्याः पलानि पञ्चाशच्चत्वारिंशत्तथाम्भसः। अग्नेस्त्रिंशत्पुनर्वायोर्विंशतिर्नभसो दश॥१९७॥ पृथिव्यां चिरकालेन लाभश्चापः क्षणाद्भवेत्। जायते पवने स्वल्पः सिद्धोऽप्यग्नौ विनश्यति॥१९८॥ पृथिव्याः पञ्च ह्यपां वेदा गुणास्तेजो द्वि वायुतः। नभस्येकगुणश्चैव तत्त्वज्ञानमिदं भवेत्॥१९९॥ फूत्कारकृत्प्रस्फुटिता विदीर्णा पतिता धरा। ददाति सर्वकार्येषु अवस्थासदृशं फलम्॥२००॥","i":"pṛthivyāḥ palāni pañcāśac catvāriṃśat tathāmbhasaḥ | agnes triṃśat punar vāyor viṃśatir nabhaso daśa ||197|| pṛthivyāṃ cirakālena lābhaś cāpaḥ kṣaṇād bhavet | jāyate pavane svalpaḥ siddho 'py agnau vinaśyati ||198|| pṛthivyāḥ pañca hy apāṃ vedā guṇās tejo dvi vāyutaḥ | nabhasy ekaguṇaś caiva tattvajñānam idaṃ bhavet ||199|| phūtkārakṛt prasphuṭitā vidīrṇā patitā dharā | dadāti sarvakāryeṣu avasthāsadṛśaṃ phalam ||200||","f":"The earth phase lasts fifty *palas*, water forty, fire thirty, air twenty, and space ten. Gain from the earth phase comes only after long delay, from water immediately; from air it is slight, and in fire even what has been achieved is lost. Earth possesses five qualities, water four, fire three, air two, and space one. When the earth phase is disturbed — hissing, cracked, split or collapsed — it yields in every undertaking a result corresponding to its condition.","a":"Verse 197 gives the phase durations as a descending arithmetic series: fifty, forty, thirty, twenty, ten *palas*. A *pala* is one sixtieth of a *ghaṭikā*, roughly twenty-four seconds, making the phases approximately twenty, sixteen, twelve, eight and four minutes, totalling sixty minutes. Two observations. First, the total is exactly one hour, which is consistent with the dominance period given in verse 72 and confirms the internal arithmetic of that verse's shorter reading. Second, and more revealing, the series is perfectly regular. A descending series in equal steps of ten is not what a measured natural phenomenon looks like. It is what a schematized one looks like. This is worth stating as a general diagnostic, because it applies well beyond this text. Measured quantities are irregular; regular series in round numbers indicate that a scheme has been imposed on the measurement. The *aṅgula* extents of verse 158 — twelve, sixteen, four, eight — are irregular and unordered, which is precisely why they carry more evidential weight than these durations do. **[X, but the inference is sound]** Where a traditional system offers numbers, the ragged ones are more likely to have come from observation than the tidy ones. Verse 199 is the most interesting of the four and it is usually passed over. It states that earth has five qualities, water four, fire three, air two, and space one. …","m":"**Prefer ragged numbers to round ones when evaluating traditional claims.** This is the practical output of verse 197, and it is a fast filter. In any inherited body of quantitative claims — dosages, durations, proportions, thresholds — the values that form clean arithmetic or geometric series were probably generated by the scheme rather than measured. The values that are irregular and resist pattern are more likely to have come from somewhere. It is not a proof either way, but it directs attention efficiently. **On latency, again.** Verse 198 repeats verse 163's claim about delayed versus immediate outcomes, and adds a third case: in the fire phase, even what has been accomplished is lost. That third case is worth extracting on its own, because it names a real and under-attended failure mode. Most attention to project failure concerns projects that do not succeed. Rather less concerns achievements that are subsequently undone — the reversal of a gain already made. The relevant behavioural point is that maintenance and acquisition are different problems requiring different effort, and that the effort required for maintenance is systematically underestimated because the acquisition is treated as complete. Weight regained, skills lost through disuse, and reverted organizational changes are the common instances. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"190, 201–204","t":"The Nakṣatra Assignments","p":"PART TEN: THE ASTRONOMICAL OVERLAY AND THE APPEAL TO PRECEDENT","d":"पूर्वायां पश्चिमे याम्ये उत्तरस्यां तथाक्रमम्। पृथिव्यादीनि भूतानि बलिष्ठानि विनिर्दिशेत्॥१९०॥ धनिष्ठा रोहिणी ज्येष्ठानुराधा श्रवणं तथा। अभिजिदुत्तराषाढा पृथ्वीतत्त्वमुदाहृतम्॥२०१॥ पूर्वाषाढा तथा श्लेषा मूलमार्द्रा च रेवती। उत्तराभाद्रपदा तोयं तत्त्वं शतभिषक् प्रिये॥२०२॥ भरणी कृत्तिकापुष्यौ मघा पूर्वा च फाल्गुनी। पूर्वाभाद्रपदा स्वाती तेजस्तत्त्वमिति प्रिये॥२०३॥ विशाखोत्तरफाल्गुन्यौ हस्तचित्रे पुनर्वसुः। अश्विनी मृगशीर्षं च वायुतत्त्वमुदाहृतम्॥२०४॥","i":"pūrvāyāṃ paścime yāmye uttarasyāṃ tathākramam | pṛthivyādīni bhūtāni baliṣṭhāni vinirdiśet ||190|| dhaniṣṭhā rohiṇī jyeṣṭhānurādhā śravaṇaṃ tathā | abhijid uttarāṣāḍhā pṛthvītattvam udāhṛtam ||201|| pūrvāṣāḍhā tathā śleṣā mūlam ārdrā ca revatī | uttarābhādrapadā toyaṃ tattvaṃ śatabhiṣak priye ||202|| bharaṇī kṛttikāpuṣyau maghā pūrvā ca phālgunī | pūrvābhādrapadā svātī tejastattvam iti priye ||203|| viśākhottaraphālgunyau hastacitre punarvasuḥ | aśvinī mṛgaśīrṣaṃ ca vāyutattvam udāhṛtam ||204||","f":"The elements are strongest in the east, west, south and north respectively. Dhaniṣṭhā, Rohiṇī, Jyeṣṭhā, Anurādhā, Śravaṇa, Abhijit and Uttarāṣāḍhā are assigned to the earth phase. Pūrvāṣāḍhā, Āśleṣā, Mūla, Ārdrā, Revatī, Uttarābhādrapadā and Śatabhiṣak to water. Bharaṇī, Kṛttikā, Puṣya, Maghā, Pūrvaphālgunī, Pūrvabhādrapadā and Svātī to fire. Viśākhā, Uttaraphālgunī, Hasta, Citrā, Punarvasu, Aśvinī and Mṛgaśīrṣa to air.","a":"These verses assign twenty-eight lunar mansions to the four material phases. The assignments have no physiological content and no mechanism. They belong with the weekday, zodiacal, planetary and directional schemes analysed in Parts Two, Seven and Eight, and the analysis given there applies without modification. What is worth noting here is the *cumulative* structure, because by this point in the text a pattern is fully visible that no single verse would establish. The *Śiva Svarodaya* has now assigned the elemental phases to: the four cardinal directions, the seven weekdays, the twelve zodiacal signs, the seven planets and Rāhu, and the twenty-eight lunar mansions. Each assignment is confidently stated. None is argued for. Several contradict others. And critically, no assignment is ever *used* to make a claim that could not have been made without it — the practical instructions throughout the text depend on which phase is currently active, determined by direct observation, and never on the astronomical correspondences at all. This is diagnostic of what the astronomical layer is doing, and it is not primarily predictive. It is *integrative*. …","m":"Nothing transfers as practice. The filter transfers as method, and it is worth applying deliberately. When evaluating any framework — a management model, a personality system, a diagnostic scheme, a theory of learning styles — trace whether each of its components is *load-bearing*. Ask what would change in the framework's outputs if a given component were removed. Components whose removal changes nothing are elaboration, and their presence should not increase confidence in the framework. This test is particularly useful because elaborate frameworks are persuasive in proportion to their elaboration, and the persuasion is unwarranted. Detail produces the sense of a well-worked-out system; it does not produce predictive power, and the two are frequently inversely related, since a system with many degrees of freedom can accommodate any observation. The load-bearing test cuts through this directly and requires no expertise in the domain. ---","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"205–208","t":"The Appeal to Precedent, and the Problem of Aptitude","p":"PART TEN: THE ASTRONOMICAL OVERLAY AND THE APPEAL TO PRECEDENT","d":"वहन्नाडीस्थितो दूतो यत्पृच्छति शुभाशुभम्। तत्सर्वं सिद्धिमाप्नोति शून्ये शून्यं न संशयः॥२०५॥ पूर्णोऽपि निर्गमश्वासे सुतत्त्वेऽपि न सिद्धिदः। सूर्यश्चन्द्रस्तथा नॄणां सन्देहे सर्वसिद्धिदः॥२०६॥ तत्त्वे रामो जयं प्राप्तः सुतत्त्वे च धनञ्जयः। कौरवा निहताः सर्वे युद्धे तत्त्वविपर्ययात्॥२०७॥ जन्मान्तरीयसंस्कारात्प्रसादादथवा गुरोः। केषाञ्चिज्जायते तत्त्ववासना विमलात्मनाम्॥२०८॥","i":"vahannāḍīsthito dūto yat pṛcchati śubhāśubham | tatsarvaṃ siddhim āpnoti śūnye śūnyaṃ na saṃśayaḥ ||205|| pūrṇo 'pi nirgamaśvāse sutattve 'pi na siddhidaḥ | sūryaś candras tathā nṝṇāṃ sandehe sarvasiddhidaḥ ||206|| tattve rāmo jayaṃ prāptaḥ sutattve ca dhanañjayaḥ | kauravā nihatāḥ sarve yuddhe tattvaviparyayāt ||207|| janmāntarīyasaṃskārāt prasādād athavā guroḥ | keṣāñcij jāyate tattvavāsanā vimalātmanām ||208||","f":"Whatever a questioner asks while standing on the side of the flowing channel will be fulfilled; where there is emptiness, the result is empty. Even a full channel during exhalation, and even in a favourable phase, does not grant success; the sun and moon grant all success where there is doubt. Rāma obtained victory through the phase, as did Arjuna; the Kauravas were destroyed in battle through its reversal. In some, through impressions carried from another birth or through a teacher's grace, an aptitude for the phases arises naturally.","a":"Verse 207 is the only place in the *Śiva Svarodaya* where evidence is explicitly offered for its central claim, and the evidence is that Rāma and Arjuna won their battles because their nasal phase was favourable and the Kauravas lost because theirs was not. The structure is worth taking seriously as an epistemic artefact, because it is not a mistake peculiar to this text. Three properties make the argument useless. The cases are fictional, so no observation of them occurred. The cases are selected, being the most celebrated victories and defeats available in the culture. And the phase attribution is retrodicted: nobody recorded the combatants' nasal dominance, so the phase is inferred *from* the outcome and then offered as its cause. Each of these three is individually fatal, and the combination is the standard form of a certain kind of argument that persists because it is compelling rather than because it is valid. The compellingness has a documented basis. Narrative cases exert influence on belief out of proportion to their evidential weight, and this is one of the more robust findings in judgement research: concrete, vivid, causally-structured single cases outweigh aggregate statistical information even when the statistical information is explicitly provided and clearly more relevant. …","m":"**Distrust the argument from famous cases.** When a practice is supported by pointing at conspicuous successes who used it, the question that resolves the matter is: how many people used it and did not succeed, and how many succeeded without it. If those numbers are unknown, the argument carries no weight regardless of how many successes are named. This applies to traditional practices, to business methodology, to training regimens, and to almost every recommendation that arrives with a testimonial attached. The specific tell to watch for is retrodiction: whether the supposed cause was recorded *before* the outcome or inferred afterward. A cause identified after the fact, in a case selected for its outcome, is not evidence. It is a story. **Treat aptitude as a starting point, not an explanation.** When you or someone else is poor at something, the useful response is to identify what specifically is failing rather than to name a trait. In the case of interoception, the specific components are separable: raw detection sensitivity, the confidence attached to a judgement, and the correspondence between the two. They dissociate, and they respond differently to training. **[E]** A person who is poor at identifying their internal states may be failing at detection, or at labelling, or at calibrating confidence, and these require different remedies. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"209–213","t":"Seed Syllable, Form, and Claimed Attainment","p":"PART ELEVEN: THE BĪJA PRACTICES","d":"लं बीजं धरणीं ध्यायेच्चतुरस्रां सुपीतभाम्। सुगन्धां स्वर्णवर्णाभां प्राप्नुयाद्देहलाघवम्॥२०९॥ वं बीजं वारुणं ध्यायेदर्धचन्द्रं शशिप्रभम्। क्षुत्तृष्णादिसहिष्णुत्वं जलमध्ये च मज्जनम्॥२१०॥ रं बीजमग्निजं ध्यायेत्त्रिकोणमरुणप्रभम्। बह्वन्नपानभोक्तृत्वमातपाग्निसहिष्णुता॥२११॥ यं बीजं पवनं ध्यायेद्वर्तुलं श्यामलप्रभम्। आकाशगमनाद्यं च पक्षिवद्गमनं तथा॥२१२॥ हं बीजं गगनं ध्यायेन्निराकारं बहुप्रभम्। ज्ञानं त्रिकालविषयमैश्वर्यमणिमादिकम्॥२१३॥","i":"laṃ bījaṃ dharaṇīṃ dhyāyec caturasrāṃ supītabhām | sugandhāṃ svarṇavarṇābhāṃ prāpnuyād dehalāghavam ||209|| vaṃ bījaṃ vāruṇaṃ dhyāyed ardhacandraṃ śaśiprabham | kṣuttṛṣṇādisahiṣṇutvaṃ jalamadhye ca majjanam ||210|| raṃ bījam agnijaṃ dhyāyet trikoṇam aruṇaprabham | bahvannapānabhoktṛtvam ātapāgnisahiṣṇutā ||211|| yaṃ bījaṃ pavanaṃ dhyāyed vartulaṃ śyāmalaprabham | ākāśagamanādyaṃ ca pakṣivad gamanaṃ tathā ||212|| haṃ bījaṃ gaganaṃ dhyāyen nirākāraṃ bahuprabham | jñānaṃ trikālaviṣayam aiśvaryam aṇimādikam ||213||","f":"Contemplate earth with the syllable *laṃ*, as a yellow square, fragrant and golden; the result is lightness of body. Contemplate water with *vaṃ*, as a white crescent with the brightness of the moon; the result is tolerance of hunger and thirst, and immersion in water. Contemplate fire with *raṃ*, as a red triangle; the result is the capacity for large intake of food and drink, and endurance of heat. Contemplate air with *yaṃ*, as a dark circle; the result is movement through the air, like a bird. Contemplate space with *haṃ*, as formless and radiant; the result is knowledge of past, present and future, and the powers beginning with minuteness.","a":"The technique specified is precise and consistent: a monosyllable repeated, a simple geometric form visualized, a colour, and a location. This is focused-attention meditation with a compound object, and it is one of the better-characterized categories of contemplative practice. Take the components in turn. **The syllable.** Sustained repetition of a phonologically simple, semantically empty syllable is a well-defined attentional task. The seeds here — *laṃ*, *vaṃ*, *raṃ*, *yaṃ*, *haṃ* — all terminate in the nasal *anusvāra*, meaning that each repetition ends in a sustained nasal hum. This is not incidental. Humming produces vibration transmitted through the skull and, more measurably, a dramatic increase in nasal nitric oxide output — humming has been shown to raise nasal NO by an order of magnitude compared with quiet exhalation, an effect attributed to increased gas exchange between the paranasal sinuses and the nasal cavity. **[E]** The functional significance for the practitioner is not established, but the physiological effect is large and reproducible. More directly relevant: mantra repetition and humming-based practices have been associated with reduced default-mode network activity and with altered connectivity between default-network and attentional regions. …","m":"**The practice is worth doing.** Choose one syllable and one form — the pairing does not matter, and the elemental attribution can be discarded entirely. Sit, close the eyes, repeat the syllable aloud or sub-vocally at a comfortable rate, and hold the geometric figure in the visual field. Ten to fifteen minutes. What is being trained is the detection of attentional lapse and the return of attention to a defined object, repeated across many trials. That is the operative component of focused-attention practice, and the documented outcomes — improved sustained attention, reduced mind-wandering, altered default-network connectivity — follow from it. **[E]** The specific syllable and the specific shape are arbitrary; what matters is that the object is unambiguous enough that drift is immediately detectable. **Two practical notes.** Aloud is easier than silent, particularly at the beginning, because the motor act provides additional feedback about whether the practice is continuing. And the terminal nasal hum is worth preserving whichever syllable is chosen, since it lengthens exhalation, which raises the vagal component of respiratory sinus arrhythmia, and produces the nasal nitric oxide effect. **[E]** **On the attainment ladder.** The general form of the caution deserves stating plainly, because it applies far beyond contemplative practice. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"214–219","t":"Prāṇa as the Primary Relation","p":"PART TWELVE: PRĀṆA AS MEASURED QUANTITY","d":"स्वरज्ञानं धनं गुप्तं धनं नास्ति ततः परम्। गम्यते तु स्वरज्ञानं ह्यनायासं फलं भवेत्॥२१४॥ देव देव महादेव महाज्ञानं स्वरोदयम्। त्रिकालविषयं चैव कथं भवति शंकर॥२१५॥ एतत्त्रिकालविज्ञानं नान्यद्भवति सुन्दरि॥२१६॥ तत्त्वे शुभाशुभं कार्यं तत्त्वे जयपराजयौ। तत्त्वे सुभिक्षदुर्भिक्षे तत्त्वं त्रिपादमुच्यते॥२१७॥ देव देव महादेव सर्वसंसारसागरे। किं नराणां परं मित्रं सर्वकार्यार्थसाधकम्॥२१८॥ प्राण एव परं मित्रं प्राण एव परः सखा। प्राणतुल्यो परो बन्धुर्नास्ति नास्ति वरानने॥२१९॥","i":"svarajñānaṃ dhanaṃ guptaṃ dhanaṃ nāsti tataḥ param | gamyate tu svarajñānaṃ hy anāyāsaṃ phalaṃ bhavet ||214|| deva deva mahādeva mahājñānaṃ svarodayam | trikālaviṣayaṃ caiva kathaṃ bhavati śaṃkara ||215|| etat trikālavijñānaṃ nānyad bhavati sundari ||216|| tattve śubhāśubhaṃ kāryaṃ tattve jayaparājayau | tattve subhikṣadurbhikṣe tattvaṃ tripādam ucyate ||217|| deva deva mahādeva sarvasaṃsārasāgare | kiṃ narāṇāṃ paraṃ mitraṃ sarvakāryārthasādhakam ||218|| prāṇa eva paraṃ mitraṃ prāṇa eva paraḥ sakhā | prāṇatulyo paro bandhur nāsti nāsti varānane ||219||","f":"Knowledge of the breath is hidden wealth; there is no wealth greater, and it is attainable without effort. How does this great knowledge encompass the three times? This alone is knowledge of the three times, and there is no other. In the phase lie favourable and unfavourable action, victory and defeat, plenty and scarcity; the phase is said to have three parts. What is the highest friend of a person in the ocean of existence, accomplishing every purpose? Breath alone is the highest friend, the highest companion; there is no kinsman equal to it.","a":"Verse 214's characterization of *svara-jñāna* as *dhanaṃ guptam*, hidden wealth, obtainable *anāyāsam*, without exertion, states a real property of the practice and is worth taking at face value. The claim is about cost. Every intervention on physiological state has a cost structure, and respiratory intervention has an unusual one: no equipment, no expenditure, no time set aside, no dependence on circumstance, and no requirement that anyone else be involved or informed. It can be performed in a meeting, on public transport, in a hospital waiting room. Almost nothing else with a comparable effect on autonomic state shares this profile — exercise requires time and space, sleep requires hours, pharmacological intervention requires a prescription and carries side effects. This matters more than it first appears, because the binding constraint on behavioural intervention is almost never efficacy. It is adherence, and adherence is dominated by friction. Interventions requiring equipment, scheduling, or the cooperation of others are abandoned at high rates regardless of how well they work. **[E]** A tool with near-zero friction can be deployed at a frequency that compensates for a modest per-use effect. The *Svarodaya*'s claim about effortlessness is therefore not a rhetorical flourish; it is an accurate statement about why this particular intervention is worth having. …","m":"**Optimize for friction, not for efficacy.** When choosing among self-regulation practices, the practice you will actually perform at high frequency beats the more effective practice you will perform rarely. Respiratory technique wins this comparison against nearly everything, which is the substance of verse 214's claim. The design implication: strip the practice down until nothing remains that could prevent it happening. No specific posture, no set duration, no requirement for a quiet room, no equipment, no app. Three slow breaths with lengthened exhalation, performed twenty times a day at moments of transition, will outperform a twenty-minute daily session that happens three times a week — not because the mechanism is stronger but because it happens. **Read the state as evidence about the recent past.** This is the most usable version of the *tri-kāla* claim and it is under-practised. When you notice elevated arousal, the useful question is not only what to do about it but *what preceded it*. States have proximate causes, often within the last hour, and often invisible at the time — a message read and dismissed, a conversation that seemed fine, a decision deferred. Reading backward from the physiological state to its trigger is a reliable way of surfacing things that were noted and not processed. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"220–223","t":"The Excursion of the Breath","p":"PART TWELVE: PRĀṆA AS MEASURED QUANTITY","d":"कथं प्राणस्थितो वायुर्देहः किं प्राणरूपकः। तत्त्वेषु सञ्चरन्प्राणो ज्ञायते योगिभिः कथम्॥२२०॥ कायानगरमध्यस्थो मारुतो रक्षपालकः। प्रवेशे दशाङ्गुलः प्रोक्तो निर्गमे द्वादशाङ्गुलः॥२२१॥ गमने तु चतुर्विंशन्नेत्रवेदास्तु धावने। मैथुने पञ्चषष्टिश्च शयने च शताङ्गुलम्॥२२२॥ प्राणस्य तु गतिर्देवि स्वभावाद्द्वादशाङ्गुलम्। भोजने वमने चैव गतिरष्टादशाङ्गुलम्॥२२३॥","i":"kathaṃ prāṇasthito vāyur dehaḥ kiṃ prāṇarūpakaḥ | tattveṣu sañcaran prāṇo jñāyate yogibhiḥ katham ||220|| kāyānagaramadhyastho māruto rakṣapālakaḥ | praveśe daśāṅgulaḥ prokto nirgame dvādaśāṅgulaḥ ||221|| gamane tu caturviṃśan netravedās tu dhāvane | maithune pañcaṣaṣṭiś ca śayane ca śatāṅgulam ||222|| prāṇasya tu gatir devi svabhāvād dvādaśāṅgulam | bhojane vamane caiva gatir aṣṭādaśāṅgulam ||223||","f":"The breath, guardian within the city of the body, extends ten finger-breadths on inhalation and twelve on exhalation. In walking it extends twenty-four, in running forty-two, in sexual activity sixty-five, and in sleep a hundred. Its natural extent is twelve; in eating and in vomiting, eighteen.","a":"This is the most substantial quantitative claim in the *Śiva Svarodaya* and it deserves to be taken seriously as data. The variable being measured is the reach of the exhaled breath — the distance at which airflow remains detectable, presumably by the back of the hand, as described at verse 158. The claim is that this quantity scales with activity: twelve at rest, eighteen while eating, twenty-four walking, forty-two running, sixty-five during sexual activity, one hundred in sleep. The first five values are unambiguously correct in their ordering, and they track something real. Minute ventilation scales with metabolic demand: tidal volume and respiratory rate both increase with exercise intensity, and peak expiratory flow rises accordingly. **[E]** A more forceful exhalation produces a jet with higher exit velocity, which remains coherent and detectable at greater distance. Walking, running and sexual activity are correctly ordered by metabolic cost, and eating sits appropriately just above rest — postprandial thermogenesis and the mechanical work of mastication and swallowing both raise ventilation modestly. **[E]** The inclusion of vomiting alongside eating is a further correct observation: emesis involves forceful expiratory effort against a closed glottis followed by explosive expulsion. …","m":"**Measure your own series.** Exhalation reach at rest, after walking, after climbing stairs, after eating. Back of the hand, distance in finger-breadths. This produces a personal version of the text's table in under an hour, using its own method, and confirms directly that respiratory output scales with metabolic demand. The value is not the numbers. It is that this is a genuine physiological measurement, made with nothing, that most people have never made. It converts an abstract fact about ventilation into something observed. **On sleep breathing.** The recommendation that follows from the anomalous sleep value is worth stating on its own merits. Nasal breathing during sleep is preferable to oral breathing, and the measures that support it are the ordinary ones: treating nasal obstruction where it exists, avoiding alcohol before bed since it causes nasal congestion and upper airway muscle relaxation, elevating the head of the bed, and side-sleeping — which, as established at verse 122, means the left side. Two cautions. First, mouth-taping has become widely promoted and the evidence base is thin; more importantly, it is potentially dangerous in anyone with undiagnosed obstructive sleep apnoea or significant nasal obstruction, since it removes a compensatory airway. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"224–225","t":"The Reduction Ladder","p":"PART TWELVE: PRĀṆA AS MEASURED QUANTITY","d":"एकाङ्गुले कृते न्यूने प्राणे स्यान्निष्कामता। आनन्दस्तु द्वितीये स्यात्कविशक्तिस्तृतीयके॥२२४॥ वाचासिद्धिश्चतुर्थे च दूरदृष्टिस्तु पञ्चमे। षष्ठे त्वाकाशगमनं चण्डवेगश्च सप्तमे॥२२५॥","i":"ekāṅgule kṛte nyūne prāṇe syān niṣkāmatā | ānandas tu dvitīye syāt kaviśaktis tṛtīyake ||224|| vācāsiddhiś caturthe ca dūradṛṣṭis tu pañcame | ṣaṣṭhe tv ākāśagamanaṃ caṇḍavegaś ca saptame ||225||","f":"When the extent of the breath is reduced by one finger-breadth, freedom from craving arises; by two, a condition of wellbeing; by three, creative capacity; by four, effective speech; by five, distant vision; by six, movement through space; by seven, extraordinary speed.","a":"These two verses state the *Śiva Svarodaya*'s central technical thesis in its most compressed and most testable form: reduce the excursion of the breath, and specific psychological consequences follow in a graded sequence. What does reducing excursion mean operationally? A shorter exhalation reach means a lower peak expiratory flow, which means either reduced tidal volume, a slower expiratory rate, or both. Sustained across a series of breaths, that is the definition of slow, shallow, controlled breathing. The reduction ladder is a description of progressively deeper respiratory downregulation. **[E]** That makes the first four rungs assessable against the slow-breathing literature. *Niṣkāmatā*, freedom from craving, at one *aṅgula*. The most defensible reading is reduced reactivity to appetitive impulse. There is a plausible mechanism and some support: higher vagally-mediated heart-rate variability is associated with better inhibitory control and reduced impulsive responding, and acute slow-breathing protocols raise HRV. **[S; the correlational evidence is reasonable, the causal chain from acute breathing to reduced craving less established]** Slow-breathing and related regulation techniques have been examined as craving interventions with mixed but not negligible results. **[S]** *Ānanda*, wellbeing, at two. …","m":"**The protocol.** Reduce breath excursion progressively over a session. Begin with the natural breath and simply observe it. Then, without forcing, allow each exhalation to become slightly slower and quieter than the last. Check the reach against the back of the hand periodically. Aim for a total cycle around ten seconds — approximately four seconds in, six out — which places the rate near six breaths per minute. Six per minute is the resonance frequency for most adults, at which respiration-driven blood pressure oscillations and the baroreflex enter phase alignment and produce very large heart-rate oscillations. **[E]** Five minutes at this rate is the best-supported acute non-pharmacological intervention on autonomic state currently available, and it is what the reduction ladder amounts to when its metaphysics is removed. **What to expect, by rung.** The wellbeing effect is reliable and arrives within minutes for most people. The reduction in impulsive reactivity is plausible and worth testing against a specific craving rather than accepted in general. The creativity claim is untested and should be treated as an experiment: try it before a task requiring idea generation, record the outcome honestly, and note that the honest record will include the failures. The speech effect is real but ordinary. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"178 and 180","t":"The Question Framework","p":"PART EIGHT: THE INTERROGATION APPARATUS","d":"जीवितव्ये जये लाभे कृष्यां च धनकर्मणि। मन्त्रार्थे युद्धप्रश्ने च गमनागमने तथा॥१७८॥ पृथिव्यां मूलचिन्ता स्याज्जीवनस्य जलवातयोः। तेजसा धातुचिन्ता स्याच्छून्याकाशतो वदेत्॥१८०॥","i":"jīvitavye jaye lābhe kṛṣyāṃ ca dhanakarmaṇi | mantrārthe yuddhapraśne ca gamanāgamane tathā ||178|| pṛthivyāṃ mūlacintā syāj jīvanasya jalavātayoḥ | tejasā dhātucintā syāc chūnyākāśato vadet ||180||","f":"Concerning survival, victory, gain, agriculture, financial matters, the efficacy of mantra, questions of battle, and departures and arrivals: in the earth phase the matter concerns what is rooted; in the water and air phases, what is living; in the fire phase, mineral things; and in the space phase, one should declare the matter void.","a":"Verse 180 describes a specific consultative procedure and it is worth setting out plainly, because its structure is the same everywhere such practices are found. A questioner arrives. The practitioner observes their own respiratory phase. From that phase, the practitioner determines not the *answer* to the question but the *category* the question falls into — vegetable, animal, mineral, or void. This is category divination, and it has an important formal property: the categories are broad, exhaustive, and mutually exclusive. Every possible subject of enquiry falls into one of them. A practitioner who declares \"the matter concerns something living\" has said something that will be judged correct for an enormous proportion of questions, since the questions people bring to diviners are overwhelmingly about people. The mechanism by which such statements are experienced as accurate is well documented. Statements that are general, high base-rate, and applicable to most people are accepted as specifically accurate by the recipient — the Barnum or Forer effect, which has been replicated many times and whose strength increases when the recipient believes the statement was generated specifically for them and when the source is perceived as authoritative. …","m":"The divinatory content should be discarded entirely. Two components of the encounter transfer. **Articulation to a listener.** When a decision has stalled, state the problem aloud, in full, to another person, with the explicit understanding that they are not required to solve it. The benefit accrues to the speaker through the act of externalizing, and it does not depend on the listener's expertise. The effect is well enough established in software engineering practice that it has a name there — explaining a problem to an inanimate object frequently produces the solution — and the underlying mechanism is that verbal articulation forces the explicit representation of steps that were being skipped. **[E]** **External termination of deliberation.** For decisions that are genuinely close — where extended analysis has not separated the options — the analysis is not going to separate them, and continued deliberation has a cost. There is experimental evidence that people facing close decisions who are induced to choose one way rather than the other report greater satisfaction afterwards than those who remain undecided, which suggests that for close calls the decision procedure matters less than the decision being made. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"226–228","t":"The Upper Rungs and the Teacher's Word","p":"PART THIRTEEN: THE LIMITS OF WRITTEN TRANSMISSION","d":"अष्टमे सिद्धयश्चैव नवमे निधयो नव। दशमे दशमूर्तिश्च छाया चैकादशे भवेत्॥२२६॥ द्वादशे हंसचारश्च गङ्गामृतरसं पिबेत्। आनखाग्रं प्राणपूर्णे कस्य भक्ष्यं च भोजनम्॥२२७॥ एवं प्राणविधिः प्रोक्तः सर्वकार्यफलप्रदः। ज्ञायते गुरुवाक्येन न विद्याशास्त्रकोटिभिः॥२२८॥","i":"aṣṭame siddhayaś caiva navame nidhayo nava | daśame daśamūrtiś ca chāyā caikādaśe bhavet ||226|| dvādaśe haṃsacāraś ca gaṅgāmṛtarasaṃ pibet | ānakhāgraṃ prāṇapūrṇe kasya bhakṣyaṃ ca bhojanam ||227|| evaṃ prāṇavidhiḥ proktaḥ sarvakāryaphalapradaḥ | jñāyate guruvākyena na vidyāśāstrakoṭibhiḥ ||228||","f":"In the eighth reduction come the accomplishments, in the ninth the nine treasures, in the tenth the tenfold form, in the eleventh the shadow, and in the twelfth the movement of the *haṃsa*, when one drinks the nectar and *prāṇa* fills the body to the fingertips — what then is there to eat or drink? Thus the method of the breath has been stated. It is known through the word of a teacher, not through ten million treatises.","a":"Verses 226 and 227 complete the reduction ladder begun at verse 224. The upper rungs are unattainable and require no further analysis beyond what was said there. Verse 228 is the important one, and it is the only place in the *Śiva Svarodaya* where the text states a limit on its own medium. *Jñāyate guru-vākyena na vidyā-śāstra-koṭibhiḥ* — known through the word of a teacher, not through ten million treatises. A written text is asserting that the knowledge it contains cannot be conveyed in writing. That is not a rhetorical defence of institutional authority, though it has often been used as one. It is a substantially correct claim about the kind of knowledge in question. The distinction being drawn is between propositional and procedural knowledge, or in Polanyi's formulation between explicit and tacit knowing. The core of this discipline is a *perceptual discrimination*: the ability to detect which nostril is dominant, to distinguish rapid alternation from stable flow, to notice the qualitative shift the text calls a change of phase. Perceptual discriminations of this kind are notoriously resistant to verbal transmission. A description of what to look for does not confer the ability to see it, in this domain or in any other. **[E]** What confers it is feedback. …","m":"**Get feedback, and prefer instruments to authorities where both are available.** For any perceptual or physical skill being learned from text — including everything in this book — the reading is not the learning. The learning happens when an attempt is checked against something that can disagree. For the specific skills in the *Śiva Svarodaya*, objective checks exist and are trivial: *Nasal dominance:* judge first, then exhale onto a mirror. The larger condensation patch is the answer. Repeated pairing of prediction with feedback is how discrimination is trained. **[E]** *Breath rate:* count against a clock rather than estimating. Most people substantially misestimate their own respiratory rate. *Exhalation reach:* measure with the hand as verse 158 instructs, rather than assessing by feel. *Heart rate:* if using any practice claimed to alter autonomic state, check the pulse before and after. A finger on the radial artery for fifteen seconds is sufficient to detect the change that six-breaths-per-minute breathing produces. **On the limits of self-taught practice.** The honest position, which follows from the same argument, is that some things in this material cannot be verified alone and should be held loosely. …","g":["E"]},{"v":"229–234","t":"Timing, Distance, and the Preparatory Formula","p":"PART FOURTEEN: THE MILITARY APPLICATION","d":"प्रातश्चन्द्रो रविः सायं यदि दैवान्न लभ्यते। मध्याह्नमध्यरात्रश्च परतस्तु प्रवर्तते॥२२९॥ दूरयुद्धे जयी चन्द्रः समासन्ने दिवाकरः। वहन्नाड्यागतः पादः सर्वसिद्धिप्रदायकः॥२३०॥ यात्रारम्भे विवाहे च प्रवेशे नगरादिके। शुभकार्याणि सिध्यन्ति चन्द्रचारेषु सर्वदा॥२३१॥ जीवं रक्ष जीवं रक्ष जीवाङ्गे परिधाय च। जीवो जपति यो युद्धे जीवं जयति मेदिनीम्॥२३३॥ भूमौ जले च कर्तव्यं गमनं शान्तकर्मसु। वह्नौ वायौ प्रदीप्तेषु खे पुनर्न भयेष्वपि॥२३४॥","i":"prātaś candro raviḥ sāyaṃ yadi daivān na labhyate | madhyāhnamadhyarātraś ca paratas tu pravartate ||229|| dūrayuddhe jayī candraḥ samāsanne divākaraḥ | vahannāḍyāgataḥ pādaḥ sarvasiddhipradāyakaḥ ||230|| yātrārambhe vivāhe ca praveśe nagarādike | śubhakāryāṇi sidhyanti candracāreṣu sarvadā ||231|| jīvaṃ rakṣa jīvaṃ rakṣa jīvāṅge paridhāya ca | jīvo japati yo yuddhe jīvaṃ jayati medinīm ||233|| bhūmau jale ca kartavyaṃ gamanaṃ śāntakarmasu | vahnau vāyau pradīpteṣu khe punar na bhayeṣv api ||234||","f":"The moon channel should flow in the morning and the sun in the evening; failing that, at midday and midnight. In combat at a distance the left channel favours victory, in close combat the right, and the first step should be taken with the foot on the side of the flowing channel. Auspicious undertakings — beginning a journey, marriage, entering a city — succeed during left-channel flow. Whoever, having secured the formula on the living side, recites \"protect the life, protect the life\" in battle conquers. Movement should be made during the earth and water phases in peaceful undertakings, during fire and air in urgent ones, and during the space phase not at all, not even under threat.","a":"Verse 230 makes a distinction that is worth taking seriously despite the absence of any mechanism connecting it to nostrils. Distant combat is assigned to the left channel, close combat to the right. These are genuinely different tasks with different physiological requirements, and the assignment is in the right direction. Ranged engagement — archery in the text's context — is a precision task. Its limiting factors are steadiness of aim, fine motor control, and the timing of release. Physiological tremor increases with catecholamine level, and elevated arousal degrades precisely this class of performance; the effect is well enough established that beta-blockade improves scores in precision shooting and is banned in Olympic archery and pistol events for that reason. **[E]** Close combat is the opposite: gross motor output, speed of reaction, tolerance of pain, and the ability to sustain force. All of these are enhanced by sympathetic mobilization. **[E]** So the text has correctly identified that two activities falling under a single cultural heading — fighting — have opposite optimal arousal states, and has assigned them accordingly. That is the same discrimination made at verses 106 and 114 between initial learning and effortful drilling, and it is the *Śiva Svarodaya*'s most reliable analytical move: subdividing a folk category by physiological demand. …","m":"**Build a pre-performance routine, and make it short, fixed and physical.** The components that carry the evidence: a fixed sequence, executed identically every time, occupying thirty to sixty seconds, involving physical action rather than thought, and concluding with a defined trigger that initiates the performance. Content is arbitrary. Consistency is not. A workable structure for non-athletic contexts — a presentation, a difficult conversation, a piece of demanding work: *Physical reset:* stand, one deliberate exhalation longer than the inhalation, shoulders released. *Attentional narrowing:* one sentence stating the immediate objective, said internally, concerning process rather than outcome — \"speak slowly and finish each sentence,\" not \"make them agree.\" *Trigger:* a fixed physical action that starts the performance — sitting down, opening the file, the first step forward. The reason this works is not mysterious. It replaces the interval of unstructured anticipation, which fills with outcome rumination, with a rehearsed sequence that occupies the same attentional capacity. **[E]** **Match arousal to task type, as verse 230 does.** Before precision work — anything where tremor, fine control, or careful discrimination is the limiting factor — downregulate. Before effortful or confrontational work, do not. …","g":["E","S","X"]},{"v":"235–245","t":"The Combat Procedures","p":"PART FOURTEEN: THE MILITARY APPLICATION","d":"जीवेन शस्त्रं बध्नीयाज्जीवेनैव विकाशयेत्। जीवेन प्रक्षिपेच्छस्त्रं युद्धे जयति सर्वदा॥२३५॥ आकृष्य प्राणपवनं समारोहेत वाहनम्। समुत्तरे पदं दद्यात्सर्वकार्याणि साधयेत्॥२३६॥ अपूर्णे शत्रुसामग्रीं पूर्णे वा स्वबलं तथा। कुरुते पूर्णतत्त्वस्थो जयत्येको वसुन्धराम्॥२३७॥ आदौ तु क्रियते मुद्रा पश्चाद्युद्धं समाचरेत्। सर्पमुद्रा कृता येन तस्य सिद्धिर्न संशयः॥२३९॥ यत्र नाड्यां वहेद्वायुस्तदङ्गे प्राणमेव च। आकृष्य गच्छेत्कर्णान्तं जयत्येव पुरन्दरम्॥२४२॥ प्रतिपक्षप्रहारेभ्यः पूर्णाङ्गं योऽभिरक्षति। न तस्य रिपुभिः शक्तिर्बलिष्ठैरपि हन्यते॥२४३॥ अङ्गुष्ठतर्जनीवंशे पादाङ्गुष्ठे तथा ध्वनिः। युद्धकाले च कर्तव्यो लक्ष्यं योद्धुं जयी भवेत्॥२४४॥","i":"jīvena śastraṃ badhnīyāj jīvenaiva vikāśayet | jīvena prakṣipec chastraṃ yuddhe jayati sarvadā ||235|| ākṛṣya prāṇapavanaṃ samāroheta vāhanam | samuttare padaṃ dadyāt sarvakāryāṇi sādhayet ||236|| apūrṇe śatrusāmagrīṃ pūrṇe vā svabalaṃ tathā | kurute pūrṇatattvastho jayaty eko vasundharām ||237|| ādau tu kriyate mudrā paścād yuddhaṃ samācaret | sarpamudrā kṛtā yena tasya siddhir na saṃśayaḥ ||239|| yatra nāḍyāṃ vahed vāyus tadaṅge prāṇam eva ca | ākṛṣya gacchet karṇāntaṃ jayaty eva purandaram ||242|| pratipakṣaprahārebhyaḥ pūrṇāṅgaṃ yo 'bhirakṣati | na tasya ripubhiḥ śaktir baliṣṭhair api hanyate ||243|| aṅguṣṭhatarjanīvaṃśe pādāṅguṣṭhe tathā dhvaniḥ | yuddhakāle ca kartavyo lakṣyaṃ yoddhuṃ jayī bhavet ||244||","f":"Fasten the weapon, draw it, and release it on the side of the flowing channel, and victory follows. Draw in the breath before mounting, and step forward. Reckon the enemy's strength by the empty side and your own by the full; whoever is established in the full phase conquers alone. Make the seal first and engage afterwards; whoever makes the serpent seal succeeds. Draw the breath on the flowing side back to the ear, and one conquers even Indra. Whoever guards the side of the flowing channel is not struck even by the strong. A sound made with thumb and forefinger, and at the great toe, should be produced at the time of battle by one engaging a target.","a":"This block is a complete preparatory protocol, and read as such it is coherent. Draw the breath. Mount. Step out with the correct foot. Form the seal. Draw the bow to the ear on the flowing side. Guard the flowing side. Make the sound. Verse 242 requires a moment's attention because it is the only technical instruction in the block with a plain physical reading. *Ākṛṣya gacchet karṇāntam* — draw back as far as the ear — is the description of a full-length bow draw to anchor at the ear rather than at the chest. The ear anchor is the longer draw, giving greater draw length, greater stored energy and higher arrow velocity, and it is the technique associated with the composite bows of the region. This is archery instruction, and it is correct archery instruction. The nasal attribution is decoration on a real technical point. **[E for the technique]** Verse 235 deserves note for a different reason. Fasten, draw and release on the same side: the underlying instruction is *consistency*. Whatever side is used, use it for every phase of the action. This is sound and has nothing to do with nostrils. Motor skills are acquired as sequences, and variability in the setup phase degrades the reliability of the terminal phase; the consistency of the preparatory sequence is what makes the skilled movement reproducible under pressure. …","m":"**Keep the consistency rule.** For any skill executed under pressure, standardize the preparatory sequence completely — the same grip, the same setup, the same order of operations, every time. The reason is that variability in preparation propagates into variability in execution, and pressure amplifies whatever variability exists. This is why elite performers in every self-paced skill have invariant setup routines, and it is what verse 235 is describing without knowing it. **[E]** **Discard anything that alters perceived risk.** This is the practical warning from verse 243 and it generalizes. Be suspicious of any practice, belief, or piece of equipment whose principal effect is to make you feel safer rather than to make you safer, particularly where the two can be distinguished. The feeling changes behaviour; the behaviour meets the actual risk. The diagnostic question is straightforward: does this reduce the probability of harm, or does it reduce my estimate of the probability of harm? If the second, the intervention is working on the wrong variable, and its effect on behaviour will be adverse in proportion to how well it works. **On the useful residue.** What survives from this block is the pre-engagement sequence: a fixed, brief, physical routine performed before something demanding, with no claim attached about the outcome. …","g":["E"]},{"v":"246–249","t":"Respiratory Phase and the Spontaneous Mantra","p":"PART FOURTEEN: THE MILITARY APPLICATION","d":"श्वासप्रवेशकाले तु दूतो जल्पति वाञ्छितम्। तस्यार्थः सिद्धिमायाति निर्गमे नैव सुन्दरि॥२४६॥ लाभादीन्यपि कार्याणि पृष्ठानि कीर्तितानि च। जीवे विशति सिध्यन्ति हानिर्निःसरणे भवेत्॥२४७॥ नरे दक्षा स्वकीया च स्त्रियां वामा प्रशस्यते। कुम्भको युद्धकाले च तिस्रो नाड्यस्त्रयीगतिः॥२४८॥ हकारस्य सकारस्य विना भेदं स्वरः कथम्। सोऽहं हंसपदेनैव जीवो जपति सर्वदा॥२४९॥","i":"śvāsapraveśakāle tu dūto jalpati vāñchitam | tasyārthaḥ siddhim āyāti nirgame naiva sundari ||246|| lābhādīny api kāryāṇi pṛṣṭhāni kīrtitāni ca | jīve viśati sidhyanti hānir niḥsaraṇe bhavet ||247|| nare dakṣā svakīyā ca striyāṃ vāmā praśasyate | kumbhako yuddhakāle ca tisro nāḍyas trayīgatiḥ ||248|| hakārasya sakārasya vinā bhedaṃ svaraḥ katham | so 'haṃ haṃsapadenaiva jīvo japati sarvadā ||249||","f":"When a questioner speaks his purpose during inhalation, that purpose is fulfilled; during exhalation it is not. Matters of gain and the questions put succeed when the breath is entering, and fail when it is leaving. For a man the right channel is his own, for a woman the left; retention is appropriate in battle. Without the distinction between *ha* and *sa* there would be no breath: the living being recites *so'ham* and *haṃsa* continuously.","a":"Verses 246 and 247 make a claim about respiratory phase — that what occurs during inhalation succeeds and what occurs during exhalation does not — and this is the one place in the entire treatise where the text's phase-dependence thesis approaches a documented finding. The finding is Zelano and colleagues', discussed in the introduction and worth restating precisely here. Recording intracranially from patients with implanted electrodes, they found that neural oscillations in piriform cortex, amygdala and hippocampus are entrained to the respiratory cycle. They then tested whether this had behavioural consequences, and found that recognition of fearful faces was faster and memory recognition performance better when stimuli were encountered during *inspiration* than during expiration — and critically, that the effect was present during nasal breathing and abolished during oral breathing. **[E]** The direction matches. The text says the inhalation phase is the favourable one for the matter at hand; the experiment finds an inspiratory advantage for two specific cognitive operations. Several qualifications are essential and the match should not be overstated. The experimental effect concerns the observer's own perception and memory, not the outcome of external events; the text is making a claim about whether an enterprise succeeds. …","m":"**Use the breath you are already taking.** The *ajapā* framing yields the lowest-friction practice available: at any moment, without changing anything, notice the sound and sensation of the current breath. No posture, no duration, no preparation. Done at transitions — before opening a door, at a red light, when a page loads — it accumulates into a substantial daily total without a single minute set aside. This is habit-stacking onto an involuntary process, and it is more robust than habit-stacking onto a voluntary one, because the carrier behaviour cannot be skipped. **On respiratory phase.** The practical implication of the phase findings is modest and should be presented as such. There is reasonable evidence that some cognitive operations are performed marginally better during inspiration and that this depends on nasal breathing. **[E]** The actionable version is not to time your sentences to your breath, which is impractical, but to breathe through the nose, which preserves the entrainment that oral breathing abolishes. That is a real recommendation with a real mechanism, and it costs nothing. **Count your resting rate once.** Most people have never done it and most estimate it wrongly. Sit still for two minutes, then count breaths for sixty seconds without altering them — which is harder than it sounds, since attending to respiration changes it. …","g":["E"]},{"v":"250–261","t":"The Full Side, the Sites of Injury, and the Two Parties","p":"PART FOURTEEN: THE MILITARY APPLICATION","d":"शून्याङ्गं पूरितं कृत्वा जीवाङ्गे गोपयेज्जयम्। जीवाङ्गे घातमाप्नोति शून्याङ्गे रक्षते सदा॥२५०॥ भूतत्त्वेनोदरे घातः पदस्थानेऽम्बुना भवेत्। उरुस्थानेऽग्नितत्त्वेन करस्थाने च वायुना॥२५२॥ शिरसि व्योमतत्त्वे च ज्ञातव्यो घातनिर्णयः। एवं पञ्चविधो घातः स्वरशास्त्रे प्रकाशितः॥२५३॥ युद्धकाले यदा चन्द्रः स्थायी जयति निश्चितम्। यदा सूर्यप्रवाहस्तु यायी विजयते सदा॥२५४॥ जयमध्येऽपि सन्देहे नाडीमध्ये तु लक्षयेत्। सुषुम्नायां गते प्राणे समरे शत्रुसङ्कटम्॥२५५॥ यदि सङ्ग्रामकाले तु वामनाडी सदा वहेत्। स्थायिनो विजयं विद्याद्रिपुवश्यादयोऽपि च॥२५७॥ यदि सङ्ग्रामकाले च सूर्यस्तु व्यावृतो वहेत्। तदा यायी जयं विद्यात्सदेवासुरमानवे॥२५८॥ पूर्णनाडीगतः पृष्ठे शून्याङ्गं च तदाग्रतः। शून्यस्थाने कृतः शत्रुर्म्रियते नात्र संशयः॥२६१॥","i":"śūnyāṅgaṃ pūritaṃ kṛtvā jīvāṅge gopayej jayam | jīvāṅge ghātam āpnoti śūnyāṅge rakṣate sadā ||250|| bhūtattvenodare ghātaḥ padasthāne 'mbunā bhavet | urusthāne 'gnitattvena karasthāne ca vāyunā ||252|| śirasi vyomatattve ca jñātavyo ghātanirṇayaḥ | evaṃ pañcavidho ghātaḥ svaraśāstre prakāśitaḥ ||253|| yuddhakāle yadā candraḥ sthāyī jayati niścitam | yadā sūryapravāhas tu yāyī vijayate sadā ||254|| jayamadhye 'pi sandehe nāḍīmadhye tu lakṣayet | suṣumnāyāṃ gate prāṇe samare śatrusaṅkaṭam ||255|| yadi saṅgrāmakāle tu vāmanāḍī sadā vahet | sthāyino vijayaṃ vidyād ripuvaśyādayo 'pi ca ||257|| yadi saṅgrāmakāle ca sūryas tu vyāvṛto vahet | tadā yāyī jayaṃ vidyāt sadevāsuramānave ||258|| pūrṇanāḍīgataḥ pṛṣṭhe śūnyāṅgaṃ ca tadāgrataḥ | śūnyasthāne kṛtaḥ śatrur mriyate nātra saṃśayaḥ ||261||","f":"Having filled the empty side, guard victory on the living side; the blow falls on the living side, while the empty side is always protected. Injury occurs to the abdomen in the earth phase, to the feet in water, to the thighs in fire, to the hands in air, and to the head in the space phase — a fivefold determination. When the left channel flows at the time of battle, the defender wins; when the right flows, the attacker wins. Amid doubt, observe the middle channel: when the breath enters Suṣumnā, there is danger from the enemy. Keep the full channel to the rear and the empty to the front; an enemy placed in the empty position is destroyed.","a":"Two things in this block deserve extended treatment: a contradiction, and a scheme that is internally coherent. **The contradiction.** Verses 252 and 253 assign the five phases to five bodily regions where injury is said to occur: earth to the abdomen, water to the feet, fire to the thighs, air to the hands, space to the head. Compare verse 156, which assigned the same five phases to bodily locations: fire to the shoulders, air to the navel, earth to the knees, water to the feet, space to the head. Two of five agree — water at the feet, space at the head. Three of five contradict directly. Earth is at the knees in verse 156 and at the abdomen in verse 252. Fire is at the shoulders in one and the thighs in the other. Air is at the navel in one and the hands in the other. This is the second documented internal contradiction in the treatise, after the planetary schemes of verses 182 to 184, and it is more damaging than the first. The planetary assignments were an imported overlay that does no work in the text's own procedures. The body map is not an overlay: the text uses it, and here it uses two incompatible versions of it a hundred verses apart, in a section where the stakes are described as fatal. The pattern is exactly what was predicted at verses 169 to 173. …","m":"**The defender and the attacker are different jobs.** This is the transferable content and it applies well outside combat. *Holding* tasks — monitoring, waiting, maintaining a position, sustaining attention over hours, not acting prematurely — require moderate, sustainable arousal and fail through impatience. The interventions that help are those that support sustained vigilance: scheduled breaks, which restore performance more effectively than continued effort; avoiding stimulant loading, which raises arousal beyond the optimum for waiting; and downregulating deliberately when the pull to act arrives. **[E]** *Advancing* tasks — the confrontation, the pitch, the committed physical effort, the difficult call — require acute mobilization over a short window and fail through hesitation. Here the intervention is the opposite: brief physical activation immediately beforehand, and no attempt at calming. The common error is to apply one arousal strategy to a day that contains both. A person who begins the morning with stimulants and stays elevated is well configured for the advancing tasks and badly configured for the holding ones, and most work contains more of the latter. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"262–264","t":"Name Syllables and the Alliance Question","p":"PART FIFTEEN: THE CLOSE OF THE COMBAT SECTION","d":"वामाचारे समं नाम यस्य तस्य जयी भवेत्। पृच्छको दक्षिणभागे विजयी विषमाक्षरः॥२६२॥ यदा पृच्छति चन्द्रस्य तदा सन्धानमादिशेत्। पृच्छेद्यदा तु सूर्यस्य तदा जानीहि विग्रहम्॥२६३॥ पार्थिवे च समं युद्धं सिद्धिर्भवति वारुणे। युद्धे हि तेजसो भङ्गो मृत्युर्वायौ नभस्यपि॥२६४॥","i":"vāmācāre samaṃ nāma yasya tasya jayī bhavet | pṛcchako dakṣiṇabhāge vijayī viṣamākṣaraḥ ||262|| yadā pṛcchati candrasya tadā sandhānam ādiśet | pṛcched yadā tu sūryasya tadā jānīhi vigraham ||263|| pārthive ca samaṃ yuddhaṃ siddhir bhavati vāruṇe | yuddhe hi tejaso bhaṅgo mṛtyur vāyau nabhasy api ||264||","f":"During left-channel flow, the party whose name has an even number of syllables prevails; during right-channel flow, the one with an odd number. When the question is put during left-channel flow, declare that the matter will end in agreement; during right-channel flow, in conflict. In the earth phase the engagement is evenly matched, in water there is success, in fire defeat, and in air and space, death.","a":"Verse 262 introduces a device that has not appeared before: the parity of the syllable count in a name. Even syllables win under left flow, odd under right. This is worth noting because it marks the point at which the system has exhausted its own physiological variables and begun importing arbitrary ones. Nasal dominance is a real quantity with real variation. Syllable parity is a property of a word, and it has no relationship to anything the text has previously discussed. The mechanism generating this verse is the one identified at verses 182 to 184 — analogical extension into a domain with no constraint — and it is running here on linguistic material. There is a structural point about why parity in particular is chosen, and it applies to divinatory systems generally. Parity is the cheapest possible binary partition of an arbitrary set. It requires no judgement, produces a determinate answer for any input, and divides the space roughly in half. A system needing to generate binary predictions from names will converge on parity for the same reason coin-flipping converges on coins: it is the simplest available randomizer that looks like a procedure. **[X]** Verse 263 is more interesting because it makes a claim about *social* outcome rather than about victory. …","m":"**Read your own state before entering a negotiation, and treat it as a prediction about the negotiation's character rather than about its outcome.** The practical version: before any conversation where agreement is the goal, run the check from verse 97 — nasal patency, pulse, muscular tension, breath rate. If the reading is mobilized, the conversation will tend toward positional bargaining regardless of your intentions, because the prosodic and lexical consequences of arousal are not under voluntary control and the other party responds to them. The intervention is the one established at verses 42 to 45: two to three minutes of extended-exhalation breathing before beginning, which produces measurable autonomic change within that window. **[E]** This is not a technique for winning. It is a technique for ensuring that the conversation is about the substance rather than about the states of the two people having it. **On arbitrary decision procedures.** Verse 262's syllable-parity rule is worth recognizing as a type, because the type recurs in modern settings. Any procedure that generates a determinate answer from an input with no causal connection to the outcome is a randomizer wearing the costume of a method. The tell is that the input variable has no independent motivation — nobody proposed syllable parity as important before it was needed to produce an answer. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"265–266","t":"The Flower Test, and an Admission","p":"PART FIFTEEN: THE CLOSE OF THE COMBAT SECTION","d":"निमित्ततः प्रमादाद्वा यदा न ज्ञायतेऽनिलः। पृच्छाकाले तदा कुर्यादिदं यत्नेन बुद्धिमान्॥२६५॥ निश्चलं धरणं कृत्वा पुष्पं हस्तान्निपातयेत्। पूर्णाङ्गे पुष्पपतनं शून्यं वा तत्परं भवेत्॥२६६॥","i":"nimittataḥ pramādād vā yadā na jñāyate 'nilaḥ | pṛcchākāle tadā kuryād idaṃ yatnena buddhimān ||265|| niścalaṃ dharaṇaṃ kṛtvā puṣpaṃ hastān nipātayet | pūrṇāṅge puṣpapatanaṃ śūnyaṃ vā tat paraṃ bhavet ||266||","f":"When, through circumstance or inattention, the state of the breath cannot be determined, then at the time of questioning a competent person should carefully do the following: holding still, let a flower fall from the hand. Whether it falls to the full side or the empty is then decisive.","a":"These two verses are the most methodologically candid passage in the *Śiva Svarodaya*, and they undermine the treatise more thoroughly than any external criticism could. Verse 265 admits that the breath cannot always be read. The reasons given are *nimitta* — circumstance — and *pramāda*, inattention or carelessness. The text is acknowledging that its central measurement fails, sometimes for environmental reasons and sometimes because the observer is not attending well enough. That admission is creditable and it is accurate. Nasal dominance is genuinely ambiguous during changeover, genuinely absent in a substantial minority of individuals who show no clear cycling, and genuinely obscured by rhinitis, exertion, recent posture change and ambient conditions. **[E]** A practitioner attempting the reading under field conditions — before a battle, in a crowded room, in cold air — would fail often. The text knows this. Verse 266 gives the remedy, and the remedy is to drop a flower and see which side it falls on. Consider what this means for the system as a whole. The procedure being replaced is the reading of a real physiological variable with a claimed causal connection to outcome. The procedure replacing it is an explicit randomizer. And the text says the substitute is *param* — decisive, authoritative. No difference in evidential status is asserted between the two. …","m":"**Know which of your procedures require accuracy and which require only determinacy.** This is the practical extraction and it is genuinely useful. Some decisions need to be *right*: they have identifiable better and worse outcomes, and effort spent improving the answer pays. Others need only to be *made*: the options are close, the analysis has stalled, and the cost of continued deliberation exceeds the expected difference between the choices. Conflating these two is the source of a great deal of wasted effort, in both directions — agonizing over close calls, and deciding important things quickly because a procedure was available. The test is to ask what you would do differently if you knew the answer. If a decisive answer changes your action and the two options differ substantially in expected outcome, invest in getting it right. If the options are close, the deliberation has already run long, and the cost of delay is real, then determinacy is what you need, and a randomizer used knowingly is a legitimate instrument — as set out at verse 180, with attention to your own reaction to the result. **The honesty requirement.** What makes the flower test defensible as a decision aid and indefensible as presented is a single distinction: whether the user knows it is a randomizer. A person who flips a coin knows the coin contains no information and uses it accordingly. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"267–269","t":"Non-Disruption of Mind","p":"PART FIFTEEN: THE CLOSE OF THE COMBAT SECTION","d":"तिष्ठन्नुपविशंश्चापि प्राणमाकर्षयन्निजम्। मनोभङ्गमकुर्वाणः सर्वकार्येषु जीवति॥२६७॥ न कालो विविधं घोरं न शस्त्रं न च पन्नगः। न शत्रुव्याधिचौराद्याः शून्यस्थानाशितुं क्षमाः॥२६८॥ जीवेन स्थापयेद्वायुं जीवेनारम्भयेत्पुनः। जीवेन क्रीडते नित्यं द्यूते जयति सर्वदा॥२६९॥","i":"tiṣṭhann upaviśaṃś cāpi prāṇam ākarṣayan nijam | manobhaṅgam akurvāṇaḥ sarvakāryeṣu jīvati ||267|| na kālo vividhaṃ ghoraṃ na śastraṃ na ca pannagaḥ | na śatruvyādhicaurādyāḥ śūnyasthānāśituṃ kṣamāḥ ||268|| jīvena sthāpayed vāyuṃ jīvenārambhayet punaḥ | jīvena krīḍate nityaṃ dyūte jayati sarvadā ||269||","f":"Whether standing or seated, one who draws in the breath without allowing the mind to be broken survives in every undertaking. Neither Time in its many terrible forms, nor weapon, nor serpent, nor enemy, disease or thief is able to consume one who stands in the empty place. Establish the breath on the living side, begin again on the living side; one who plays always on that side wins at dice.","a":"Verse 267 contains a phrase that repays attention: *manobhaṅgam akurvāṇaḥ*, not causing a breaking of the mind. It is paired with a physical instruction — draw in the breath — and a posture-neutral qualifier: standing or sitting, it does not matter. *Manobhaṅga* is a compound of *manas* and *bhaṅga*, breaking or fracture. What is being described is not calm but *non-fragmentation*: the maintenance of an intact, continuous mental state under conditions that tend to shatter it. That is a different target from relaxation and a more accurate one for the circumstances the verse addresses. The relevant modern account concerns what happens to cognition under acute stress. The characteristic failure is not simply degraded performance but a specific pattern: attention narrows onto threat-relevant cues at the expense of peripheral information; working memory capacity drops; and control shifts from goal-directed to habitual systems. **[E]** The subjective experience corresponding to this is discontinuity — losing the thread, forgetting what one was doing, acting without deciding. *Bhaṅga* is a good description of it. The instruction pairs a physical act with the injunction to prevent this fragmentation, and the pairing is correct in structure. …","m":"**Train the anchor, not the calm.** The target under acute stress is continuity of function rather than absence of arousal, and the two are different. Arousal is appropriate and useful; fragmentation is not. The technique with the best support and the lowest requirements: at the onset of an acute demand, take three breaths with deliberately extended exhalation while continuing whatever you are doing. Not stopping, not sitting down, not closing the eyes — the point is that the anchor operates *inside* the situation. Combat and emergency services teach variants of this as tactical or box breathing, and while the outcome evidence in field conditions is thinner than the adoption suggests, the underlying autonomic effect is established. **[E for the physiology; S for field performance outcomes]** The marker to watch for is the one the verse names. Not \"am I calm\" — you will not be — but \"do I know what I am doing and what comes next.\" When the answer to that goes, the anchor is needed. **On chance.** Verse 269's claim requires a plain statement, and it is not merely a historical matter. No personal state, ritual, breathing pattern, posture, or system influences the outcome of a randomizing device. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"275–282","t":"The Subjugation Passage","p":"PART SIXTEEN: THE VAŚĪKARAṆA VERSES","d":"नरयुद्धं यमयुद्धं त्वया प्रोक्तं महेश्वर। इदानीं देव देवानां वशीकारणकं वद॥२७५॥ चन्द्रं सूर्येण चाकृष्य स्थापयेज्जीवमण्डले। आजन्मवशगा रामा कथितं यत्तपोधनैः॥२७६॥ जीवेन गृह्यते जीवो जीवो जीवस्य दीयते। जीवस्थाने गतो जीवो बाला जीवान्तकारकः॥२७७॥ रात्र्यन्तयामवेलायां प्रसुप्ते कामिनीजने। ब्रह्मजीवं पिबेद्यस्तु बालाप्राणहरो नरः॥२७८॥ शयने वा प्रसङ्गे वा युवत्यालिङ्गनेऽपि वा। यः सूर्येण पिबेच्चन्द्रं स भवेन्मकरध्वजः॥२८०॥ सप्त नव त्रयः पञ्च वारान्सङ्गस्तु सूर्यभे। चन्द्रे द्विचतुःषट्कृत्वा वश्या भवति कामिनी॥२८२॥","i":"narayuddhaṃ yamayuddhaṃ tvayā proktaṃ maheśvara | idānīṃ deva devānāṃ vaśīkāraṇakaṃ vada ||275|| candraṃ sūryeṇa cākṛṣya sthāpayej jīvamaṇḍale | ājanmavaśagā rāmā kathitaṃ yat tapodhanaiḥ ||276|| jīvena gṛhyate jīvo jīvo jīvasya dīyate | jīvasthāne gato jīvo bālā jīvāntakārakaḥ ||277|| rātryantayāmavelāyāṃ prasupte kāminījane | brahmajīvaṃ pibed yas tu bālāprāṇaharo naraḥ ||278|| śayane vā prasaṅge vā yuvatyāliṅgane 'pi vā | yaḥ sūryeṇa pibec candraṃ sa bhaven makaradhvajaḥ ||280|| sapta nava trayaḥ pañca vārān saṅgas tu sūryabhe | candre dvicatuḥṣaṭkṛtvā vaśyā bhavati kāminī ||282||","f":"Combat between men and combat with Death have been described; now describe the means of subjugation. Drawing the left channel by means of the right and establishing it in the vital sphere, a woman is brought under control from birth — so the ascetics have declared. The vital breath is seized by the vital breath, and given to it; the breath that goes to the other's place becomes the taker of the young woman's life. Whoever, in the last watch of the night, when the woman is deeply asleep, drinks her vital breath is a taker of her life-breath. Whoever, in bed or in intercourse or in embrace, draws in her left by his right becomes as the god of desire. Union during right-channel flow seven, nine, three or five times, and during left-channel flow twice, four or six times, brings a woman under control.","a":"These verses describe procedures for making another person compliant without her knowledge or consent. Verse 278 specifies that the procedure be performed on a woman while she is asleep. The stated aim is *vaśya* — the condition of being controlled — and the operator is described as *bālā-prāṇa-haraḥ*, one who takes away the life-breath of a young woman. The techniques do not work. There is no mechanism by which one person's respiratory phase affects another person's volition, and no such effect has ever been demonstrated. This section has no practical content whatever. What it does have is documentary content, and it is worth being precise about what it documents. First, it establishes that the *Śiva Svarodaya*'s framework was extended, by its own tradition, to non-consensual ends. This is relevant to how the text as a whole should be read. A body of practical knowledge is not a neutral instrument; the uses to which its own transmitters put it are evidence about the culture of its transmission. The same tradition that produced the accurate observation about sleep posture at verse 122 produced verse 278. Second, it illustrates a structural feature of magical thinking about other minds that has not diminished. The procedures described share a form: an operation performed by the actor, unobservable by the target, producing a change in the target's disposition. …","m":"Every other chapter in this book concludes with a section extracting whatever is usable from the verse. This one does not, and the reason should be stated rather than left implicit. The material in these verses has no valid application. The techniques are ineffective, so there is no mechanism to explain. The aim is the covert overriding of another person's consent, so there is no legitimate purpose to redirect them toward. And the only \"practical\" content that could be extracted — advice on influencing someone without their knowledge — is precisely the content that should not be extracted. Presenting this section without an application section is therefore not squeamishness or an omission. It is the correct output of the same procedure applied everywhere else in this book: assess what is real, assess what is useful, and report both honestly. Here the answer to both questions is nothing. What can be said in place of an application is this. The historical study of coercive practices has value — it tells us what people attempted, what they believed about one another, and how a knowledge system's authority gets extended into domains where it does harm. That value is served by translating the verses accurately and saying plainly what they are. It is not served by looking for a usable kernel where none exists.","g":[]},{"v":"286–292","t":"Conception Timing","p":"PART SEVENTEEN: CONCEPTION AND SEX PREDICTION","d":"ऋतुस्नाता रता नारी पञ्चमेऽह्नि यदा भवेत्। सूर्यचन्द्रमसोर्योगे सेवनात्पुत्रसम्भवः॥२८६॥ ऋतुस्नाता पिबेन्नारी ऋतुदानं तु योजयेत्। रूपलावण्यसम्पन्नो नरसिंहः प्रसूयते॥२८८॥ सुषुम्ना सूर्यवाहेन ऋतुदानं तु योजयेत्। अङ्गहीनः पुमान्यस्तु जायतेऽत्र कुविग्रहः॥२८९॥ ऋत्वारम्भे रविः पुंसां स्त्रीणां च सुधाकरः। उभयोः सङ्गमे प्राप्ते वन्ध्या पुत्रमवाप्नुयात्॥२९१॥","i":"ṛtusnātā ratā nārī pañcame 'hni yadā bhavet | sūryacandramasor yoge sevanāt putrasambhavaḥ ||286|| ṛtusnātā piben nārī ṛtudānaṃ tu yojayet | rūpalāvaṇyasampanno narasiṃhaḥ prasūyate ||288|| suṣumnā sūryavāhena ṛtudānaṃ tu yojayet | aṅgahīnaḥ pumān yas tu jāyate 'tra kuvigrahaḥ ||289|| ṛtvārambhe raviḥ puṃsāṃ strīṇāṃ ca sudhākaraḥ | ubhayoḥ saṅgame prāpte vandhyā putram avāpnuyāt ||291||","f":"When a woman, having bathed after menstruation, unites on the fifth day under the conjunction of the channels, a son results. If union is arranged after the prescribed preparation, a child of fine form is born. If union occurs while Suṣumnā flows alongside the right channel, a malformed child results. At the onset of the cycle the right channel is prescribed for the man and the left for the woman; when both are so aligned, even a woman thought barren conceives a son.","a":"The claims here divide into three kinds, and they have different evidential statuses. **The timing claim is partially correct.** Verse 286 specifies conception on the fifth day after the end of menstruation. In a woman with a cycle of average length, this falls in the follicular phase approaching ovulation, and intercourse in the days preceding ovulation is when conception probability is highest — the fertile window spans approximately the five days before ovulation and the day of ovulation itself, with probability peaking in the two days before. **[E]** The traditional prescription lands in roughly the right region of the cycle for a woman with an average cycle length, which is not a coincidence: any tradition attending to conception outcomes would eventually locate the fertile window empirically. The qualification matters, however. Cycle length varies substantially between and within women, and the day of ovulation is correspondingly variable; fixed-day rules perform poorly for anyone whose cycle departs from the average, which is a large proportion of women. **[E]** This is why calendar-based fertility methods have higher failure rates than methods based on direct indicators such as basal body temperature or cervical mucus observation. …","m":"The one usable element is the timing observation, appropriately corrected. **For couples trying to conceive:** the fertile window is the five days preceding ovulation plus the day of ovulation, with the highest probability in the two days before. Intercourse every one to two days across that window is the standard recommendation, and it does not require precise identification of the ovulation day. **[E]** Ovulation can be tracked with urinary LH tests, basal body temperature charting, or cervical mucus observation, all of which are more reliable than a fixed calendar day. **Nothing else in this section applies.** Not the respiratory timing, not the offspring-quality claims, not the infertility remedy. **When to seek assessment:** twelve months of unprotected intercourse without conception, or six months if the woman is thirty-five or older, or sooner where there is known irregularity of cycle, previous pelvic surgery or infection, or known male factor risk. Assessment should include both partners from the outset.","g":["E"]},{"v":"293–299","t":"Sex Prediction","p":"PART SEVENTEEN: CONCEPTION AND SEX PREDICTION","d":"चन्द्रनाडी यदा प्रश्ने गर्भे कन्या तदा भवेत्। सूर्यो भवेत्तदा पुत्रो द्वयोर्गर्भो विहन्यते॥२९३॥ पृथिव्यां पुत्री जले पुत्रः कन्यका तु प्रभञ्जने। तेजसि गर्भपातः स्यान्नभस्यपि नपुंसकः॥२९४॥ चन्द्रे स्त्री पुरुषः सूर्ये मध्यमार्गे नपुंसकः। गर्भप्रश्ने यदा दूतः पूर्णे पुत्रः प्रजायते॥२९५॥ महीन्द्रे सुसुतोत्पत्तिर्वारुणे दुहिता भवेत्। शेषेषु गर्भहानिः स्याज्जातमात्रस्य वा मृतिः॥२९९॥","i":"candranāḍī yadā praśne garbhe kanyā tadā bhavet | sūryo bhavet tadā putro dvayor garbho vihanyate ||293|| pṛthivyāṃ putrī jale putraḥ kanyakā tu prabhañjane | tejasi garbhapātaḥ syān nabhasy api napuṃsakaḥ ||294|| candre strī puruṣaḥ sūrye madhyamārge napuṃsakaḥ | garbhapraśne yadā dūtaḥ pūrṇe putraḥ prajāyate ||295|| mahīndre susutotpattir vāruṇe duhitā bhavet | śeṣeṣu garbhahāniḥ syāj jātamātrasya vā mṛtiḥ ||299||","f":"When the left channel flows at the time of the question, the child will be a girl; when the right, a boy; when both, the pregnancy will be lost. In the earth phase a daughter, in water a son, in air a girl, in fire a miscarriage, in space a child of neither sex. In the earth phase a fine son is born, in water a daughter, and in the remaining phases the pregnancy is lost or the child dies at birth.","a":"These verses are false, and the falsity is not a matter of interpretation. They are also internally inconsistent, and the inconsistency is worth documenting because it is the third such case in the treatise. **The biology.** Fetal sex is determined at fertilization by whether the fertilizing spermatozoon carries an X or a Y chromosome. The determination is complete before implantation and cannot be influenced by anything either parent does subsequently. **[E]** No respiratory state, dietary regimen, positional practice, timing scheme or ritual affects it. **The internal contradiction.** Compare the assignments across three adjacent verses. Verse 293: left channel → girl; right channel → boy. Verse 294: earth → daughter; water → son; air → girl. Verse 299: earth → son; water → daughter. Verses 294 and 299 assign *opposite* sexes to both the earth and water phases, six verses apart. This is the same failure documented at verses 182 to 184 for the planetary schemes and at verses 156 and 252 for the body map, and it is again the signature of content that was never checked. On a binary question with a fifty per cent base rate, a scheme that contradicts itself will be right half the time whichever version is used, which is precisely why the contradiction could persist unnoticed. **[X, but the textual evidence is direct]** There is a further structural point. …","m":"As with Part Sixteen, no practical extraction follows, and the reason is the same in structure and different in emphasis. The prediction scheme carries no information. There is nothing to correct or reinterpret, because the underlying quantity — fetal sex — is fixed at fertilization and unrelated to anything the scheme measures. More importantly, an application section here would be actively harmful in a way that the ineffectiveness alone does not capture. Any framing that treats fetal sex as a *desirable outcome subject to influence* participates in the preference structure, regardless of whether the method offered works. The correct response to a false technique in service of a harmful preference is not a better technique. What can be said instead: a child's sex is determined at conception, is not influenced by anything the parents do, and is not a legitimate basis for the valuation of a pregnancy. Prenatal sex determination for selection purposes is illegal in India and in a number of other jurisdictions, and the laws exist for well-documented reasons.","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"301–312","t":"Rainfall, Harvest and the Public Forecast","p":"PART EIGHTEEN: THE YEAR'S FORECAST","d":"चैत्रशुक्लप्रतिपदि प्रातस्तत्त्वविभेदतः। पश्येद्विचक्षणो योगी दक्षिणे चोत्तरायणे॥३०१॥ मेषसंक्रान्तिवेलायां स्वरभेदं विचारयेत्। संवत्सरफलं ब्रूयाल्लोकानां तत्त्वचिन्तकः॥३०५॥ सुभिक्षं राष्ट्रवृद्धिः स्याद्बहुशस्या वसुन्धरा। बहुवृष्टिस्तथा सौख्यं पृथ्वीतत्त्वं वहेद्यदि॥३०७॥ अतिवृष्टिः सुभिक्षं स्यादारोग्यं सौख्यमेव च। बहुशस्या तथा पृथ्वी आपस्तत्त्वं वहेद्यदि॥३०८॥ दुर्भिक्षं राष्ट्रभङ्गः स्यादुत्पत्तिश्च विनश्यति। अल्पादल्पतरा वृष्टिरग्नितत्त्वं वहेद्यदि॥३०९॥ मेषसंक्रान्तिवेलायां व्योमतत्त्वं वहेद्यदि। तत्रापि शून्यता ज्ञेया स्याद्धान्यादीनां सुखस्य च॥३११॥ पूर्णप्रवेशने श्वासे शस्यं तत्त्वेन सिध्यति। सूर्यचन्द्रेऽन्यथाभूते सङ्ग्रहः सर्वसिद्धिदः॥३१२॥","i":"caitraśuklapratipadi prātas tattvavibhedataḥ | paśyed vicakṣaṇo yogī dakṣiṇe cottarāyaṇe ||301|| meṣasaṃkrāntivelāyāṃ svarabhedaṃ vicārayet | saṃvatsaraphalaṃ brūyāl lokānāṃ tattvacintakaḥ ||305|| subhikṣaṃ rāṣṭravṛddhiḥ syād bahuśasyā vasundharā | bahuvṛṣṭis tathā saukhyaṃ pṛthvītattvaṃ vahed yadi ||307|| ativṛṣṭiḥ subhikṣaṃ syād ārogyaṃ saukhyam eva ca | bahuśasyā tathā pṛthvī āpas tattvaṃ vahed yadi ||308|| durbhikṣaṃ rāṣṭrabhaṅgaḥ syād utpattiś ca vinaśyati | alpād alpatarā vṛṣṭir agnitattvaṃ vahed yadi ||309|| meṣasaṃkrāntivelāyāṃ vyomatattvaṃ vahed yadi | tatrāpi śūnyatā jñeyā syād dhānyādīnāṃ sukhasya ca ||311|| pūrṇapraveśane śvāse śasyaṃ tattvena sidhyati | sūryacandre 'nyathābhūte saṅgrahaḥ sarvasiddhidaḥ ||312||","f":"At dawn on the first day of the bright fortnight of Caitra, and at the sun's entry into Aries, the discerning practitioner should examine the state of the breath and declare the year's outcome for the people. If the earth phase flows, there will be plenty, prosperity, abundant crops, good rain and general wellbeing. If water, abundant rain, plenty, health and wellbeing. If fire, famine, disorder, the failure of produce and rain less than little. If the space phase flows, there will be emptiness of grain and of comfort. When the breath enters on the full side, the harvest succeeds; when conditions are otherwise, the storing of grain is what succeeds.","a":"This is meteorological forecasting from a physiological variable, and it has no predictive value. Rainfall over the Indian subcontinent is governed by the monsoon system — the seasonal reversal of winds driven by differential heating of land and ocean, modulated on interannual timescales by sea surface temperature patterns including the El Niño–Southern Oscillation and the Indian Ocean Dipole. **[E]** None of these is detectable at a human nostril. The section is nevertheless worth analysing, because it differs from the rest of the text in a way that has consequences. Everything examined so far concerned the practitioner or an individual client. This concerns the *public*: verse 305 specifies that the forecast be declared *lokānām*, for the people. The practitioner is functioning as a state or community forecaster, and the forecast concerns whether there will be famine. Two things follow. The first is about incentives, and it is visible in verse 312. The verse says that when conditions are unfavourable, *saṅgraha* — storing, accumulating grain — is what succeeds. This is a hedge, and it is a hedge with a specific structure: whatever the forecast, there is an action that will appear to have been correct. Good year, plant; bad year, store. …","m":"**Evaluate any forecast against its base rate, not against its hit rate.** This is the practical extraction and it applies to weather, markets, business projections, medical prognosis and every other domain where predictions are made and remembered. The questions that determine whether a forecaster has skill: *What is the base rate?* If the event happens seventy per cent of the time, predicting it always yields seventy per cent accuracy and zero skill. *Was the prediction recorded before the outcome?* If not, no evaluation is possible, for the reasons set out at verse 207. *Was it probabilistic?* A forecaster who says \"it will rain\" cannot be scored properly; one who says \"seventy per cent chance of rain\" can be, over many forecasts. Probabilistic forecasts are scorable and categorical ones are not, which is why the fields with genuine forecasting skill have all moved to probabilities. **[E]** *Were the misses counted?* This is the question that eliminates almost every traditional and informal forecasting claim. **On hedged advice.** Watch for the structure in verse 312: a prediction accompanied by advice that is sound regardless of the prediction. When advice is good on its own merits, its success is not evidence for the framework that delivered it. Separate the two before crediting the framework — grain storage is prudent whatever the nostril says. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"316–325","t":"Will the Patient Live","p":"PART NINETEEN: THE BEDSIDE INTERROGATION","d":"आदौ शून्यागतो दूतः पश्चात्पूर्णे विशेद्यदि। मूर्च्छितोऽपि ध्रुवं जीवेदद्यर्थं प्रतिपृच्छति॥३१६॥ यस्मिन्नङ्गे स्थितो जीवस्तत्रस्थः परिपृच्छति। तदा जीवति जीवोऽसौ यदि रोगैरुपद्रुतः॥३१७॥ प्रश्ने चाधःस्थितो जीवो नूनं जीवो हि जीवति। ऊर्ध्वचारस्थितो जीवो जीवो याति यमालयम्॥३२१॥ चन्द्रस्थाने स्थितो जीवः सूर्यस्थाने तु पृच्छकः। तदा प्राणवियुक्तोऽसौ यदि वैद्यशतैर्वृतः॥३२३॥ एकस्य भूतस्य विपर्ययेण रोगाभिभूतिर्भवतीह पुंसाम्। तयोर्द्वयोर्बन्धुसुहृद्विपत्तिः पक्षद्वये व्यत्ययतो मृतिः स्यात्॥३२५॥","i":"ādau śūnyāgato dūtaḥ paścāt pūrṇe viśed yadi | mūrcchito 'pi dhruvaṃ jīved adyarthaṃ pratipṛcchati ||316|| yasminn aṅge sthito jīvas tatrasthaḥ paripṛcchati | tadā jīvati jīvo 'sau yadi rogair upadrutaḥ ||317|| praśne cādhaḥsthito jīvo nūnaṃ jīvo hi jīvati | ūrdhvacārasthito jīvo jīvo yāti yamālayam ||321|| candrasthāne sthito jīvaḥ sūryasthāne tu pṛcchakaḥ | tadā prāṇaviyukto 'sau yadi vaidyaśatair vṛtaḥ ||323|| ekasya bhūtasya viparyayeṇa rogābhibhūtir bhavatīha puṃsām | tayor dvayor bandhusuhṛdvipattiḥ pakṣadvaye vyatyayato mṛtiḥ syāt ||325||","f":"If the enquirer first approaches on the empty side and then moves to the full, the patient will certainly live, even if unconscious. If he stands on the side where the breath is flowing, the patient lives even if severely afflicted. If the breath is directed downward at the time of the question, the patient lives; if upward, he goes to the abode of Death. If the breath is in the left channel while the enquirer stands on the right, the patient will die though surrounded by a hundred physicians. By the reversal of one element, disease overcomes a person; by the reversal of two, calamity befalls his kin; by reversal in both fortnights, death follows.","a":"Verse 323 contains the phrase that determines how this section must be read: *yadi vaidya-śatair vṛtaḥ* — even if surrounded by a hundred physicians. The prognosis is asserted to override medical intervention. This is not a neutral claim, and its consequences are not confined to the fifteenth century. A prognosis of certain death, delivered with authority, has effects on the patient, on the family, and on the care that follows. **On the patient.** The nocebo effect is real and reasonably well documented: negative expectation produces measurable symptom worsening and physiological change, through pathways involving cholecystokinin, anxiety-mediated amplification of symptom perception, and altered pain processing. **[E]** Expectation of poor outcome is associated with worse outcome across several clinical contexts, including post-surgical recovery and treatment response. **[E]** The magnitude of these effects should not be overstated — expectation does not determine the course of serious organic disease — but it is not zero, and it operates in the direction the pronouncement specifies. **On the family.** A confident prognosis of death alters the decisions made by those caring for the patient. Care may be withdrawn or not sought; interventions may not be pursued; the patient may be moved or left. …","m":"**Do not deliver confident prognoses you cannot support.** The rule stated at verse 186 applies with greater force here, because the setting is illness and the audience is a frightened family. The specific harms are three: the patient's expectation, the family's decisions, and the effort that is or is not expended. All three run in the direction of the prediction, which is what makes confident prognostication from an invalid method more dangerous than mere uselessness. **What to say instead.** When someone asks whether a sick person will recover and you do not know — which is the usual case, including for clinicians — the honest answer has a structure: what is known, what is not known, what would change the picture, and what can be done now. This is more useful than a verdict, and it is also what the questioner generally needs, since the underlying request is usually for orientation rather than for a prediction. **On dose-response.** The one transferable idea from verse 325 is that severity and duration of derangement matter more than its presence. Applied to self-monitoring: a single abnormal reading carries little information; a reading that is abnormal, large in magnitude, and sustained over time carries considerably more. …","g":["E"]},{"v":"326–334","t":"Loss of Alternation and the Reckoning of Time","p":"PART TWENTY: THE CYCLE THAT STOPS","d":"मासादौ चैव पक्षादौ वासरादौ यथाक्रमम्। क्षयकालं परीक्षेत वायुचारवशात्सुधीः॥३२६॥ पञ्चभूतात्मकं दीपं शिवस्नेहेन सिञ्चितम्। रक्षयेत्सूर्यवातेन प्राणी जीवः स्थिरो भवेत्॥३२७॥ अहोरात्रं यदैकत्र वहते यस्य मारुतः। तदा तस्य भवेन्मृत्युः सम्पूर्णे वत्सरत्रये॥३३१॥ अहोरात्रद्वयं यस्य पिङ्गलायां सदा गतिः। तस्य वर्षद्वयं प्रोक्तं जीवितं तत्त्ववेदिभिः॥३३२॥ त्रिरात्रं वहते यस्य वायुरेकपुटे स्थितः। तदा संवत्सरायुस्तं प्रवदन्ति मनीषिणः॥३३३॥ रात्रौ चन्द्रो दिवा सूर्यो वहेद्यस्य निरन्तरम्। जानीयात्तस्य वै मृत्युः षण्मासाभ्यन्तरे भवेत्॥३३४॥","i":"māsādau caiva pakṣādau vāsarādau yathākramam | kṣayakālaṃ parīkṣeta vāyucāravaśāt sudhīḥ ||326|| pañcabhūtātmakaṃ dīpaṃ śivasnehena siñcitam | rakṣayet sūryavātena prāṇī jīvaḥ sthiro bhavet ||327|| ahorātraṃ yadaikatra vahate yasya mārutaḥ | tadā tasya bhaven mṛtyuḥ sampūrṇe vatsaratraye ||331|| ahorātradvayaṃ yasya piṅgalāyāṃ sadā gatiḥ | tasya varṣadvayaṃ proktaṃ jīvitaṃ tattvavedibhiḥ ||332|| trirātraṃ vahate yasya vāyur ekapuṭe sthitaḥ | tadā saṃvatsarāyus taṃ pravadanti manīṣiṇaḥ ||333|| rātrau candro divā sūryo vahed yasya nirantaram | jānīyāt tasya vai mṛtyuḥ ṣaṇmāsābhyantare bhavet ||334||","f":"At the beginning of the month, the fortnight and the day, the discerning should examine the time of decline by the movement of the breath. Guarding the lamp of the five elements, the living being remains stable. When the breath flows on one side for a full day and night, death follows at the completion of three years. When it remains in the right channel for two days and nights, two years of life are declared. When it remains in one nostril for three nights, one year. When the left flows continuously at night and the right by day without interruption, death follows within six months.","a":"These verses must be dealt with in two separate movements: what is wrong with them, and what they may be pointing at. Both are necessary, and the order matters. **What is wrong.** The claims are not valid and must not be acted upon. Persistent unilateral nasal obstruction is common and has ordinary causes. A deviated nasal septum, present in a substantial proportion of the population, produces fixed asymmetry that abolishes or masks the normal cycle. Nasal polyps, chronic rhinosinusitis, allergic rhinitis, turbinate hypertrophy and prior trauma all do the same. **[E]** None of these predicts death at any interval. Several are treatable, and persistent one-sided nasal obstruction is a reason to see an otolaryngologist — where the differential includes conditions requiring prompt attention — not a reason to count months. Further, a substantial minority of healthy people do not display a clear nasal cycle at all. Studies of nasal cycling in healthy adults consistently find that classical regular alternation is present in some but by no means all subjects, with reported prevalence varying widely by method and criteria. **[E]** On the text's scheme, a large fraction of the healthy population would be given a terminal prognosis. **Why this matters more than ordinary error.** A specific prediction of death, believed, is not inert. …","m":"**Do not use these verses for prognosis. There is nothing to salvage in the specific claims.** **If your nasal dominance does not alternate:** the overwhelmingly likely explanations are anatomical or inflammatory. Persistent one-sided obstruction warrants an ENT assessment — septal deviation, polyps and chronic rhinosinusitis are common, diagnosable and treatable, and the differential also includes less common conditions for which early assessment matters. This is a reason to make an appointment, not to count months. **If you find yourself checking anxiously:** that is the signal to act on. Repeated bodily checking in search of a feared sign is the maintaining mechanism of health anxiety, not a way of resolving it, and the checking reliably increases rather than reduces the fear. **[E]** If reading this section has produced that pattern, the appropriate response is to stop the checking behaviour and, if it persists, to discuss it with a clinician — health anxiety is common and responds well to treatment. **The transferable principle, stated properly.** Variability in a physiological rhythm is generally a marker of health, and its loss is generally a marker of derangement. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"337–345","t":"The Signs","p":"PART TWENTY-ONE: THE ARIṢṬA SIGNS","d":"अकस्माच्चित्तविकृतिरकस्मात्पुरुषोत्तमः। अकस्मादिन्द्रियोत्पातः सन्निपाताग्रलक्षणम्॥३३७॥ शरीरं शीतलं यस्य प्रकृतिर्विकृता भवेत्। तदरिष्टं समासेन व्यासतस्तु निबोध मे॥३३८॥ हुंकारः शीतलो यस्य फुत्कारो वह्निसन्निभः। महावैद्यो भवेद्यस्य तस्य मृत्युर्भवेद्ध्रुवम्॥३४०॥ अरश्मिबिम्बं सूर्यस्य वह्नेः शीतांशुमालिनः। दृष्ट्वैकादशमासायुर्नरश्चोर्ध्वं न जीवति॥३४२॥ क्वचित्पश्यति यो दीपं सुवर्णं श्याममेव वा। विरूपाणि च भूतानि नवमासान्न जीवति॥३४४॥","i":"akasmāc cittavikṛtir akasmāt puruṣottamaḥ | akasmād indriyotpātaḥ sannipātāgralakṣaṇam ||337|| śarīraṃ śītalaṃ yasya prakṛtir vikṛtā bhavet | tad ariṣṭaṃ samāsena vyāsatas tu nibodha me ||338|| huṃkāraḥ śītalo yasya phutkāro vahnisannibhaḥ | mahāvaidyo bhaved yasya tasya mṛtyur bhaved dhruvam ||340|| araśmibimbaṃ sūryasya vahneḥ śītāṃśumālinaḥ | dṛṣṭvaikādaśamāsāyur naraś cordhvaṃ na jīvati ||342|| kvacit paśyati yo dīpaṃ suvarṇaṃ śyāmam eva vā | virūpāṇi ca bhūtāni navamāsān na jīvati ||344||","f":"Sudden derangement of mind, sudden alteration of the person, sudden upheaval of the senses — these are the leading signs of systemic collapse. One whose body is cold and whose nature is altered shows a fatal sign. One whose exhaled breath is cold while the forced breath is hot, and who requires a great physician, will certainly die. One who sees the sun's disc without rays, or a halo about fire or the moon, has eleven months. One who sees a lamp as golden or dark, or sees misshapen forms, will not live nine months. One who, directed to look, cannot clearly see the pole star, the Milky Way, or Arundhatī has a year. One whose body suddenly wastes or swells, whose complexion suddenly changes, or whose character abruptly reverses, has eight months.","a":"The intervals are false. No sign predicts death at nine, ten or eleven months, and the numbering is schematic in the manner identified at verse 197 — a descending series of round figures, which is what an imposed scheme looks like rather than a measurement. The *signs themselves* are a different matter, and several of them are genuine clinical red flags of a quality that deserves explicit acknowledgement. This is the strongest clinical material in the treatise. **Verse 337: sudden change in mental state.** *Akasmāt citta-vikṛti* — sudden derangement of mind — is named as the foremost sign. This is correct, and it is arguably the single most important clinical observation in the entire text. Acute change in mental status is the cardinal diagnostic feature of delirium, and delirium is not a benign accompaniment of illness: it is independently associated with substantially increased mortality, longer hospital stay, and persistent cognitive decline, and it is frequently the presenting sign of sepsis, hypoxia, metabolic derangement, stroke, or drug toxicity in older adults. **[E]** The emphasis on *akasmāt*, suddenness, is precisely right: what distinguishes delirium from dementia is acute onset and fluctuating course. …","m":"**Treat these as red flags requiring assessment, never as prognoses.** That single substitution converts the section from a fatalistic timetable into a clinically useful list. The signs that warrant prompt medical attention: *Sudden change in mental state* — confusion, disorientation, altered alertness, or a marked change in behaviour developing over hours to days, particularly in an older person or anyone acutely unwell. This is the most important item on the list. It should be treated as urgent, because delirium is frequently the first sign of a serious underlying process, and because it is frequently missed. **[E]** *Cold, clammy skin with confusion or drowsiness* — assess urgently. This can indicate shock or sepsis. *New haloes around lights, particularly with eye pain, redness, headache or nausea* — this can indicate acute angle-closure glaucoma, which is an emergency and can cause permanent vision loss within hours. Same-day assessment. **[E]** *New visual hallucinations* — assessment, with the understanding that formed visual hallucinations in someone with intact insight and failing vision are often Charles Bonnet syndrome, which is benign, while their appearance in acute illness suggests delirium. *Unexplained weight loss* — a loss of more than five per cent of body weight over six to twelve months without intention warrants investigation. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"348–350, 363–364","t":"The Monthly Series","p":"PART TWENTY-TWO: THE COUNTDOWN","d":"न यस्य स्मरणं किञ्चिद्विद्यते स्तनचर्मणि। सोऽवश्यं पञ्चमे मासे स्कन्धारूढो भविष्यति॥३४८॥ यस्य न स्फुरति ज्योतिः पीड्यते नयनद्वयम्। मरणं तस्य निर्दिष्टं चतुर्थे मासे निश्चितम्॥३४९॥ सम्पूर्णं वहते सूर्यश्चन्द्रमा नैव दृश्यते। पक्षेण जायते मृत्युः कालज्ञेनानुभाषितम्॥३६३॥ मूत्रं पुरीषं वायुश्च समकालं प्रवर्तते। तदासौ चलितो ज्ञेयो दशाह्ने म्रियते ध्रुवम्॥३६४॥","i":"na yasya smaraṇaṃ kiñcid vidyate stanacarmaṇi | so 'vaśyaṃ pañcame māse skandhārūḍho bhaviṣyati ||348|| yasya na sphurati jyotiḥ pīḍyate nayanadvayam | maraṇaṃ tasya nirdiṣṭaṃ caturthe māse niścitam ||349|| sampūrṇaṃ vahate sūryaś candramā naiva dṛśyate | pakṣeṇa jāyate mṛtyuḥ kālajñenānubhāṣitam ||363|| mūtraṃ purīṣaṃ vāyuś ca samakālaṃ pravartate | tadāsau calito jñeyo daśāhne mriyate dhruvam ||364||","f":"In whom no sensation remains in the skin of the chest, that person will be carried out within five months. In whom no flash of light appears when the eyes are pressed, death is declared within four months. When the right channel flows continuously and the left is not seen at all, death follows within a fortnight. When urine, faeces and wind pass simultaneously, the person is departing and dies within ten days.","a":"The intervals are false and the schematic descent — seven, six, five, four, three months — is an imposed series of the kind identified at verse 197. Two of the four signs, however, describe real clinical findings, and one of them is a genuine terminal sign. **Verse 348: loss of sensation over the chest.** Segmental sensory loss over the trunk is a neurological finding, and a well-demarcated sensory level on the trunk is one of the classic signs of a spinal cord lesion — compression, transverse myelitis, or a mass. **[E]** Loss of sensation in a dermatomal or patchy distribution also occurs in peripheral neuropathies, and in leprosy, which was endemic in the region and in which anaesthetic skin patches are the cardinal early sign. **[E]** Whichever the tradition was observing, this is a real neurological finding requiring assessment, and it is a finding a physician would elicit by exactly the method implied: testing whether sensation is present. **Verse 349: absence of pressure phosphenes.** This is the more striking item. Pressing on the closed eye produces a phosphene — a perceived flash or pattern of light — through mechanical deformation of the retina, which depolarizes photoreceptors and ganglion cells in the absence of any light. **[E]** The perception requires an intact retina, an intact optic nerve, and functioning visual cortex. …","m":"**Three of these are genuine red flags. None is a prognosis.** *New loss of sensation over an area of skin* — particularly if it forms a level on the trunk, follows a dermatomal pattern, or is accompanied by weakness — requires assessment. A sensory level on the trunk with any limb weakness is an emergency. *New combined bladder and bowel incontinence* — this is the most urgent item in this volume. Combined with back pain, leg weakness, or numbness in the saddle region, it should be treated as a possible cauda equina syndrome and assessed the same day, in an emergency department. Delay costs function permanently. **[E]** *Loss of the pressure phosphene in one eye*, or any sudden change in vision, warrants ophthalmological assessment. Sudden painless vision loss has a differential including retinal detachment and retinal artery occlusion, both of which are time-critical. **On the structure of the countdown.** The descending series across verses 346 to 350 and 362 to 369 is worth recognizing as a form. Signs are arranged by how grave they appear and assigned progressively shorter intervals; the arrangement is by *severity of the sign*, not by measured survival. This is intuitively reasonable and formally invalid, because the relationship between a sign's dramatic quality and its prognostic weight is not close. …","g":["E","X"]},{"v":"366–369","t":"The Star Test, Internalized","p":"PART TWENTY-TWO: THE COUNTDOWN","d":"अरुन्धतीं ध्रुवं चैव विष्णोस्त्रीणि पदानि च। आयुर्हीना न पश्यन्ति चतुर्थं मातृमण्डलम्॥३६६॥ अरुन्धती भवेज्जिह्वा ध्रुवो नासाग्रमेव च। भ्रुवौ विष्णुपदं ज्ञेयं तारकं मातृमण्डलम्॥३६७॥ नव भ्रुवं सप्त घोषं पञ्च तारां त्रिनासिकाम्। जिह्वामेकदिनं प्रोक्तं म्रियते मानवो ध्रुवम्॥३६८॥ कोणावक्ष्णोरङ्गुलिभ्यां किञ्चित्पीड्य निरीक्षयेत्। यदा न दृश्यते बिन्दुर्दशाहेन भवेन्मृतिः॥३६९॥","i":"arundhatīṃ dhruvaṃ caiva viṣṇos trīṇi padāni ca | āyurhīnā na paśyanti caturthaṃ mātṛmaṇḍalam ||366|| arundhatī bhavej jihvā dhruvo nāsāgram eva ca | bhruvau viṣṇupadaṃ jñeyaṃ tārakaṃ mātṛmaṇḍalam ||367|| nava bhruvaṃ sapta ghoṣaṃ pañca tārāṃ trināsikām | jihvām ekadinaṃ proktaṃ mriyate mānavo dhruvam ||368|| koṇāv akṣṇor aṅgulibhyāṃ kiñcit pīḍya nirīkṣayet | yadā na dṛśyate bindur daśāhena bhaven mṛtiḥ ||369||","f":"Those whose lifespan is short cannot see Arundhatī, the pole star, the three steps of Viṣṇu, or fourthly the Mother-circle. Understand that Arundhatī is the tongue, the pole star the tip of the nose, Viṣṇu's step the eyebrows, and the Mother-circle the pupil. Nine days remain for one who cannot see the eyebrow, seven for the sound, five for the pupil, three for the nose and one for the tongue. Pressing lightly at the corners of the eyes, observe: when no point of light appears, death follows within ten days.","a":"Verse 367 performs an operation that has appeared before in this text and is worth naming: it *internalizes* an external test. The astronomical targets of verse 366 — Arundhatī, Dhruva, Viṣṇu's steps, the Mother-circle — are reassigned to parts of the observer's own face. Arundhatī becomes the tongue, Dhruva the nose-tip, and so on. This is the same move made at verses 137 and 138, where *veda* and *sandhyā* were detached from their external referents and reassigned to internal events. The pattern is consistent across the treatise and it is one of its defining habits: whatever the tradition inherits as an outward observance, this text relocates inward. The relocation has a consequence here that the text does not seem to register. The astronomical version, analysed at verse 341, was a *scotopic acuity test* with real diagnostic content — the ability to resolve Alcor tests low-light vision, and loss of low-light vision is the earliest sign of vitamin A deficiency, which carries genuine mortality risk in deficient populations. **[E]** The internalized version is something else entirely: the ability to see one's own nose-tip, eyebrows and tongue. Some of these have content and some do not. Seeing one's own nose-tip and eyebrows requires convergence and downward or upward gaze, so failure could indicate a gaze palsy or a cranial nerve lesion. …","m":"**The external version of the test is the useful one, and it should be corrected rather than internalized.** Difficulty seeing in low light — trouble adapting on entering a dark room, difficulty driving at night, inability to see stars that were previously visible — is a real symptom with a real differential. In well-nourished populations the common causes are ophthalmological: cataract, retinitis pigmentosa, glaucoma affecting peripheral vision, and simple uncorrected refractive error. **[E]** In populations where vitamin A deficiency is prevalent it is the earliest sign of a condition that causes preventable childhood blindness and elevated mortality, and it responds to supplementation. **[E]** Either way, it warrants assessment rather than reinterpretation. **On the phosphene test.** It can be performed and it means something narrow: pressing gently at the outer corner of the closed eye should produce a perceived flash or patterned glow. Its absence in one eye, particularly if accompanied by reduced vision, is a reason for ophthalmological assessment. Its presence tells you the retina and pathway are transmitting something. The caution from verse 151 applies with full force: press *gently*, and not at all if you have glaucoma, retinal disease, recent eye surgery or high myopia. Sustained pressure raises intraocular pressure and can trigger the oculocardiac reflex. …","g":["E"]},{"v":"351–357","t":"The Procedure and the Reading","p":"PART TWENTY-THREE: THE SHADOW PRACTICE","d":"कालो दूरस्थितो वापि येनोपायेन लक्ष्यते। तं वदामि समासेन यथादिष्टं शिवागमे॥३५१॥ एकान्तं विजनं गत्वा कृत्वादित्यं च पृष्ठतः। निरीक्षयेन्निजच्छायां कण्ठदेशे समाहितः॥३५२॥ ततश्चाकाशमीक्षेत ह्रीं परब्रह्मणे नमः। अष्टोत्तरशतं जप्त्वा ततः पश्यति शंकरम्॥३५३॥ तद्रूपं कृष्णवर्णं यः पश्यति व्योम्नि निर्मले। षण्मासान्मृत्युमाप्नोति स योगी नात्र संशयः॥३५६॥ पीते व्याधिभयं रक्ते नीले हानिं च निर्दिशेत्। नानावर्णोऽथ चेत्तस्मिन्सिद्धिश्च गीयते महान्॥३५७॥","i":"kālo dūrasthito vāpi yenopāyena lakṣyate | taṃ vadāmi samāsena yathādiṣṭaṃ śivāgame ||351|| ekāntaṃ vijanaṃ gatvā kṛtvādityaṃ ca pṛṣṭhataḥ | nirīkṣayen nijacchāyāṃ kaṇṭhadeśe samāhitaḥ ||352|| tataś cākāśam īkṣeta hrīṃ parabrahmaṇe namaḥ | aṣṭottaraśataṃ japtvā tataḥ paśyati śaṃkaram ||353|| tadrūpaṃ kṛṣṇavarṇaṃ yaḥ paśyati vyomni nirmale | ṣaṇmāsān mṛtyum āpnoti sa yogī nātra saṃśayaḥ ||356|| pīte vyādhibhayaṃ rakte nīle hāniṃ ca nirdiśet | nānāvarṇo 'tha cet tasmin siddhiś ca gīyate mahān ||357||","f":"The means by which Time, though distant, may be discerned, I shall state as taught in the tradition. Go to a solitary place, place the sun behind you, and observe your own shadow, concentrating at the region of the neck. Then look at the sky and recite the formula one hundred and eight times; the form is then seen. Whoever sees that form black against a clear sky obtains death within six months. If yellow, there is fear of disease; if red or blue, loss; if it appears in many colours, great accomplishment is declared.","a":"The procedure is precisely specified and it produces a reliable result. The result is a negative afterimage, and the mechanism is completely understood. Sustained fixation on a high-contrast stimulus produces local adaptation in the photoreceptors and post-receptoral neurons corresponding to the fixated region. When gaze shifts to a uniform field — here, the open sky — the adapted region responds differently from its surround, producing a perceived image of the original stimulus in complementary contrast: a dark stimulus yields a bright afterimage, and a coloured stimulus yields its opponent colour. **[E]** The effect is entirely ordinary, is demonstrable by anyone in seconds, and requires no special state. The specific procedure optimizes for a strong afterimage. Bright sunlight behind the observer produces a sharply defined shadow with maximal contrast. Fixation on a single point — the neck region — for the duration of one hundred and eight repetitions of a formula gives a long, stable adaptation period. And the sky is close to an ideal uniform field for afterimage projection, being large, unstructured, and evenly illuminated. The tradition arrived at a well-designed afterimage demonstration by trial and error. **[E]** The colour of the afterimage is determined by the adapting stimulus, the ambient illumination, and the observer's adaptation state at the moment of transfer. …","m":"**The afterimage demonstration is safe and worth doing. The divinatory reading is not, and the extended version should not be attempted casually.** **The safe version.** Stand with the sun behind you and look at your shadow on a light surface for thirty to sixty seconds. Then look at a clear patch of sky. A luminous form will appear, drift with your gaze, and fade over ten to twenty seconds. This is a complete demonstration of retinal adaptation and takes a minute. Its value is the same as that of the *ṣaṇmukhī* demonstration at verse 151: it converts an abstract proposition about constructed perception into something you have watched your own visual system do. Most people accept intellectually that perception is constructed and disbelieve it experientially. A visible object that is not there fixes that. **What not to do.** Do not read the colour as information about your health. The colour is a function of the stimulus, the sky, and your adaptation state, and the reading is determined by what you expect to see. Do not extend the practice to the full traditional form — prolonged solitary fixation with sustained repetitive recitation — without knowing what you are doing. The commentary's caution is sound and the mechanism is identifiable: the conditions specified are those that induce perceptual anomaly and can produce distressing experiences of unreality. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"370–374","t":"Curable, Manageable, Incurable","p":"PART TWENTY-FOUR: THE YOGIC CLOSE","d":"तीर्थस्नानेन दानेन तपसा सुकृतेन च। जपैर्ध्यानेन योगेन जायते कालवञ्चना॥३७०॥ शरीरं नाशयन्त्येते दोषा धातुमलास्तथा। समस्तु वायुर्विज्ञेयो बलतेजोविवर्धनः॥३७१॥ रक्षणीयस्ततो देहो यतो धर्मादिसाधनम्। योगाभ्यासात्समायान्ति साध्या याप्यास्तु साध्यताम्। असाध्या जीवितं घ्नन्ति न तत्रास्ति प्रतिक्रिया॥३७२॥ इडा गङ्गेति विज्ञेया पिङ्गला यमुना नदी। मध्ये सरस्वतीं विद्यात्प्रयागादिसमस्तथा॥३७४॥","i":"tīrthasnānena dānena tapasā sukṛtena ca | japair dhyānena yogena jāyate kālavañcanā ||370|| śarīraṃ nāśayanty ete doṣā dhātumalās tathā | samas tu vāyur vijñeyo balatejovivardhanaḥ ||371|| rakṣaṇīyas tato deho yato dharmādisādhanam | yogābhyāsāt samāyānti sādhyā yāpyās tu sādhyatām | asādhyā jīvitaṃ ghnanti na tatrāsti pratikriyā ||372|| iḍā gaṅgeti vijñeyā piṅgalā yamunā nadī | madhye sarasvatīṃ vidyāt prayāgādisamas tathā ||374||","f":"The outwitting of Time comes about through pilgrimage, giving, austerity, good conduct, recitation, meditation and practice. The humours, tissues and wastes destroy the body; but breath in balance increases strength and vigour. The body is therefore to be protected, being the instrument of every good end. Through practice the curable are cured and the manageable become curable; the incurable destroy life, and for those there is no remedy. Know Iḍā as the Ganges, Piṅgalā as the Yamunā, and Sarasvatī in the middle — the confluence at Prayāga is thus within.","a":"Verse 372 contains the *Śiva Svarodaya*'s most sophisticated piece of clinical thinking, and it is inherited rather than original: the threefold classification of disease as *sādhya*, *yāpya* and *asādhya* — curable, manageable, and incurable — which is standard Āyurvedic prognostic taxonomy. The taxonomy is correct in structure and it is better than the binary that lay thinking defaults to. Conditions do not divide into those that can be fixed and those that cannot. There is a large intermediate class — chronic conditions that cannot be eliminated but can be controlled indefinitely with continuing management — and this class contains most of the burden of modern disease. Type 1 diabetes, hypertension, HIV infection, epilepsy, rheumatoid arthritis and asthma are *yāpya* conditions in this sense: not curable, entirely manageable, compatible with a normal lifespan under treatment. **[E]** The distinction has practical consequences that are frequently missed. Treating a manageable condition as though it were curable produces the characteristic failure of chronic disease management — treatment adhered to until symptoms resolve, then stopped, followed by relapse. Non-adherence in asymptomatic chronic conditions such as hypertension is substantial and is a major driver of preventable cardiovascular events. …","m":"**Know which category your condition is in, and manage accordingly.** *Curable:* complete the treatment. The commonest failure is stopping when symptoms resolve — the classic instance being incomplete antibiotic courses, though the guidance here has become more nuanced and course lengths should follow current clinical advice rather than folk rules. *Manageable:* the failure mode is stopping treatment during periods of wellness. Asymptomatic chronic conditions — hypertension, high cholesterol, well-controlled epilepsy or diabetes — provide no felt feedback when treatment is working, which is precisely why adherence declines. **[E]** The countermeasure is to attach the treatment to a fixed daily cue, as argued at verse 65, and to arrange objective feedback: a blood pressure reading, a glucose measurement, a scheduled review. Feedback is what sustains a behaviour whose benefit is invisible. *Incurable:* the question shifts from cure to quality, and the shift should be made explicitly rather than by default. Early involvement of palliative care in advanced illness is associated with better quality of life and, in some studies, with survival at least as long as with aggressive treatment alone. **[E]** Naming the category is what permits the conversation. **On the internalized pilgrimage.** The transferable content of verse 374 is a point about access. …","g":["E"]},{"v":"375–387","t":"The Breathing Practice","p":"PART TWENTY-FOUR: THE YOGIC CLOSE","d":"आदौ साधनमाख्यातं सद्यः प्रत्ययकारकम्। बद्धपद्मासनो योगी बन्धयेदुड्डियानकम्॥३७५॥ पूरकः कुम्भकश्चैव रेचकश्च तृतीयकः। ज्ञातव्यो योगिभिर्नित्यं देहसंशुद्धिहेतवे॥३७६॥ पूरकः कुरुते वृद्धिं धातुसाम्यं तथैव च। कुम्भके स्तम्भनं कुर्याज्जीवरक्षाविवर्धनम्॥३७७॥ कुम्भयेत्सहजं वायुं यथाशक्ति प्रकल्पयेत्। रेचयेच्चन्द्रमार्गेण सूर्येणापूरयेत्सुधीः॥३७९॥ चन्द्रं पिबति सूर्यश्च सूर्यं पिबति चन्द्रमाः। अन्योन्यकालभावेन जीवेदाचन्द्रतारकम्॥३८०॥ निराशो निष्कलो योगी न किञ्चिदपि चिन्तयेत्। वासनामुन्मनां कृत्वा कालं जयति लीलया॥३८४॥","i":"ādau sādhanam ākhyātaṃ sadyaḥ pratyayakārakam | baddhapadmāsano yogī bandhayed uḍḍiyānakam ||375|| pūrakaḥ kumbhakaś caiva recakaś ca tṛtīyakaḥ | jñātavyo yogibhir nityaṃ dehasaṃśuddhihetave ||376|| pūrakaḥ kurute vṛddhiṃ dhātusāmyaṃ tathaiva ca | kumbhake stambhanaṃ kuryāj jīvarakṣāvivardhanam ||377|| kumbhayet sahajaṃ vāyuṃ yathāśakti prakalpayet | recayec candramārgeṇa sūryeṇāpūrayet sudhīḥ ||379|| candraṃ pibati sūryaś ca sūryaṃ pibati candramāḥ | anyonyakālabhāvena jīved ācandratārakam ||380|| nirāśo niṣkalo yogī na kiñcid api cintayet | vāsanām unmanāṃ kṛtvā kālaṃ jayati līlayā ||384||","f":"The practice is declared first, since it produces conviction at once: seated in the bound lotus, the practitioner applies the upward lock. Filling, retention and emptying are to be known constantly, for the body's purification. Filling produces increase and balance; retention produces steadiness and the preservation of life. Retain the natural breath according to capacity, exhale by the left channel and fill by the right. The sun drinks the moon and the moon the sun; by their mutual alternation one lives long. The practitioner free of craving, thinking of nothing, having stilled the disposition, conquers Time without effort.","a":"Verse 379 describes *nāḍī-śodhana* — alternate nostril breathing — in its essential form: inhale through one nostril, retain, exhale through the other, and reverse. Verse 380 states the alternation principle. This is the practice that has received more experimental attention than anything else in this text, and the evidence deserves a careful summary rather than either enthusiasm or dismissal. The consistent findings concern acute autonomic effects. Alternate nostril breathing performed at a slow rate produces reductions in heart rate and blood pressure and increases in heart-rate variability indices during and shortly after practice, across a number of small trials. **[S to E]** The difficulty is attribution: slow breathing alone produces these effects reliably, and most protocols confound the alternation with the slowing. Studies that have compared alternate nostril breathing against rate-matched controls are fewer, smaller, and less consistent. The honest position is that the *slow breathing* component is well supported and the *alternation* component is not clearly demonstrated to add anything. **[S]** The literature has further limitations that should be stated. Sample sizes are typically small, blinding is difficult or absent, control conditions are often inadequate, and the field shows the publication-bias profile characteristic of research on traditional practices. …","m":"**The practice, with its evidence stated honestly.** *Nāḍī-śodhana without retention.* Seated comfortably — the lotus posture is not required and is unavailable to most adults — close the right nostril with the thumb, inhale through the left over about four seconds; close the left, exhale through the right over about six; inhale right, exhale left. Continue for five to ten minutes. What is supported: the autonomic effects of slow breathing at approximately six cycles per minute, which are well documented. **[E]** What is not established: that the alternation adds anything beyond the slowing. **[S]** Practise it if you like it — the hand position is a useful attentional anchor and the pattern imposes a rate — but understand that the active ingredient is probably the tempo. *On retention.* Do not add retention until the unretained practice is comfortable, and then only briefly and without strain. Do not combine retention with abdominal locks without instruction. Do not practise retention at all if you have uncontrolled hypertension, a cardiac arrhythmia, glaucoma or retinal disease, or if you are pregnant. If you experience dizziness, chest discomfort, visual disturbance or headache, stop. *On posture.* Verse 375 specifies bound lotus. …","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"388–395","t":"The Close","p":"PART TWENTY-FIVE: THE COLOPHON","d":"एतज्जानाति यो योगी एतत्पठति नित्यशः। सर्वदुःखविनिर्मुक्तो लभते वाञ्छितं फलम्॥३८८॥ प्रणवः सर्ववेदानां ब्राह्मणानां रविर्यथा। मृत्युलोके तथा पूज्यः स्वरज्ञानी पुमानपि॥३९०॥ स्वरतत्त्वं तथा युद्धे देवि वश्यं स्त्रियस्तथा। गर्भाधानं च रोगश्च कालाख्यानं तथोच्यते॥३९३॥ एवं प्रवर्तितं लोके प्रसिद्धं सिद्धयोगिभिः। चन्द्रार्कग्रहणे जाप्यं पठतां सिद्धिदायकम्॥३९४॥ स्वस्थाने तु समासीनो निद्रां चाहारमल्पकम्। चिन्तयेत्परमात्मानं यो वेद स भवेत्कृती॥३९५॥","i":"etaj jānāti yo yogī etat paṭhati nityaśaḥ | sarvaduḥkhavinirmukto labhate vāñchitaṃ phalam ||388|| praṇavaḥ sarvavedānāṃ brāhmaṇānāṃ ravir yathā | mṛtyuloke tathā pūjyaḥ svarajñānī pumān api ||390|| svaratattvaṃ tathā yuddhe devi vaśyaṃ striyas tathā | garbhādhānaṃ ca rogaś ca kālākhyānaṃ tathocyate ||393|| evaṃ pravartitaṃ loke prasiddhaṃ siddhayogibhiḥ | candrārkagrahaṇe jāpyaṃ paṭhatāṃ siddhidāyakam ||394|| svasthāne tu samāsīno nidrāṃ cāhāram alpakam | cintayet paramātmānaṃ yo veda sa bhavet kṛtī ||395||","f":"The practitioner who knows this and recites it constantly is freed from suffering and obtains what is desired. As *om* is among the Vedas and the sun among the learned, so is the knower of the breath honoured in the world. The science of the breath has been declared, and its application in battle, in subjugation, in conception, in disease and in the reckoning of time. Thus it has been set forth in the world by accomplished practitioners, to be recited at eclipse. Seated in one's own place, with sleep and food kept moderate, one should contemplate the supreme; whoever knows this is accomplished.","a":"Verse 393 is the treatise's own table of contents, and it is worth reading as such because it is an honest summary of what the text contains: breath science, warfare, subjugation, conception, disease, and the reckoning of the time of death. Set against the analysis conducted across these nine volumes, the list divides cleanly. The breath science is the part with observational substance. The warfare material is applied performance psychology wrapped around a false prognostic claim. The subjugation material has no valid content of any kind. The conception material contains one correct timing observation surrounded by false claims oriented toward a harmful preference. The disease material contains the treatise's best clinical observations attached to its most dangerous prognoses. And the reckoning of death is where a real physiological insight — that lost rhythmic variability is a bad sign — was converted into a timetable. That distribution is the finding of this book, and the text states its own contents in an order that makes it visible. Verse 395 closes with a practical instruction that has not appeared before and deserves note: *nidrāṃ cāhāram alpakam* — sleep and food kept moderate. Read charitably as moderation rather than as restriction, this is unremarkable dietary and lifestyle counsel. …","m":"**On the closing lifestyle advice.** Protect sleep. Seven to nine hours for most adults, and the widespread belief that one functions well on substantially less is, in the great majority of cases, an artefact of impaired self-assessment under sleep restriction. **[E]** No practice in this book, or any other, compensates for chronic sleep deficit. Eat adequately. If a contemplative or disciplinary framework is being used to justify progressive restriction, that is a warning sign rather than an achievement. In India, the Vandrevala Foundation and iCall provide mental health support lines; the National Alliance for Eating Disorders operates a helpline for eating-disorder concerns. **On the recitation claim.** Verse 388's promise that reciting the text produces freedom from suffering is the standard *phalaśruti* — the statement of benefits — that closes Sanskrit works of every genre, and it is a genre convention rather than a claim. It is worth naming because the convention is easy to mistake for an assertion, and because its presence at the end of a text tells you something about the text's intended circulation: it was meant to be recited, transmitted, and valued as an object, not only used as a manual.","g":["E","S"]},{"v":"CONCL-ledger","t":"The ledger","p":"Conclusion","d":"","i":"","f":"**What the text observed correctly.** The nasal cycle is real, is centrally generated, and is a continuously available external readout of autonomic asymmetry — a fact not documented in Western medicine until 1895 and described here with precision. Its period is roughly correct. The reciprocal, antiphase character of the alternation is correctly characterized. The five *vāyus* constitute a functional partition of visceral regulation whose territories correspond closely to the actual segmental organization of autonomic innervation. …","a":"**What the text observed correctly.** The nasal cycle is real, is centrally generated, and is a continuously available external readout of autonomic asymmetry — a fact not documented in Western medicine until 1895 and described here with precision. Its period is roughly correct. The reciprocal, antiphase character of the alternation is correctly characterized. The five *vāyus* constitute a functional partition of visceral regulation whose territories correspond closely to the actual segmental organization of autonomic innervation. Verses 191 to 196 arrange the organism into a hierarchy from structural tissue through fluids and homeostatic drive to affect, with the drive category grouping hunger, thirst, sleep and energy in a way that corresponds to genuine hypothalamic organization. Verses 221 to 223 establish a monotonic relationship between activity intensity and respiratory output, quantified across six activities and measured with a hand. Verse 122 encodes a correct clinical recommendation about sleep posture. And the *ariṣṭa* verses assemble a checklist of red flags — acute mental status change first among them — that is largely sound. **What it got right for the wrong reasons.** Left-lateral sleeping posture reduces reflux, and the text arrives at it through a metaphysics of vital-force conservation. …","m":"","g":[]},{"v":"CONCL-transmission","t":"The transmission thesis","p":"Conclusion","d":"","i":"","f":"The most useful finding of this study is not about breathing. It is about how knowledge survives. Across this single text, content that could be checked against the world was transmitted with high fidelity, and content that could not drifted into mutually exclusive versions. The *aṅgula* measurements appear identically in two independent recensions at verses 164 to 173, while the qualitative descriptors in the same passage diverge. The planetary assignments contradict themselves across verses 182 to 184. The body map at verse 156 contradicts the one at verses 252 to 253 on three of five locations. The sex assignments at verses 294 and 299 are opposite. …","a":"The most useful finding of this study is not about breathing. It is about how knowledge survives. Across this single text, content that could be checked against the world was transmitted with high fidelity, and content that could not drifted into mutually exclusive versions. The *aṅgula* measurements appear identically in two independent recensions at verses 164 to 173, while the qualitative descriptors in the same passage diverge. The planetary assignments contradict themselves across verses 182 to 184. The body map at verse 156 contradicts the one at verses 252 to 253 on three of five locations. The sex assignments at verses 294 and 299 are opposite. And at verses 366 and 367, a good external test — resolving a faint star — is internalized into a poor one, with the original and its replacement preserved side by side. The mechanism is not mysterious. Verifiable content is corrected by reality at every generation of transmission. Unverifiable content is corrected only by the memory of the transmitter, and over centuries the two classes diverge in fidelity. The drift is always in one direction, from the checkable toward the unfalsifiable, because the unfalsifiable version never fails and therefore never gets revised. This yields two cheap tests applicable to any inherited body of practical knowledge, requiring no expertise in its domain. …","m":"","g":[]},{"v":"CONCL-works","t":"What actually works","p":"Conclusion","d":"","i":"","f":"Stripped of everything else, the practices from this text with genuine support: **Slow breathing at approximately six cycles per minute**, with exhalation longer than inhalation, for three to five minutes. This is the best-supported acute non-pharmacological intervention on autonomic state available, and it is what the reduction ladder of verses 224 and 225 amounts to once its metaphysics is removed. **Nasal rather than oral breathing**, which preserves the respiratory entrainment of limbic and mnemonic circuitry that oral breathing abolishes. **Left-lateral sleeping posture**, particularly after meals and for anyone prone to reflux. …","a":"Stripped of everything else, the practices from this text with genuine support: **Slow breathing at approximately six cycles per minute**, with exhalation longer than inhalation, for three to five minutes. This is the best-supported acute non-pharmacological intervention on autonomic state available, and it is what the reduction ladder of verses 224 and 225 amounts to once its metaphysics is removed. **Nasal rather than oral breathing**, which preserves the respiratory entrainment of limbic and mnemonic circuitry that oral breathing abolishes. **Left-lateral sleeping posture**, particularly after meals and for anyone prone to reflux. **A brief daily observation** — verse 149's morning check — recorded before interpretation, self-referenced rather than compared to norms. **A fixed, short, physical pre-performance routine** before demanding tasks, with no claim attached about the outcome. **State-matched scheduling:** precision and learning to the settled state, force and confrontation to the mobilized one, and a designated null period for the interval when neither is available. **Sustained attention to the breath**, ten to fifteen minutes, where the operative component is not calm but the repeated detection of attentional lapse and the return of attention. Nothing on this list requires belief in *nāḍīs*, *tattvas*, or anything else in the treatise's vocabulary.","m":"","g":[]},{"v":"CONCL-limit","t":"The instrument and its limit","p":"Conclusion","d":"","i":"","f":"The *Śiva Svarodaya* was produced by people with no instruments, attending very carefully to the one signal available to them without any. What they achieved with that method is the measure of what attention alone can do: a real rhythm identified, a real state variable located, real task-demand distinctions drawn, real clinical signs assembled. What they could not do is the measure of the method's limit. They could not distinguish a nasal cycle disrupted by autonomic failure from one disrupted by a deviated septum. They could not tell whether their five phases were five states or one continuum divided by convention. They could not count the misses. …","a":"The *Śiva Svarodaya* was produced by people with no instruments, attending very carefully to the one signal available to them without any. What they achieved with that method is the measure of what attention alone can do: a real rhythm identified, a real state variable located, real task-demand distinctions drawn, real clinical signs assembled. What they could not do is the measure of the method's limit. They could not distinguish a nasal cycle disrupted by autonomic failure from one disrupted by a deviated septum. They could not tell whether their five phases were five states or one continuum divided by convention. They could not count the misses. And they had no way to check an interpretation against anything outside the tradition that produced it, which is why the interpretations proliferated without constraint while the measurements stayed still. The correct posture toward such a text is neither reverence nor dismissal. It is extraction: take the measurements, test the recommendations, discard the explanations, and grade every claim against its own evidence rather than against the authority of the whole. That procedure is available to any reader, requires no Sanskrit, and is the only thing that separates reading a tradition from being transmitted by it. The verse that states the method best is 153, where the practitioner is told to breathe onto a mirror and look. …","m":"","g":[]},{"v":"APPX-cautions","t":"Consolidated cautions","p":"Appendix A","d":"","i":"","f":"Collected from throughout this volume. Each is stated where it arises; they are gathered here because they are easy to lose in eight hundred pages. **Do not practise breath retention** if you have uncontrolled hypertension, cardiac arrhythmia, glaucoma or retinal disease, or if you are pregnant. Retention with abdominal locks reproduces Valsalva haemodynamics. **Do not press on the eyeballs** if you have glaucoma, retinal disease, recent eye surgery or high myopia. The *ṣaṇmukhī* and phosphene procedures require a light touch, and pressure can trigger the oculocardiac reflex. **Never gaze at the sun.** Solar retinopathy occurs within seconds to minutes, is painless, and is permanent. …","a":"Collected from throughout this volume. Each is stated where it arises; they are gathered here because they are easy to lose in eight hundred pages. **Do not practise breath retention** if you have uncontrolled hypertension, cardiac arrhythmia, glaucoma or retinal disease, or if you are pregnant. Retention with abdominal locks reproduces Valsalva haemodynamics. **Do not press on the eyeballs** if you have glaucoma, retinal disease, recent eye surgery or high myopia. The *ṣaṇmukhī* and phosphene procedures require a light touch, and pressure can trigger the oculocardiac reflex. **Never gaze at the sun.** Solar retinopathy occurs within seconds to minutes, is painless, and is permanent. The text correctly places the sun behind the observer; other traditions do not. **Do not use nasal dominance as a prognosis.** Persistent unilateral obstruction has ordinary causes — septal deviation, polyps, chronic rhinosinusitis — and warrants an ENT assessment. **Do not tape your mouth for sleep** on the basis of a traditional text. It is potentially dangerous in undiagnosed sleep apnoea or nasal obstruction. **Do not force lotus posture.** The rotation comes from the hip; forcing it at the knee is the common injury mechanism. **Do not attempt prolonged solitary shadow-gazing** without knowing what you are doing. The conditions specified are those that induce perceptual anomaly, and the source's own commentary warns of this. **Do not attempt to suppress thought.** Suppression produces rebound; the operable instruction is disengagement. **Do not restrict sleep** as a discipline. And if a contemplative framework is being used to justify progressive food restriction, treat that as a warning sign. **Seek assessment, not a countdown, for:** acute change in mental status; new combined bladder and bowel incontinence, particularly with back pain or leg weakness — this is a same-day emergency; new haloes around lights with eye pain or nausea; sudden vision change; unexplained weight loss; new loss of sensation forming a level on the trunk; abrupt personality change. **If reading the prognostic sections has produced an impulse to check your body repeatedly for signs**, that impulse is the thing to address. Repeated checking maintains health anxiety rather than resolving it, and health anxiety responds well to treatment.","m":"","g":[]}];
